/* Original authored course; rebuild with node scripts/build-course.cjs. */
(function(root){
const data={"version":1,"reviewedOn":"2026-09-22","sources":{"maths":{"title":"MOE Primary Mathematics syllabus, October 2025","url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/api/media/92bff26d-b2b4-4535-b868-b8415c744b91/2021-Primary-Mathematics-Syllabus-P1-to-P6-Updated-October-2025.pdf"},"science":{"title":"MOE Primary Science syllabus, updated January 2026","url":"https://www.moe.gov.sg/api/media/ba3562d3-5b31-4459-8693-45cde7b97273/Primary-Science-Syllabus-2023_May24.pdf"},"admissions":{"title":"NUS High Year 1 admissions","url":"https://www.nushigh.edu.sg/admissions/year-1-and-3-admissions/year-1-admissions/"},"faq":{"title":"NUS High admissions FAQ, especially question 11","url":"https://www.nushigh.edu.sg/admissions/year-1-and-3-admissions/admissions-faq/"}},"units":[{"id":"m-number","title":"Numbers have structure","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"number","prerequisites":[],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.37; P5 p.41","goals":["Explain place value","Compare and round numbers","Use an estimate to judge an answer"],"visual":"place","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Start with a question","text":"Imagine that a library has 406,080 books. What does each digit contribute? Reading a large number is easier when you describe its structure: four hundred thousands, no ten thousands, six thousands, no hundreds, eight tens and no ones. The number is 400,000 + 6,000 + 80. The zeros keep the other digits in their proper places; they are not invitations to close the gaps.\n\nIn this class, use a place-value chart before calculating. Draw columns for millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens and ones. Put one digit in each column. Say both the digit and its value: the digit 6 here has value 6,000. This distinction prevents many errors later with decimals and measurement."},{"title":"Build the idea","text":"Our base-ten system exchanges ten units of one place for one unit of the next place. Ten hundreds equal one thousand. Therefore 3,000 can also be described as 30 hundreds or 300 tens. The quantity stays the same while its grouping changes. Written addition, subtraction, multiplication and division all depend on this exchange.\n\nTo compare numbers, first compare the number of digits when there are no leading zeros. For equal-length whole numbers, work from the greatest place downwards. Compare 406,080 and 460,008: the hundred-thousands digits agree, but 0 ten thousands is less than 6 ten thousands. Later digits cannot reverse that decision because their combined value is smaller than one ten thousand."},{"title":"Work through an example","text":"Round 46,780 to the nearest thousand. It lies between 46,000 and 47,000. The halfway point is 46,500. Since 46,780 is above halfway, the nearest thousand is 47,000. Its distances are 780 and 220 respectively. This distance explanation is the reason behind the familiar rounding rule.\n\nNow round the same number to the nearest hundred: the neighbours are 46,700 and 46,800, so choose 46,800. The requested place matters. Write 46,780 ≈ 47,000 when rounding to a thousand. The approximately-equal sign reminds us that rounding changes the number, whereas 46,780 = 46,000 + 780 is an exact statement."},{"title":"Investigate a common mistake","text":"A pupil says that multiplying 408 by 10 means putting a zero anywhere. Explain why that cannot be right. Every quantity becomes ten times as large: 4 hundreds become 4 thousands, and 8 ones become 8 tens. Thus 408 × 10 = 4,080. The place-value change is the explanation, and the written zero is a consequence.\n\nRounding also does not always make a number larger. Rounding 46,210 to the nearest thousand produces 46,000. An estimate is useful for judging size, but it is not automatically an acceptable final answer. Read whether the problem asks for an exact quantity or a specified approximation."},{"title":"Try it together","text":"A school buys 198 packs containing 21 sheets each. Before doing the exact multiplication, estimate 200 × 20 = 4,000 sheets. The exact answer should be close to that scale. An answer of 416 or 41,580 would need checking. Work out the exact answer by 198 × (20 + 1) = 3,960 + 198 = 4,158.\n\nExplain why this estimate does not prove the exact answer. Many nearby numbers would pass the same size check. Use a second method, such as 200 × 21 − 2 × 21, to confirm 4,158. Estimation and exact verification play different roles."},{"title":"Use your notebook","text":"Make cards for 0, 2, 5 and 8. Form the greatest and smallest four-digit numbers using each card once; do not begin with zero. Explain the first position you chose and why it controls the result. Then round both numbers to the nearest hundred and compare the rounding errors.\n\nPause before checking: the greatest number is 8,520; the smallest is 2,058. Their nearest hundreds are 8,500 and 2,100. Explain why simply sorting all digits upwards would fail for a four-digit number. This is a small example of solving a problem while respecting constraints."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Teach someone the difference between a number, a digit and a place value. Give an example where the same digit has two different values. Then read a large number from a newspaper or a school report and identify whether it is an exact count or an estimate.\n\nBefore independent practice, check that you can explain the halfway point used in rounding, regroup a thousand as hundreds, and reject an answer with an implausible size. If one idea remains uncertain, revisit the place-value chart. Being able to recite a rule is less useful than being able to reconstruct it."}]},{"id":"m-operations","title":"Operations and the meaning of a method","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"number","prerequisites":["m-number"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.37; P5 p.41","goals":["Choose operations from relationships","Explain regrouping and distributivity","Use brackets and inverse checks"],"visual":"","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"What are we trying to describe?","text":"Addition joins amounts or extends a quantity. Subtraction can find a remainder or compare two quantities. Multiplication describes equal groups and scaling. Division can share a total into a known number of groups, or find how many groups of a known size fit. The same arithmetic can represent different stories, so name what every number means.\n\nFor 24 ÷ 6, one story shares 24 counters among six children and asks how many each receives. Another packs 24 counters into groups of six and asks how many groups there are. Both give 4, but the answer has a different meaning. Write the answer with its meaning, not as an isolated number."},{"title":"Build a reliable written method","text":"For 327 × 24, partition 24 into 20 and 4. The first partial product is 6,540, and the second is 1,308. Their sum is 7,848. The second row in a written multiplication method represents tens, not another set of ones. A place-value chart explains its position.\n\nFor division, remember that each remainder still has a place value. Dividing 154 by 4, 1 hundred can be regrouped as 10 tens. After sharing tens and then ones, the result is 38 remainder 2. Verify 4 × 38 + 2 = 154. The remainder must be smaller than the divisor; otherwise another full group could still be made."},{"title":"Worked example with a decision","text":"There are 154 pupils and each van holds 4 pupils. The arithmetic is 154 ÷ 4 = 38 remainder 2. We need 39 vans because two pupils still require places. If the story instead asks how many complete teams of four can be made, the answer is 38 teams with two pupils left over.\n\nIf 154 metres of ribbon are shared equally among four people, the remaining 2 metres can be divided: each receives 38.5 metres. A remainder does not prescribe a single response. Decide whether the objects can be split and whether every object must be accommodated."},{"title":"Order expresses structure","text":"Consider 18 + 12 ÷ 3. Division acts on the 12, giving 18 + 4 = 22. In (18 + 12) ÷ 3, the entire sum is divided, giving 10. Brackets tell the reader which quantity is being treated as a group. They are part of the meaning, not decoration.\n\nMultiplication and division have equal priority and are evaluated from left to right when no grouping says otherwise. Thus 24 ÷ 6 × 2 = 4 × 2 = 8. Addition and subtraction likewise share a priority. A mnemonic is only useful if it preserves these equal-priority rules."},{"title":"Find and repair a mistake","text":"A pupil writes 36 − 8 + 2 = 36 − 10 = 26. The intended expression first removes eight and then adds two: 28 + 2 = 30. The pupil has silently inserted brackets to make 36 − (8 + 2). Explain this difference with counters or a number line.\n\nAnother pupil says multiplication always makes a number larger. It does when a positive quantity is multiplied by a whole number greater than one. Multiplying by one preserves it, and multiplying by zero gives zero. Later, multiplying by a proper fraction will scale it down. State the conditions of a rule."},{"title":"Guided practice on paper","text":"A shop has 18 boxes containing 24 pencils each. It sells 75 pencils. Draw the relationship before calculating: total pencils minus sold pencils equals remaining pencils. Work 18 × 24 = 432, then 432 − 75 = 357. Check by adding back 75 to 357.\n\nTry a second route: 20 × 24 − 2 × 24 − 75. Explain why subtracting two boxes corrects the temporary use of twenty boxes. A second method is especially valuable when it groups the calculation differently, because repeating the same mistaken steps may repeat the same error."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Invent two stories for 35 ÷ 4 that need different final answers. Include a story about sharing a length and one about booking vehicles. Tell someone why a numerical calculation alone does not settle the practical question.\n\nBefore practice, make a habit of four checks: identify the relationship, estimate the scale, perform the calculation, and interpret the answer in the original setting. This routine is more reliable than searching the sentence for a word such as 'altogether' and automatically choosing an operation."}]},{"id":"m-factors","title":"Factors, multiples and systematic search","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"number","prerequisites":["m-operations"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.37","goals":["Distinguish factors from multiples","Find common factors and multiples","Organise a search so no case is missed"],"visual":"","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Start with arrangements","text":"Arrange 24 counters in equal rows. One row of 24, two rows of 12, three rows of 8 and four rows of 6 all work. Five rows do not work without a remainder. A factor divides a number exactly: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 and 24 are factors of 24.\n\nA multiple is a number obtained by multiplying by a whole number. The positive multiples of 6 begin 6, 12, 18, 24 and 30. Six is a factor of 24, and 24 is a multiple of six. Say the entire relationship; saying only that two numbers 'go together' hides the direction."},{"title":"A complete search","text":"Look for factor pairs in increasing order of the smaller number. For 36, the pairs are 1 × 36, 2 × 18, 3 × 12, 4 × 9 and 6 × 6. Once the smaller and larger factors meet, later pairs would repeat the same products in reverse order. This gives a reason for stopping.\n\nList the factors in order: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36. Notice that 6 appears only once, even though both members of its pair are equal. A square number has a middle factor pair of this kind. You can explain that observation with a square arrangement of counters."},{"title":"Worked example: equal packs","text":"You have 18 purple beads and 24 white beads. You want identical packs, using every bead, with the greatest possible number of packs. The number of packs must divide both 18 and 24. Their common factors are 1, 2, 3 and 6, so six packs are possible at most.\n\nEach pack contains 18 ÷ 6 = 3 purple beads and 24 ÷ 6 = 4 white beads. Check the result by rebuilding the totals. The choice of common factors follows from sharing two whole-number quantities without leftovers; it is not just a keyword rule about 'greatest'."},{"title":"Worked example: meeting again","text":"One light flashes every four seconds and another every six seconds. They flash together now. List future flash times: 4, 8, 12, 16… and 6, 12, 18… . Their next shared time is 12 seconds. We seek the smallest positive common multiple because we want the first meeting after now.\n\nZero is a common multiple too, but it describes the starting moment and cannot answer 'next'. Be explicit about the positive condition. The same reasoning applies to repeating patterns, tiles fitting a length exactly and regular schedules, as long as the repeating intervals remain constant."},{"title":"Diagnose the reasoning","text":"A pupil chooses 18 as the greatest common factor of 18 and 24 because it is the smaller number. Test the claim: 24 ÷ 18 is not a whole number. A common factor must pass both divisibility tests. A plausible-looking answer is not enough.\n\nAnother pupil multiplies every pair of intervals to find when they meet again. The product is a common multiple, but may not be the smallest. For intervals four and six, 24 works but 12 comes earlier. Explain why shared factors can allow an earlier meeting."},{"title":"Explore with objects","text":"Use twelve counters to make every different rectangular arrangement, counting rotated copies only once. Then try thirteen counters. Thirteen makes only a one-by-thirteen arrangement. A whole number greater than one with exactly two positive factors is called a prime number; this vocabulary is enrichment here.\n\nDo not say one is prime: it has only one positive factor. Try numbers from 2 to 20 and record your factor pairs. The useful habit is systematic testing, not memorising a list. If someone disagrees with your classification, a factor pair can settle the disagreement."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Suppose two repeating bells ring together every time both cycles finish. Explain how a multiples list answers that question, then explain how a factors list solves identical-pack questions. The operations may look related, but the unknown plays a different role.\n\nFor independent work, show enough of your list or factor pairs for another person to see that your search is complete. If you use a quicker method, explain why it cannot miss a better answer. That is the beginning of mathematical proof through an organised search."}]},{"id":"m-fractions","title":"Fractions: name the whole","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"fraction","prerequisites":["m-factors"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P1–P4 pp.31–37","goals":["Identify equal parts and the reference whole","Compare and simplify fractions","Connect mixed numbers with improper fractions"],"visual":"fractions","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A fraction needs a whole","text":"A fraction describes a quantity relative to a chosen whole. If a ribbon is split into four equal lengths, each length is one quarter of that ribbon. Three such lengths make three quarters. The denominator tells the size of the equal parts; the numerator tells how many of those parts are counted.\n\nTwo children can each have half a cake and still have different amounts if their cakes differ in size. Before comparing fractions of physical objects, check that the wholes are equal. Before comparing abstract fractions as numbers, the unit whole is shared by definition. This small distinction matters in almost every fraction word problem."},{"title":"Equivalent names","text":"Cut each third of a rectangle into two equal pieces. The whole now contains six equal pieces, and two thirds cover four of them. Thus 2/3 = 4/6. Both numerator and denominator have doubled because we changed the size of the pieces without changing the amount covered.\n\nTo simplify 12/18, divide both numbers by their common factor 6 to get 2/3. Dividing only the numerator changes the quantity. Explain equivalence by a diagram, by a number line, or by comparing both fractions with the same total. Simplifying is a change of name, not a reduction in the amount."},{"title":"Compare thoughtfully","text":"For fractions with the same denominator, more parts mean more quantity: 5/8 > 3/8. For unit fractions with the same whole, more equal divisions make each part smaller: 1/8 < 1/3. Neither rule says that a larger denominator always means a smaller fraction when the numerator also changes.\n\nTo compare 3/4 and 5/6, rename them in twelfths: 9/12 and 10/12. Hence 5/6 is greater. You can also compare their gaps from one: 3/4 is missing 1/4, while 5/6 is missing only 1/6. A second representation gives you another way to check the reasoning."},{"title":"Worked example: beyond one","text":"Seven quarters contain one full set of four quarters and three quarters left. Therefore 7/4 = 1 3/4. Conversely, 2 1/3 contains six thirds in its two wholes, plus one more third, giving 7/3. Draw separate wholes so that each is divided into the same-sized pieces.\n\nAn improper fraction is not an error. It is a convenient way of naming a quantity at least as large as one. Mixed numbers can be easier to interpret in a measurement story, while improper fractions often make multiplication easier. Choose the representation that makes the relationship clear."},{"title":"Fractions of a set","text":"If three fifths of 25 counters are purple, partition the total into five equal groups. One fifth is 25 ÷ 5 = 5 counters. Three fifths are 3 × 5 = 15 counters. The denominator counts equal groups of the whole set, not individual counters.\n\nCheck with the remaining fraction: two fifths are 10 counters, and 15 + 10 = 25. If the result were not a whole number of counters, reconsider whether the problem permits splitting the objects. A fraction can describe a set even though the set consists of separate items."},{"title":"Use your notebook","text":"Draw a number line from zero to two. Mark 1/2, 3/4, 5/4 and 1 1/2. Keep the scale constant on both sides of one. Explain why 5/4 and 1 1/4 occupy the same point. Then place 2/3 approximately and explain why it lies between one half and three quarters.\n\nMake an intentionally misleading drawing in which three unequal pieces are labelled thirds. Ask another person to identify the mistake. Correct it by measuring or folding. A convincing-looking picture must still satisfy the definition of equal parts."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"When you encounter a fraction, ask: What is the whole? Are the parts equal? What does the numerator count? Could I locate the amount on a number line? These questions work for ribbon, money, collections and liquid measurements.\n\nBefore independent practice, explain why 2/5 and 4/10 are equal without simply saying 'multiply by two'. Your explanation should describe what happens to every part of the whole. That explanation will later justify fraction addition, multiplication and division."}]},{"id":"m-fraction-ops","title":"Adding, subtracting and multiplying fractions","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"fraction","prerequisites":["m-fractions"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.37; P5 p.41","goals":["Add equal-sized fraction units","Regroup mixed numbers","Explain multiplication as scaling"],"visual":"fraction-product","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Why common units matter","text":"You would not add three metres to four centimetres and call the result seven metres. Fractions also need matching units. One third and one quarter are different-sized pieces. Rename them as fourths of thirds, or twelfths: 1/3 + 1/4 = 4/12 + 3/12 = 7/12.\n\nThe denominator stays twelve when adding because the pieces remain twelfths. It does not become twenty-four. Use two equal paper strips to build the sum, then copy the idea onto a number line. The calculation should represent a total distance or amount, not just a manipulation of symbols."},{"title":"Add and subtract mixed amounts","text":"To find 2 1/3 + 1 1/4, combine the whole numbers and then the fractions: 3 + 4/12 + 3/12 = 3 7/12. If the fraction sum reaches one, regroup it as another whole. Keep the equal-parts meaning visible throughout the calculation.\n\nFor 3 1/4 − 1 2/3, rename quarters and thirds as twelfths: 3 3/12 − 1 8/12. Regroup one whole from the first number: 2 15/12 − 1 8/12 = 1 7/12. Alternatively use improper fractions: 13/4 − 5/3 = 39/12 − 20/12 = 19/12. Both methods describe the same difference."},{"title":"Multiplication means part of a quantity","text":"Find two thirds of three quarters of a rectangle. Shade three of four equal columns first. Then keep two of three equal rows within that shaded portion. Six of twelve equal small rectangles remain, so 2/3 × 3/4 = 6/12 = 1/2.\n\nThe product can be smaller than either factor. Multiplying by a proper fraction scales a positive quantity down. Multiplying 3/4 by 2 makes two copies and gives 1 1/2; multiplying 3/4 by 1/2 gives half a copy, or 3/8. Multiplication describes scaling, not necessarily enlargement."},{"title":"Worked example in context","text":"A recipe needs 2/3 cup of oats for one batch. For three batches, calculate 3 × 2/3 = 6/3 = 2 cups. For half a batch, calculate 1/2 × 2/3 = 1/3 cup. State which quantity is being scaled and by what factor.\n\nSuppose you already have 1 1/4 cups and need 2 cups. The extra amount is 2 − 1 1/4 = 3/4 cup. Check by adding 1 1/4 and 3/4. A problem may combine multiplication and subtraction; each step should answer a named intermediate question."},{"title":"Diagnose an error","text":"A pupil writes 2/3 + 1/4 = 3/7. Test it against a rough estimate: two thirds is already more than one half, but three sevenths is less than one half. Adding a positive amount cannot make that quantity smaller. The estimate reveals the error before a detailed correction.\n\nFor multiplication, a pupil writes 3/5 × 2/3 = 6/5 because 'the threes cancel'. Correct cancellation divides one numerator and one denominator by the same non-zero factor. Here the product is 6/15 = 2/5. Cancellation is a compact form of simplifying a product, not permission to erase arbitrary matching digits."},{"title":"Guided drawing and calculation","text":"Draw a square and use different shading directions to show 3/4 of 2/3. Count the overlapping small regions and compare with 2/3 of 3/4. The overlap is one half in either order. Explain why the numerical result is the same, while the order of instructions in a story may differ.\n\nNow solve 1 1/2 × 4 using both repeated addition and an improper fraction. Four copies of one and a half make six. The improper fraction calculation, 3/2 × 4 = 6, confirms it. Choose a method you can explain rather than always selecting the shortest-looking notation."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Write a short rule in your own words for each operation. Addition and subtraction combine or compare equal-sized units. Multiplication scales a quantity or finds a fraction of it. This difference explains why common denominators are necessary for addition but not for the multiplication rule.\n\nBefore practice, predict whether your result should be above or below one, and above or below the starting amount. If a computed answer contradicts that prediction, revisit the model. A sensible estimate does not prove an answer, but it is an excellent error detector."}]},{"id":"m-fraction-division","title":"Why fraction division works","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"fraction","prerequisites":["m-fraction-ops"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P6 p.43","goals":["Distinguish sharing from measuring groups","Derive fraction division from equal units","Check a quotient by multiplication"],"visual":"fraction-divide","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Begin with sharing","text":"A jug holds 3/4 litre of juice. Three people share it equally. Draw three quarter-litre portions and give one to each person. Each receives 1/4 litre, so 3/4 ÷ 3 = 1/4. The original quantity has been divided into three equal shares.\n\nNow imagine only 1/4 litre shared among three people. Each quarter must be split into three equal parts. A full litre would contain twelve such parts, so each person receives 1/12 litre. This gives a reason for 1/4 ÷ 3 = 1/12. Avoid memorising a rule without knowing what the smaller pieces represent."},{"title":"Division can count groups","text":"How many half-litre cups can be filled from three litres? There are two halves in every litre, so six half-litres fit. Thus 3 ÷ 1/2 = 6. Dividing by a proper fraction can produce a quotient larger than the starting number because the groups being counted are smaller than one unit.\n\nCompare the two questions 'How much does each of two people receive?' and 'How many groups of size one half fit?' The first is 3 ÷ 2 = 1.5 litres per person. The second is 3 ÷ 1/2 = 6 groups. The divisor has a different meaning; draw it before choosing an operation."},{"title":"Derive the method","text":"Find 3/4 ÷ 2/3 by measuring both quantities in twelfths. Three quarters are nine twelfths; two thirds are eight twelfths. Nine of these equal units contain 9/8 groups of eight. Therefore 3/4 ÷ 2/3 = 9/8 = 1 1/8.\n\nAlgebraically, renaming both fractions in common units gives (3 × 3)/(4 × 2), the same as 3/4 × 3/2. Multiplying by the reciprocal is a compact consequence of counting equal units. Only the divisor is inverted. The dividend remains the quantity being measured."},{"title":"Work through a ribbon problem","text":"A ribbon is 5/6 metre long. Each decoration needs 1/4 metre. The quotient 5/6 ÷ 1/4 = 20/6 = 3 1/3 tells us how many quarter-metre lengths the ribbon contains. Only three complete decorations can be made. They use 3/4 metre.\n\nThe leftover length is 5/6 − 3/4 = 1/12 metre. Notice that the fractional part of the quotient, one third, is one third of a decoration-length, not one third of a metre. Keeping units attached to the steps prevents that subtle misinterpretation."},{"title":"Repair a mistaken explanation","text":"A pupil says division always makes a number smaller. Test 2 ÷ 1/4 with a drawing of two wholes, each split into quarters. There are eight groups. The division is correct because it asks how many small groups fit, not how to shrink a quantity.\n\nAnother pupil divides 2/5 by 3 and gets 6/5. Check by multiplying the proposed share by three: 18/5 is not 2/5. The correct share, 2/15, satisfies 3 × 2/15 = 2/5. The inverse operation is a general check that works beyond this example."},{"title":"A small investigation","text":"Take a paper strip representing one metre. Mark halves, then quarters, then eighths. Ask how the number of groups fitting in one metre changes as the group size halves. The count doubles. Explain that inverse relationship in words before writing equations.\n\nPredict 1 ÷ 1/16 without drawing all sixteen pieces. Then explain what 1/2 ÷ 1/16 means and why it equals eight. A good generalisation lets you solve a new case while still being able to return to the physical model if challenged."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Before every division, identify the starting amount, the group size or number of recipients, and what the quotient measures. Estimate whether the quotient should be greater or smaller than one. Then verify by multiplying quotient and divisor to recover the dividend.\n\nFor a final notebook task, invent a sharing story and a measuring-groups story that both use 3/4. Explain why one answer has a liquid-volume unit while the other may count cups. This is the understanding that makes the reciprocal rule reliable instead of fragile."}]},{"id":"m-remainders","title":"Fractions when the whole changes","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"fractionProblem","prerequisites":["m-fraction-division"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"Fraction applications, P4–P6 pp.37,41,43","goals":["Label the reference whole at each stage","Work forwards and backwards","Distinguish a fraction removed from a fixed amount removed"],"visual":"remainder","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"The word remaining changes the model","text":"Euna spends one quarter of her money, then one third of the remaining money. The second fraction refers to a smaller whole. We cannot simply add 1/4 and 1/3 of the original amount. Draw one bar for the original amount, shade the first quarter, and label the unshaded three quarters 'new reference whole'.\n\nThe phrase 'one third of the remainder' means partitioning that unshaded portion into three equal parts. Each of those parts is one quarter of the original bar. This picture reveals the relationship before any original dollar amount is known."},{"title":"Follow the amount forwards","text":"Suppose the original amount is $80. After spending a quarter, Euna has 3/4 × $80 = $60. She next spends one third of $60, which is $20, leaving $40. The final remainder is one half of the original amount.\n\nWe can express the same reasoning without dollars: remaining fraction = 3/4 × 2/3 = 1/2. The factors describe what survives each stage. Multiplying the spent fractions instead would describe a different quantity. Label 'left' and 'spent' explicitly in your notebook."},{"title":"Work backwards from the end","text":"Now suppose only the final $40 is given. It represents two thirds of the amount before the second purchase. One third is $20, so that earlier amount is $60. This $60 represents three quarters of the original. One quarter is $20, so the original was $80.\n\nWorking backwards reverses each relationship in reverse order. It is not enough to reverse operations while keeping their original order. Draw arrows between 'original', 'after first purchase' and 'after second purchase', and write a fraction of the earlier amount on each arrow."},{"title":"A different kind of second step","text":"A bottle starts full. One quarter of the water is poured out, then another 120 ml is poured out. There are 330 ml left. Here the second removal is a fixed volume, not a fraction of the remainder. Add back 120 ml first to recover 450 ml after the first removal.\n\nThat 450 ml is three quarters of the full bottle. One quarter is 150 ml, giving an original volume of 600 ml. Check forwards: 600 − 150 − 120 = 330. Similar-looking wording can encode different relationships; identify the quantities rather than matching the question to a memorised template."},{"title":"Spot the hidden assumption","text":"A pupil says spending half your money twice leaves nothing. That is true if both halves mean half of the original amount. It is false if the second half means half of what remains: first one half remains, then half of that remains, leaving one quarter of the original.\n\nAsk the pupil to write two precise stories and solve each with $40. One ends at $0 and the other at $10. The disagreement is about the reference whole, not about arithmetic. Clear labels can resolve an apparently difficult problem before calculating."},{"title":"Guided task with a diagram","text":"A class lends one third of its books, then one quarter of the books still present. It ends with 30 books. The last 30 are three quarters of the intermediate amount, so the intermediate amount is 40. That 40 is two thirds of the original, so the original is 60.\n\nDraw an original bar in twelve equal parts. Removing one third takes four parts. Removing one quarter of the remaining eight takes two more, leaving six of twelve. Explain why this confirms that the final 30 books represent half of the original collection."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Create a two-stage story using a fraction followed by a fixed amount. Give only the final remainder to another learner. Ask them to identify the two reference quantities before solving. Keep your own forward calculation as an independent check of their answer.\n\nBefore practice, use this checklist: name each whole, identify whether each change is fractional or fixed, show one intermediate amount, and check the final result forwards. The method should work for money, water, ribbon or books because the underlying relationship is the same."}]},{"id":"m-decimals","title":"Decimals are fractions in place-value clothing","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"decimal","prerequisites":["m-fractions","m-operations"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.38; P5 p.41","goals":["Read and compare decimal places","Explain decimal operations","Connect decimals, fractions and measurement"],"visual":"place","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Extend the place-value chart","text":"Place a decimal point after the ones column. To its right are tenths, hundredths and thousandths. Each step right divides the unit size by ten. Thus 0.406 means four tenths, no hundredths and six thousandths: 406/1000. The zero still protects a place, just as in whole numbers.\n\nA decimal with more digits is not necessarily larger. Compare 0.8 and 0.75 by writing 0.80 and 0.75. Eight tenths are greater than seven tenths, so 0.8 is greater. Adding a zero at the far right of a decimal does not change its value; adding one between existing digits usually does."},{"title":"Add and subtract matching units","text":"For 3.4 + 0.27, write 3.40 + 0.27 and align the place values. The result is 3.67. Aligning decimal points is useful because it aligns ones with ones and hundredths with hundredths. It is not an arbitrary writing convention.\n\nFor 5 − 2.38, write 5.00 and regroup as needed. The difference is 2.62. Verify by 2.38 + 2.62 = 5.00. Money can make hundredths familiar, but decimals also describe length, mass, temperature and other quantities that may need more than two decimal places."},{"title":"Multiply and divide with meaning","text":"To calculate 0.24 × 3, regard 0.24 as 24 hundredths. Three copies are 72 hundredths, or 0.72. For 0.24 ÷ 3, share 24 hundredths into three groups to get eight hundredths, or 0.08. The place value explains where the digits belong.\n\nMultiplying by 100 changes 0.247 into 24.7 because each digit gains a value one hundred times as large. Dividing 24.7 by 100 reverses that scaling. A description about 'moving the decimal point' can help record the calculation, but cannot replace the explanation of which quantity changes."},{"title":"Worked example: a measurement","text":"A ribbon is 2.4 m long and is cut into six equal pieces. Each piece measures 2.4 ÷ 6 = 0.4 m. Convert to centimetres by noting that one metre contains 100 centimetres: 0.4 m = 40 cm. Check that six pieces of 40 cm total 240 cm, or 2.4 m.\n\nA different ribbon measures 2.43 m. Dividing by six gives 0.405 m, or 40.5 cm. Do not round halfway through unless instructed. A premature approximation can produce a total that no longer matches the original length."},{"title":"Rounding and the boundary","text":"Round 7.468 to two decimal places. The neighbouring hundredths are 7.46 and 7.47; the midpoint is 7.465. The number lies above that midpoint, so round to 7.47. Keep enough digits to decide the requested rounding once, from the original number.\n\nFor example, 1.449 rounded directly to one decimal place is 1.4. Rounding first to two decimal places gives 1.45, which would then round to 1.5. This is why repeated rounding can change the answer. Use the original measurement when reporting the requested precision."},{"title":"Guided comparison","text":"Arrange 0.507, 0.57 and 0.075 in increasing order. Rename them as thousandths: 507, 570 and 75 thousandths. The order is 0.075, 0.507, 0.57. Explain the first place where each comparison is decided.\n\nNext turn 3/8 into a decimal by division, obtaining 0.375. Reverse the representation: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8. Converting in both directions checks that the two notations describe the same number. Some fractions have non-terminating decimals, so a rounded decimal may only be approximate."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Find a supermarket mass label or a measuring jug scale. Read the unit, identify what one small division represents and estimate where a quantity between marks would fall. The scale, not the number of printed digits, determines what you can measure confidently.\n\nBefore practice, explain why 0.6 = 0.60, why 0.06 is different, and why 0.6 × 10 = 6. If these statements make sense in tenths and hundredths, decimal algorithms become easier to reconstruct when you forget a rule."}]},{"id":"m-percent","title":"Percentages describe a part per hundred","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"percent","prerequisites":["m-decimals"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P5 p.41","goals":["Connect percentages, fractions and decimals","Calculate discounts and stated taxes","Distinguish a rate from the amount it produces"],"visual":"percent","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Name the reference quantity","text":"Percent means per hundred. Twenty-five percent describes 25 out of every 100 equal parts of a reference quantity. It equals 25/100 = 1/4 = 0.25. A percentage is not a dollar amount until you identify the quantity that counts as 100%.\n\nIf 25% of one class is 8 pupils and 25% of another class is 10 pupils, the classes have different totals. Equal percentages do not guarantee equal counts. Always write '100% = …' beside your first model. This habit also prepares you for reverse percentages and changing quantities."},{"title":"Find a percentage part","text":"To find 15% of $80, first find 10% = $8 and 5% = $4. Their sum is $12. Alternatively calculate 15/100 × 80 = 12. These are different descriptions of the same proportional relationship, not competing rules.\n\nSome percentages have useful fraction forms: 50% is one half, 25% is one quarter, 20% is one fifth and 10% is one tenth. Derive each by simplifying the fraction over 100. When a percentage is unfamiliar, the unitary method of finding 1% still works."},{"title":"Worked example: a discount","text":"A bag is priced at $60 and has a 15% discount. The discount is 15% of the original $60, or $9. The sale price is $60 − $9 = $51. Equivalently, the customer pays 85% of $60. Distinguish the amount taken off from the amount paid.\n\nCheck that the sale price is less than the original and that their difference is $9. A common error reports $9 as the price because the percentage calculation itself was correct. Solving the requested question requires one further interpretation."},{"title":"A stated tax or fee","text":"For a practice problem, suppose a service costs $50 before a stated 8% tax. The tax is $4 and the total is $54. This is a hypothetical rate supplied by the problem, not a claim about the current Singapore GST rate. Use the rate given unless a real-world task explicitly asks you to look it up.\n\nIf a discount applies before the tax, the tax reference may be the discounted price. Write each stage in order rather than adding or subtracting percentages blindly. For example, a $100 item with 20% off becomes $80; a stated 10% tax on $80 adds $8, giving $88."},{"title":"Annual interest as an application","text":"Simple annual interest at a stated 3% on $200 is 3/100 × 200 = $6 for one year. The interest is the extra amount, while $206 is principal plus that year's interest. Name which of these the question requests.\n\nDo not assume every savings product works by this simplified model. A question may specify changing rates, different time periods or interest added to the balance. For core practice, use the stated annual relationship. General financial claims require the actual product's terms, beyond this mathematical example."},{"title":"Guided task and error analysis","text":"A pupil scores 18 out of 24 on a quiz. The fraction is 18/24 = 3/4, so the percentage is 75%. Dividing 18 by 100 would answer a different question because the actual total is 24. To express a part as a percentage, divide the part by its whole, then multiply by 100%.\n\nCompare 18/24 with 20/25. The second is 80%, so it represents a larger fraction correct even though the totals differ. Percentages provide a common scale, but they do not prove the quizzes had the same difficulty or measured the same skills."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Choose an everyday percentage claim and identify its numerator, denominator and reference group. A percentage without a clearly defined whole can mislead. Ask whether the claim concerns the original amount, a current amount, a total population or only a selected sample.\n\nBefore practice, explain both 30% of 50 and 30 as a percentage of 50. The first asks for a part and gives 15. The second asks for a rate and gives 60%. Similar numbers can belong to different questions."}]},{"id":"m-percent-whole","title":"Reverse percentages and changing bases","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"percent","prerequisites":["m-percent","m-remainders"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P6 p.43","goals":["Recover an unknown whole","Calculate percentage change from the original","Explain why equal percentage changes may not cancel"],"visual":"percent","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"The given amount may be only a part","text":"If 20% of a group is 14 pupils, the group is not 14 pupils. Fourteen corresponds to one of five equal 20% portions. Therefore the whole contains 14 × 5 = 70 pupils. Draw five equal boxes and label one box 14 to make the correspondence explicit.\n\nThe general relationship is part = percentage fraction × whole. To recover the whole, divide the part by that fraction. For 14 ÷ 0.2, the quotient 70 is larger than 14 because the known part is less than the whole. Predicting that direction provides an immediate check."},{"title":"Worked example: reverse discount","text":"A jacket sells for $72 after a 20% discount. The sale price represents 80% of the original, not 20%. Eighty percent is four fifths, so one fifth is $18 and five fifths are $90. The original price was $90 and the discount was $18.\n\nCheck forwards: 20% of $90 is $18, and $90 − $18 = $72. Adding 20% of $72 would give $86.40, which fails this check. The percentage base for the original discount was $90, not the later sale price."},{"title":"Percentage increase and decrease","text":"A plant grows from 40 cm to 50 cm. The increase is 10 cm. As a fraction of the original height, that is 10/40 = 1/4, or 25%. The calculation describes how large the change is compared with the starting amount.\n\nIf the height instead falls from 50 cm to 40 cm, the decrease is still 10 cm but the base is now 50 cm. The percentage decrease is 10/50 = 20%. Equal absolute differences can produce different percentage changes because their reference quantities differ."},{"title":"Two changes in sequence","text":"Start with $100. Increase it by 20% to get $120. Then decrease the new amount by 20%, removing $24 and leaving $96. The increase and decrease do not cancel because the second 20% is a percentage of a different whole.\n\nA useful representation is a chain of multipliers: 100 × 1.2 × 0.8 = 96. The final amount is 96% of the original, an overall decrease of 4%. In core work you may use a bar or unitary method instead; the essential idea is to name the whole at each stage."},{"title":"Find the missing part of a tank","text":"A tank is 60% full. Adding 18 litres fills it completely. The added water represents 40% of the capacity. Ten percent is 18 ÷ 4 = 4.5 litres, so the full capacity is 45 litres. The starting water volume was 27 litres.\n\nCheck both the percentage and the physical total: 27 + 18 = 45 and 27/45 = 60%. A pupil who treats 18 litres as 60% will obtain a different answer by using the wrong correspondence. Write down the percentage that belongs to every given quantity."},{"title":"Guided task: compare claims","text":"Two shops offer a discount of $12. At Shop A the original price is $60, and at Shop B it is $80. Shop A's discount is 20%; Shop B's is 15%. A larger percentage discount does not necessarily mean a larger dollar saving when original prices differ.\n\nNow ask which shop has the lower final price: $48 versus $68. This is another question again. Keep 'discount amount', 'discount percentage' and 'price paid' as three distinct quantities. An advertisement may emphasise whichever looks most attractive."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Explain to a partner why a 50% increase followed by a 50% decrease does not restore an amount. Use a simple starting value such as 20 and show the intermediate quantity. Then ask what percentage decrease would restore 30 to 20: the required decrease is one third of 30.\n\nFor independent work, name the 100% quantity, label the given amount's percentage, calculate one convenient portion, and verify forwards. These steps apply to population changes, capacity, prices and test-score comparisons without relying on a memorised story type."}]},{"id":"m-ratio","title":"Ratios and equal units","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"ratio","prerequisites":["m-fractions","m-percent"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P6 p.43","goals":["Read two- and three-part ratios","Connect a ratio with a fraction of the total","Track an unchanged quantity"],"visual":"ratio","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A relationship between quantities","text":"A mixture contains two cups of concentrate for every three cups of water. Its ratio of concentrate to water is 2 : 3. The order matters: water to concentrate is 3 : 2. The ratio describes relative amounts, not necessarily the actual number of cups in a particular jug.\n\nFour cups of concentrate and six cups of water have the same ratio. Each part has been multiplied by two. Adding the same amount to both quantities does not generally preserve the ratio: 2 : 3 and 3 : 4 describe different mixtures. Use matching units before writing a ratio."},{"title":"Draw ratio units","text":"Represent 2 : 3 by two equal boxes for one quantity and three equal boxes for the other. The total contains five boxes. Therefore concentrate is 2/5 of the whole mixture, not 2/3 of it. The fraction 2/3 compares concentrate with water only.\n\nIf the mixture totals 25 cups, one box represents 25 ÷ 5 = 5 cups. Concentrate is 10 cups and water is 15 cups. The boxes have equal values within this model. They are not physical cups until we calculate what each box represents."},{"title":"Work from a difference","text":"Ali and Bea have beads in the ratio 3 : 5. Bea has 14 more beads than Ali. The difference of two ratio units corresponds to 14 beads, so one unit is seven. Ali has 21 beads and Bea has 35. Their total is 56.\n\nA common mistake divides 14 by eight because eight is the number of total units. But 14 describes the difference, not the total. Before using any given number, draw an arrow showing which part of the model it labels. This single step resolves many ratio difficulties."},{"title":"Three-part sharing","text":"Share $72 among three people in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. The total is nine units, each worth $8. The shares are $16, $24 and $32. Add them to check $72, then divide by their common factor eight to check the original ratio.\n\nTo simplify 12 : 18 : 24, divide all terms by the same common factor six, giving 2 : 3 : 4. Simplifying only two terms would alter the three-way relationship. The current primary core uses whole-number ratio terms; more elaborate ratio notation belongs to later enrichment."},{"title":"A changing collection","text":"Initially red and blue beads are in the ratio 2 : 3. Only red beads are added, and the ratio becomes 4 : 3. Since the blue count is unchanged and its ratio number is already three in both descriptions, the same unit size works. The added red beads represent two original units.\n\nIf the stated ratios were 2 : 3 and 5 : 6, first rename the initial ratio as 4 : 6. Now six units of blue match in both states, making the red increase one common unit. 'One ratio unit' has no fixed value across separately drawn models until a relationship establishes it."},{"title":"Guided task","text":"A recipe uses flour, sugar and butter in the ratio 5 : 2 : 3 by mass. If butter is 180 g, three units are 180 g, so one unit is 60 g. Flour is 300 g and sugar is 120 g. Total mass is 600 g.\n\nAsk whether doubling only the flour keeps the recipe's proportions. It does not. Doubling every ingredient does. Explain the distinction using equivalent ratios, then describe why adding 60 g to every ingredient changes the relative composition even though the additions are equal."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"When you read a ratio, identify the order, the units and whether the given amount labels one part, a total or a difference. If quantities change, search for what stays unchanged: a count, a total or a difference. Scale models to that common anchor.\n\nBefore practice, explain why a 2 : 3 ratio gives a two-fifths share of the total. Use both boxes and actual objects. Then create a problem where the same ratio comes with a known difference instead of a known total, and solve it without changing the meaning of a box."}]},{"id":"m-rate","title":"Rates connect different units","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"rate","prerequisites":["m-decimals","m-ratio"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P5 p.41","goals":["Interpret per-unit quantities","Calculate rates and totals","Check whether a constant-rate assumption is justified"],"visual":"rate","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"What does per mean?","text":"A printer produces 18 pages per minute. The rate compares a number of pages with a duration. 'Per minute' means for each one minute under the conditions stated. A price of $3 per kilogram and a tap delivering 2 litres per minute have the same mathematical structure even though their units differ.\n\nWrite both units. Eighteen alone could mean pages, minutes or dollars. Eighteen pages per minute describes a relationship. In a constant-rate model, doubling the number of minutes doubles the pages printed. Real devices may pause or slow down, so a word problem must state or justify the constant-rate assumption."},{"title":"Build a rate table","text":"If four notebooks cost $10 at a fixed price each, one notebook costs $10 ÷ 4 = $2.50. Three notebooks cost $7.50 and eight cost $20. Use a table with a column for notebooks and a column for total cost. Every row should preserve total cost divided by number of notebooks.\n\nThis is the unitary method: find the amount for one unit, then scale. Sometimes scaling directly is simpler. Twelve notebooks are three times as many as four, so they cost three times $10. Both methods depend on the same constant price relationship."},{"title":"Worked example with an unknown duration","text":"A tap fills a container at 3 litres per minute. How long does it take to deliver 24 litres? Count the number of three-litre groups: 24 ÷ 3 = 8 minutes. Conversely, eight minutes at this rate gives 3 × 8 = 24 litres.\n\nIf the container already holds 6 litres and needs to reach 24 litres, only 18 litres must be added. The duration is 18 ÷ 3 = 6 minutes. A correct rate calculation can still answer the wrong question if you forget the starting amount."},{"title":"Compare options fairly","text":"A 600 g packet costs $4.20 and a 1 kg packet costs $6.50. Convert both to a common mass unit before comparing prices. The smaller packet costs $4.20 ÷ 6 = $0.70 per 100 g. The larger costs $6.50 ÷ 10 = $0.65 per 100 g.\n\nThe larger packet has the lower unit price, but the better purchase may also depend on how much you need and whether any is wasted. Mathematics answers the stated comparison; a practical decision may use other relevant evidence. Explain the distinction instead of declaring that the largest packet is always best."},{"title":"Diagnose an assumption","text":"A taxi fare contains a fixed starting charge plus a charge for distance. Doubling the distance need not double the total fare. This is not a constant total-cost-per-kilometre relationship. The starting charge remains the same while only one part scales.\n\nLikewise, a worker who finishes one job in ten minutes may need rest or setup time before the next. Use a constant rate only when the problem gives that model. Spotting a missing assumption is a reasoning skill, not a reason to invent details that make the arithmetic convenient."},{"title":"Guided task","text":"A machine packs 84 biscuits in seven minutes at a constant rate. First calculate 12 biscuits per minute. In 15 minutes it packs 180 biscuits. To pack 300 biscuits it takes 25 minutes. Verify each answer by returning to the same rate.\n\nNow suppose a five-minute setup is needed before packing begins. Packing 300 biscuits still takes 25 active minutes, but the whole task takes 30 minutes. Draw a timeline separating setup from production. Naming the two kinds of time prevents a misleading average."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Choose a rate you see at home: filling a bottle, paying for fruit or printing pages. Identify the two quantities and propose a measurement that could test whether the rate is reasonably constant. Record several pairs rather than relying on one observation.\n\nBefore practice, write the unknown's unit and decide whether you need to multiply or divide. Multiplication combines rate and number of units into a total; division can recover either missing quantity. Always test the proposed answer against the original relationship."}]},{"id":"m-algebra","title":"Letters, expressions and balanced equations","subject":"maths","strand":"Number and algebra","skill":"algebra","prerequisites":["m-operations"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P6 p.43","goals":["Use a letter for an unknown","Simplify and substitute","Solve a linear equation while preserving equality"],"visual":"balance","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A letter names a quantity","text":"If a notebook costs n dollars, three notebooks cost 3n dollars. The letter is not a unit and does not stand for the word notebook: it represents the unknown numerical price of one notebook. In one problem, keep its meaning consistent unless a change is explicitly defined.\n\nAn expression such as 3n + 2 describes a quantity. An equation such as 3n + 2 = 17 states that two quantities are equal. You can evaluate an expression when n is given; you can solve an equation to find which n makes the statement true."},{"title":"Combine like quantities","text":"Three bags each containing x counters and two more such bags contain 3x + 2x = 5x counters. If there are also four loose counters, the expression is 5x + 4. You cannot combine that as 9x because the four loose counters are not four more bags of x.\n\nSimilarly, 7a − 2a + 3 = 5a + 3. Core simplification here involves simple linear expressions without brackets. More complicated algebra is enrichment rather than something to rush into before the meaning of a letter is secure."},{"title":"Substitution is a check of meaning","text":"Evaluate 4n + 3 when n = 5. Four groups of five plus three give 23. The notation 4n means 4 × n, not a two-digit number with n pasted after 4. Write the multiplication sign during substitution if it helps keep the meaning visible.\n\nFor n/3 + 2 when n = 12, divide twelve by three before adding two to obtain six. Follow the same order of operations used with ordinary numbers. Algebra has not created a new arithmetic system; it has given us a way to describe relationships before every number is known."},{"title":"Solve while balancing","text":"To solve 3x + 5 = 26, think of a balanced scale. Remove five from both sides, leaving 3x = 21. Divide both sides into three equal groups to get x = 7. Performing the same valid operation on both sides preserves their equality.\n\nCheck by substitution in the original equation: 3 × 7 + 5 = 26. Do not check only in the final simplified equation, because an error might have occurred earlier. Describing terms as 'moving to the other side' is shorthand; balancing explains why their signs or operations change."},{"title":"Translate a story","text":"Three identical tickets and a $4 booking fee cost $31 altogether. Let t be the price of one ticket. The relationship is 3t + 4 = 31. Remove the booking fee to leave $27 for the tickets, then divide by three to find t = 9.\n\nThe answer is $9 per ticket, not simply 9 tickets. If the booking fee were $4 for each ticket instead of one fee for the order, the equation would describe a different total. Write a sentence defining every fixed and repeated amount before forming the equation."},{"title":"Diagnose and practise","text":"A pupil solves x + 8 = 20 by subtracting eight only on the left, producing x = 20. The two sides no longer describe equal quantities after that unequal operation. Correct it by subtracting eight from both sides to get x = 12.\n\nNow solve 5y − 6 = 29 using a bar and an equation. Restore the six first, making five equal groups total 35. Then divide by five to obtain seven. Show that the pictorial and symbolic methods preserve the same relationship."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Invent a real situation described by 4p + 6 = 38. Specify what p measures, what is repeated four times and what the six represents. Solve it, then check that the solution makes practical sense in your invented setting.\n\nBefore independent practice, explain the difference between an expression and an equation, and justify one balancing step in words. Algebra becomes a useful reasoning language when you can connect symbols to quantities and verify their meaning."}]},{"id":"m-measure","title":"Measurement, units and sensible precision","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"measure","prerequisites":["m-decimals"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"Earlier foundations pp.31–36; P5 p.41","goals":["Choose units and apparatus","Convert by the size of a unit","Distinguish mass, volume and capacity"],"visual":"","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"What property is being measured?","text":"Length measures how far along something extends. Mass measures the amount of matter using units such as grams and kilograms. Volume measures occupied space; capacity describes how much a container can hold. These quantities are related in everyday tasks but are not interchangeable.\n\nA litre is a volume unit, not a mass unit. Two liquids with equal volumes need not have equal masses. In mathematics, use the conversion or physical condition given by the problem; do not assume that one millilitre always has a mass of one gram for every substance."},{"title":"Units are repeatable references","text":"One metre is 100 centimetres, one kilometre is 1,000 metres, one kilogram is 1,000 grams and one litre is 1,000 millilitres. When converting to smaller units, more units are needed to describe the same quantity. Thus 2.35 m becomes 235 cm.\n\nWhen converting 750 ml to litres, divide by 1,000 to obtain 0.75 litre. The liquid amount has not changed; only the name of the unit and the number used to count it have changed. This is a form of equivalence, not a physical transformation."},{"title":"Read a scale carefully","text":"A measuring jug may label 100 ml and 200 ml with four equally spaced marks between them. Those marks divide the gap into five intervals, so each interval is 20 ml. Count spaces between marks rather than only the printed marks themselves.\n\nRead a ruler from zero when possible. If an object begins at 2 cm and ends at 9.5 cm, its length is 9.5 − 2 = 7.5 cm. Reporting 9.5 cm would confuse the ending position with the distance between endpoints. Sketch both endpoints before subtracting."},{"title":"Worked comparison","text":"A parcel has mass 1.8 kg and another has mass 1,950 g. Convert to a common unit: 1.8 kg = 1,800 g. The second parcel is heavier by 150 g. Alternatively convert 1,950 g to 1.95 kg and find a difference of 0.15 kg.\n\nCheck that both forms of the difference agree. Writing 1.8 − 1,950 without matching units mixes quantities expressed on different scales. Units should stay attached throughout the calculation, particularly when a problem includes several kinds of measurement."},{"title":"Precision and estimation","text":"A ruler marked in centimetres cannot support a confident measurement to one thousandth of a centimetre. Record a sensible precision for the apparatus. In a word problem, follow the requested accuracy; in a real measurement, acknowledge uncertainty rather than inventing extra digits.\n\nEstimate first. The length of a classroom is more plausibly several metres than several millimetres. A child's mass is more plausibly tens of kilograms than tens of grams. These comparisons catch unit errors that an otherwise correct multiplication algorithm might not reveal."},{"title":"Guided practical task","text":"Choose three safe household objects and estimate their lengths. Measure them with a ruler, record the units and compare with your estimates. If your ruler is shorter than an object, measure successive lengths without leaving gaps or counting the same section twice.\n\nPour room-temperature water between two containers of different shapes with an adult nearby if needed. A higher water level does not necessarily mean more water: the container's cross-sectional shape also matters. Use a measuring jug to compare volumes instead of judging from height alone."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Create a conversion chain for 3.06 kg: 3 kg + 60 g = 3,060 g. Explain why the decimal part is sixty grams rather than six grams. Then create a similar chain for metres and centimetres, noticing that their conversion factor is one hundred rather than one thousand.\n\nBefore practice, identify the measured property, choose a common unit and predict whether the numerical value should increase or decrease after conversion. Finally check the answer against a familiar object of similar size. Measurement connects abstract calculation to the physical world."}]},{"id":"m-time","title":"Time is a number line with a different base","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"time","prerequisites":["m-operations"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"Time foundations pp.33–36; duration applications","goals":["Read 12- and 24-hour time","Calculate elapsed time across boundaries","Work backwards from an arrival time"],"visual":"","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Distinguish a time from a duration","text":"At 14:35 describes a moment on a clock. For 35 minutes describes a duration. Subtracting two moments can produce a duration, and adding a duration to a moment can produce a new moment. These quantities play different roles even when their written numbers look similar.\n\nAn hour contains sixty minutes, and a minute contains sixty seconds. Clock arithmetic is not ordinary decimal arithmetic. The notation 1 hour 30 minutes means one and a half hours, not 1.30 hours. Convert units or use a timeline to keep the relationship clear."},{"title":"Read the clock unambiguously","text":"In the 24-hour system, 09:15 is morning and 21:15 is evening. Noon is 12:00 and midnight begins a new day at 00:00. In 12-hour notation, 12 noon is 12 p.m. and 12 midnight is 12 a.m.; when confusion is possible, write noon or midnight explicitly.\n\nTo convert 3:40 p.m. to 24-hour time, add twelve to the hour to get 15:40. Do not add twelve to 12 noon. For 00:25, write 12:25 a.m. in the 12-hour system. The date becomes relevant whenever an interval crosses midnight."},{"title":"Bridge to a convenient boundary","text":"A lesson begins at 10:45 and ends at 12:20. From 10:45 to 11:00 is 15 minutes; from 11:00 to 12:00 is 60 minutes; from 12:00 to 12:20 is 20 minutes. Total duration is 95 minutes, or one hour 35 minutes.\n\nThe timeline makes regrouping visible. A written subtraction method must borrow one hour as sixty minutes, not as one hundred. Both methods work if they respect the unit relationship. Choose a method that lets you explain every step rather than one that merely resembles whole-number subtraction."},{"title":"Work backwards","text":"A train arrives at 16:10 after a journey of one hour 45 minutes. Subtract an hour to reach 15:10, then subtract ten minutes to 15:00 and thirty-five more to 14:25. The departure time was 14:25.\n\nCheck forwards: 14:25 plus one hour is 15:25, plus 45 minutes is 16:10. In reverse-time problems, this forward check is especially useful because it detects mistakes around hour boundaries. If there is a separate waiting period, label it as a separate segment."},{"title":"Cross midnight carefully","text":"A trip begins at 23:35 and ends at 00:20 the next day. There are 25 minutes until midnight and 20 minutes afterwards, giving 45 minutes. Simply subtracting the displayed hour values would give a meaningless negative duration.\n\nFor multi-day contexts, include the date or an explicit 'next day' label. Do not invent a next-day assumption if the problem is ambiguous. Good mathematical communication includes clarifying the timeline before calculating rather than hiding uncertainty inside an answer."},{"title":"Guided planning task","text":"You need to arrive at an appointment at 15:00. Travel takes 35 minutes and getting ready takes 20 minutes. Work backwards to depart at 14:25 and begin getting ready at 14:05. Draw three labelled moments and the two durations between them.\n\nNow add a desired ten-minute arrival buffer. The appointment still begins at 15:00, but your target arrival is 14:50, departure 14:15 and preparation start 13:55. Explain why the buffer changes your plan without changing the actual travel duration."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Make a short timetable containing two activities, a break and a journey. Ask someone to find a missing start time or duration. Check their answer by tracing the entire sequence forwards and ensuring the total duration equals the sum of all segments.\n\nBefore independent practice, name the start, end, duration and date when relevant. Decide whether the unknown is a clock reading or an amount of time, then give it in the appropriate notation. That small distinction prevents many otherwise avoidable errors."}]},{"id":"m-area","title":"Area measures inside; perimeter measures around","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"area","prerequisites":["m-measure"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.39; P5 p.42","goals":["Derive rectangle and triangle areas","Find missing dimensions","Trace composite boundaries without internal edges"],"visual":"area","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Two measurements of one shape","text":"A garden needs fencing around its edge and grass over its surface. Fencing depends on perimeter, measured in length units. Grass coverage depends on area, measured in square units. The same shape has both measurements, but they answer different questions.\n\nCover a rectangle with square tiles. If each row contains eight tiles and there are five rows, it covers forty unit squares. This explains area = length × breadth for a rectangle. Counting boundary edges explains perimeter = 2 × (length + breadth). State the unit before calculating."},{"title":"Recover a missing dimension","text":"A rectangle has area 48 cm² and length 8 cm. Six rows of eight unit squares cover the rectangle, so the breadth is 48 ÷ 8 = 6 cm. Dividing area by a length recovers another length. Do not report six square centimetres as the breadth.\n\nIf a rectangle instead has perimeter 28 cm and length 8 cm, half the perimeter is 14 cm, equal to length plus breadth. Therefore breadth is 14 − 8 = 6 cm. The same answer can arise from different information, but the steps must match the measurement supplied."},{"title":"Why a triangle uses one half","text":"Take two congruent right triangles and join them to make a rectangle. Each triangle occupies half the area of that rectangle. More generally, a triangle with base b and perpendicular height h has area b × h ÷ 2. The height measures the shortest distance from the opposite vertex to the line containing the base.\n\nA sloping side is not automatically the height. For a triangle with base 10 cm and perpendicular height 6 cm, area is 30 cm². If the height falls outside an obtuse triangle, extend the base line mentally or on the given diagram. The perpendicular relationship still determines the height."},{"title":"Break a composite shape into parts","text":"An L-shaped floor can be viewed as a large rectangle with a smaller rectangle removed. If the outer rectangle is 8 m by 6 m and the missing corner is 3 m by 2 m, the area is 48 − 6 = 42 m². Alternatively partition the remaining floor into two non-overlapping rectangles.\n\nFor perimeter, trace the actual exposed boundary. Internal dividing lines used in an area calculation are not fencing edges. Mark every boundary segment once. If a length is missing, deduce it from aligned totals, and explain the subtraction you use."},{"title":"Challenge an appealing rule","text":"Two rectangles can have the same area but different perimeters. A 3 by 4 rectangle has area 12 and perimeter 14; a 2 by 6 rectangle has area 12 and perimeter 16. Equal coverage does not guarantee equal boundary length.\n\nLikewise, doubling both dimensions multiplies area by four but perimeter by two. Explain this with rows and columns, not just by inserting numbers into formulae. The different scaling reveals why square units behave differently from ordinary length units."},{"title":"Guided paper activity","text":"Draw a rectangle on squared paper and cut it along a diagonal. Rearrange the two triangles without stretching them. Their combined area remains unchanged because the same pieces are present. The external boundary may change when edges that were separate become joined.\n\nNow draw two triangles on the same base with the same perpendicular height but different sloping sides. Their areas are equal. Use a ruler to compare heights and explain why appearance alone can make one seem larger. Measurements should come from stated dimensions or reliable construction."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For each problem, say whether you are covering, enclosing or finding a dimension. Label square units for area and length units for perimeter. Before adding areas, ensure the pieces neither overlap nor leave gaps; before adding edge lengths, exclude internal lines.\n\nA final explanation should connect the formula with rows, equal halves or decomposition. If you forget a formula, reconstruct it from those ideas. This makes unfamiliar composite shapes manageable without requiring a new rule for every outline."}]},{"id":"m-angles","title":"Angles and shape properties","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"angle","prerequisites":["m-number"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.39; P5 p.42; P6 p.44","goals":["Name and measure angles","Deduce unknown angles from stated properties","Distinguish evidence from the appearance of a drawing"],"visual":"angles","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"An angle measures a turn","text":"An angle describes the turn between two rays meeting at a vertex. Its size does not depend on how long the drawn rays are. A right angle is 90°, a straight angle 180° and one full turn 360°. An acute angle is smaller than 90°; an obtuse angle lies between 90° and 180°.\n\nIn ∠ABC, the middle letter B names the vertex. When using a protractor, place its centre at the vertex and align its zero line with one ray. Choose the scale starting at that zero, rather than whichever printed number is easier to see."},{"title":"Combine known relationships","text":"Angles forming a straight line total 180°. Angles around a point total 360°. When two straight lines cross, vertically opposite angles are equal. This follows because each is supplementary to the same adjacent angle: subtract that adjacent angle from 180°.\n\nFor adjacent angles of 65° and x on a straight line, x = 180° − 65° = 115°. State the straight-line relationship, not just the subtraction. In a complex diagram, a correct arithmetic operation needs a geometric reason to justify using those particular angles together."},{"title":"Triangles","text":"The three interior angles of a triangle total 180°. A triangle with angles 48° and 67° therefore has a third angle of 65°. In an isosceles triangle, equal sides face equal angles. In an equilateral triangle all three sides and angles are equal, giving 60° each.\n\nIf an isosceles triangle's unequal angle is 40°, the other two share 140°, so each is 70°. Do not assume the labelled angle is the unequal one merely because it is drawn at the top. Use the equal-side marks or an explicit statement to identify the relevant pair."},{"title":"Quadrilaterals","text":"A rectangle has four right angles and opposite sides equal and parallel. A square also has all four sides equal. A parallelogram has opposite sides parallel and equal, opposite angles equal, and adjacent interior angles summing to 180°. A rhombus is a parallelogram with all sides equal.\n\nIn Singapore school usage, a trapezium has one pair of opposite sides parallel. For this course, use the properties stated or marked in a question when classifying special cases. Do not infer equal sides or extra parallel sides solely from how a trapezium has been sketched."},{"title":"Worked deduction","text":"A parallelogram has one interior angle of 72°. Its adjacent angle is 180° − 72° = 108°, and the opposite angles match these values. If a diagonal divides a known angle into two pieces, their sum still equals the original interior angle.\n\nSuppose the 108° angle is split into 35° and x. Then x = 73°. This is a sequence of deductions: first identify the whole angle using the shape property, then subtract the known part. Write those reasons beside the calculations so another person can follow the chain."},{"title":"Diagnose and investigate","text":"A pupil measures an angle from a screen drawing and gets 58°, while stated properties imply 60°. A schematic diagram may not be drawn to scale. For a deduction question, use given numbers and properties; measuring the picture is not a substitute for reasoning.\n\nCut a paper triangle and arrange its three corners along a straight line as an exploration of the angle sum. This physical demonstration helps you see the relationship, but imperfect cutting and measurement will not produce an exact proof. Distinguish an illustrative model from an exact logical argument."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Draw a diagram with two intersecting lines and label one angle. Deduce the other three using both the straight-line sum and vertically opposite equality. Then create an isosceles triangle problem and mark the equal sides clearly.\n\nBefore practice, name the vertex, identify the relationship, calculate, and check whether the result fits all given constraints. If a deduction requires an unmarked right angle or a new line not justified by the problem, reconsider it. The drawing is a record of information, not permission to invent it."}]},{"id":"m-solids","title":"Symmetry, solids and nets","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"spatial","prerequisites":["m-area","m-angles"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.39; P5 p.42","goals":["Test reflection symmetry","Connect faces, edges and vertices","Reason about folding nets"],"visual":"net","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Reflect rather than guess","text":"A line of symmetry divides a figure so that reflection across the line maps the figure onto itself. Fold a paper shape along a proposed line: matching boundary points must coincide. Equal area on either side is necessary for many examples, but is not enough to establish symmetry.\n\nA non-square rectangle has two lines of symmetry through the midpoints of opposite sides. Its diagonals are not reflection lines. A square has four: the two midlines and the two diagonals. Changing the shape's orientation on the page does not change its number of symmetry lines."},{"title":"Complete a reflection","text":"On squared paper, each reflected point lies the same perpendicular distance from the mirror line on the opposite side. If a corner is three squares to the left of a vertical mirror, its image is three squares to the right at the same height.\n\nReflect the important corners first, then join them in the same order. For a sloping mirror line, distance must still be perpendicular to that line, not simply counted horizontally. A ruler and careful construction help when visual intuition becomes unreliable."},{"title":"Know the parts of a solid","text":"A cube has six square faces, twelve edges and eight vertices. Each face meets neighbouring faces along edges; three edges meet at each vertex. A cuboid has the same counts, but its rectangular faces need not all be squares.\n\nA triangular prism has two congruent parallel triangular end faces and three rectangular side faces. A square-based pyramid has a square base and four triangular faces meeting at a vertex. Cylinders and cones include curved surfaces, so do not treat every surface as a flat polygon face."},{"title":"From a solid to a net","text":"A net is a connected flat arrangement of the faces that can fold into a solid without overlapping faces or leaving a missing face. Six squares alone do not guarantee a cube net. Their arrangement matters because folding may place two squares over the same face.\n\nChoose one square as the base and imagine folding its neighbours up. Then track where the remaining squares go. Opposite cube faces do not share an edge after folding. Marking each square with a different colour or letter helps you track faces through the transformation."},{"title":"A worked folding strategy","text":"For a strip of four equal squares, add one square above the second and one below the second. This is a valid cube net. The strip wraps around four side faces, while the two attached squares close the opposite ends. Build a paper model to test your mental prediction.\n\nA straight strip of six squares is not a cube net: folding successive edges cannot close the solid into six distinct face positions. Explain the overlap or missing closure, rather than merely reporting that it 'looks wrong'. The exact interactive diagram uses six counted squares; decorative illustrations are not folding templates."},{"title":"Guided spatial investigation","text":"Draw the valid six-square net described above. Before cutting, predict which faces will be opposite, then fold it and compare. If your prediction fails, trace the fold that changed your mental picture. The mismatch is useful evidence about the representation you used.\n\nNext build a small cuboid from unit cubes. Draw what you see from the front, side and top. A single view can hide cubes behind others, so it may not determine the full solid. State when more than one arrangement fits the supplied views."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Find a cardboard package and identify its solid shape. Carefully unfold a spare empty package with adult help for cutting, then distinguish structural faces from glue tabs. Tabs help assembly but do not count as additional faces of the mathematical solid.\n\nBefore practice, test symmetry by reflection and test nets by tracking actual face positions. These are transformation questions: ask what stays the same while the shape is reflected, rotated or folded. That habit is more dependable than memorising silhouettes."}]},{"id":"m-circles","title":"Circles and composite boundaries","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"circle","prerequisites":["m-area","m-fractions"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P6 p.44","goals":["Connect radius and diameter","Separate circumference from area","Include straight edges in parts of circles"],"visual":"circle","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Define the circle","text":"A circle consists of points at the same distance from a centre. That distance is its radius. A diameter passes through the centre and joins two points on the circle, so its length is twice the radius. A chord joins two points on the circle but need not pass through the centre.\n\nThe circumference is the distance around the full circle. The area is the space enclosed. We use π to express the constant ratio of circumference to diameter. When a problem specifies 3.14 or 22/7 for π, use that value consistently rather than switching approximations mid-calculation."},{"title":"Connect the formulae to meaning","text":"Circumference = π × diameter = 2πr. Area = πr², where r² means r × r. The units differ: circumference uses cm or m; area uses cm² or m². A formula written without its quantity and units is easy to misuse.\n\nDoubling a radius doubles circumference but quadruples area. Both width and height of the covered region scale. This parallels the rectangle lesson and helps you predict the effect of changing a circular garden or a wheel's size."},{"title":"Worked full-circle example","text":"A circle has radius 7 cm. Using π = 22/7, its circumference is 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm. Its area is 22/7 × 7 × 7 = 154 cm². A diagram should identify the radius clearly so you do not substitute the 14 cm diameter into the area formula.\n\nCheck with scale: the circle fits inside a 14 cm by 14 cm square of area 196 cm². An answer much larger than 196 cm² cannot be its area. This bounding check helps catch the common radius-versus-diameter error."},{"title":"Semicircles and quarter circles","text":"A semicircle's curved edge is half the full circumference, but its entire perimeter also includes one diameter. For radius 7 cm and π = 22/7, the curved edge is 22 cm and the perimeter is 22 + 14 = 36 cm. Its area is half the full area, 77 cm².\n\nA quarter circle's perimeter contains one quarter of the circumference plus two radii. For the same radius, that is 11 + 7 + 7 = 25 cm. Draw a finger along the complete boundary to identify what is included before writing any formula."},{"title":"Composite figures","text":"Imagine a semicircle attached along the 10 cm side of a rectangle. The shared diameter lies inside the combined shape and is not part of its external perimeter. The semicircle's radius is 5 cm because its diameter matches that rectangle side.\n\nFor area, add the rectangle and semicircle areas if they do not overlap except along the joining edge. For perimeter, add only the exposed rectangle edges and the semicircular arc. The same shared line can be essential for finding a radius while still being excluded from the outside boundary."},{"title":"Guided error analysis","text":"A pupil calculates the perimeter of a quarter circle by dividing the full circumference by four. That finds the curved arc only. Ask them to show where they started tracing and whether the path returned to the starting point. The two radii complete the boundary.\n\nAnother pupil uses d² in the area formula. Since d = 2r, this makes the area four times too large. Compare the claimed area with an enclosing square. A geometric size check can reveal an algebraic substitution error without repeating the same calculation."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Cut a paper disc into equal sectors and rearrange them to explore why circle area relates to a base near half the circumference and height near the radius. This is an approximation that improves with narrower sectors, not an exact rectangular dissection using only a few pieces.\n\nBefore independent practice, label radius and diameter, identify the requested measurement, state the π value and trace any composite boundary. Explain every included edge. A correct formula applied to the wrong boundary still gives the wrong answer."}]},{"id":"m-volume","title":"Volume, layers and conserved water","subject":"maths","strand":"Measurement and geometry","skill":"volume","prerequisites":["m-area","m-measure","m-solids"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P5 p.42; P6 p.44","goals":["Build volume from unit-cube layers","Recover unknown dimensions","Use conservation when liquid moves between containers"],"visual":"volume","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Count space in three dimensions","text":"A unit cube has edges one unit long and volume one cubic unit. A cuboid five cubes long and three cubes across contains fifteen cubes in one layer. Four identical layers contain sixty cubes, explaining volume = length × breadth × height.\n\nArea counts two-dimensional coverage; volume counts three-dimensional occupied space. Their units must differ. A cuboid with edges measured in centimetres has volume in cubic centimetres, written cm³. A metre cube is much larger than a centimetre cube; conversion between cm³ and m³ is outside the core focus here."},{"title":"Base area times height","text":"The product of length and breadth is the base area. Therefore volume = base area × perpendicular height. A tank with base area 120 cm² and water depth 8 cm contains 960 cm³ of water. Use water depth, not the full height of the empty space above it.\n\nThe same relationship can recover an unknown: height = volume ÷ base area. If 1,200 cm³ fills a tank with base area 150 cm², the depth is 8 cm. Explain the division as the number of equal one-centimetre layers needed to hold the water."},{"title":"Capacity and liquid volume","text":"One millilitre corresponds to one cubic centimetre, so 1,000 cm³ = 1,000 ml = 1 litre. A cuboid tank measuring 20 cm by 15 cm by 10 cm internally has capacity 3,000 cm³, or 3 litres, when completely filled.\n\nA half-full tank of those dimensions contains 1.5 litres. The tank's capacity has not changed; its current contents have. In real measurement, wall thickness matters when outside dimensions are provided, so use internal dimensions or the assumptions stated by the problem."},{"title":"Worked transfer problem","text":"Tank A contains water to depth 12 cm over a 10 cm by 8 cm base. Its water volume is 960 cm³. All of it is poured into Tank B, whose base is 16 cm by 10 cm. Assuming no loss or overflow, B's depth is 960 ÷ 160 = 6 cm.\n\nThe wider base spreads the same volume into fewer layers. The heights need not match because height is not the conserved quantity. State the conditions: all water transferred, no leakage, no evaporation of consequence and sufficient receiving capacity. These assumptions justify equating the volumes."},{"title":"Recover a cube edge","text":"A cube has volume 125 cm³. Its edge length is the number that multiplies by itself three times to give 125. Since 5 × 5 × 5 = 125, the edge is 5 cm. Dividing the volume by three would not reverse cubing.\n\nFor a cuboid of volume 240 cm³ and height 6 cm, the base area is 40 cm². That does not uniquely determine its length and breadth: 8 by 5 and 10 by 4 both work. Distinguish what the information determines from what remains unknown."},{"title":"Guided investigation","text":"Build two cuboids using the same twelve unit cubes. One may be 1 by 3 by 4 and another 2 by 2 by 3. Their volumes agree even though their shapes and exposed surface areas differ. Draw the layers you counted, including cubes hidden from view.\n\nPour equal measured amounts of room-temperature water into a narrow and a wide container. Predict which water level will be higher before checking. If the containers do not have constant rectangular cross-sections, the simple cuboid formula may not apply; measurement can still establish equal volume."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"When solving a tank problem, label the base, the water depth and the tank's maximum height separately. Convert litres to cubic centimetres when needed before dividing by a base area measured in cm². Check whether the calculated depth fits inside the tank.\n\nBefore practice, explain why doubling a cuboid's height alone doubles its volume, while doubling all three dimensions multiplies volume by eight. The explanation should refer to layers and cubes, connecting the formula back to a physical model."}]},{"id":"m-data","title":"Read data, calculate averages, question claims","subject":"maths","strand":"Statistics","skill":"data","prerequisites":["m-fractions","m-decimals"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"P4 p.40; P6 p.44","goals":["Read tables, graphs and pie charts","Connect average with total","Evaluate what the data can and cannot establish"],"visual":"data","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Begin with labels","text":"A graph does not speak for itself. Read its title, category labels, units, scale and source. A bar reaching a height of four grid spaces might represent four objects, forty objects or another quantity depending on the scale. Check whether the axis begins at zero before comparing apparent differences.\n\nTables preserve exact values and make it easier to compare particular rows. Bar graphs compare categories; line graphs often show change across an ordered variable such as time. A pie chart represents parts of one whole. Choose a representation that matches the question rather than the most decorative option."},{"title":"Interpret a relationship","text":"If a line graph shows a plant's height at weekly intervals, the vertical coordinate gives height and the difference between two coordinates gives growth over that interval. A steep segment suggests a larger change per horizontal interval only when the scales and intervals are comparable.\n\nThe segment between measured points does not prove exactly what happened at every moment. It is a visual connection or model. Do not infer a daily measurement from a graph that only records weekly heights unless interpolation is explicitly intended."},{"title":"Average is equal sharing of a total","text":"For values 6, 8, 10 and 12, the total is 36 and the average is 36 ÷ 4 = 9. Imagine redistributing thirty-six counters into four equal groups. An average need not be one of the original observations.\n\nThe relationship can be reversed: total = average × number of data values. If five bags have an average mass of 240 g, their total mass is 1,200 g. This total is useful when a bag is added or removed; do not simply add a new measurement to the old average."},{"title":"Worked missing-value problem","text":"Four quiz scores have an average of 15. Their total is 60. Three scores are 12, 16 and 14, totalling 42. The missing score is 60 − 42 = 18. Check that all four now sum to 60 and divide by four to recover 15.\n\nIf a fifth score of 20 is added, the new total is 80 and the new average is 80 ÷ 5 = 16. The average rises by one, not by five, because the new total is redistributed across five values. Tracking totals makes the reasoning visible."},{"title":"Combine groups correctly","text":"One group of two pupils averages 10 points; another group of six averages 18 points. Their totals are 20 and 108, giving 128 points across eight pupils. The combined average is 16 points. Averaging 10 and 18 to get 14 would treat the differently sized groups as equal.\n\nAverages can also hide variation. Two sets, 8 and 12 versus 0 and 20, both average ten, yet their values are spread differently. A fair description may need the individual values or their range as well as the average. An average alone cannot describe every feature of a data set."},{"title":"Read a pie chart critically","text":"If one quarter of a pie chart represents 30 pupils, the complete group contains 120 pupils. Half of that circle represents 60 pupils. Use the stated fraction or clear labels; do not estimate a precise numerical fraction from a rough screen picture.\n\nTwo equally sized drawn pie charts may represent populations of different sizes. Half of a group of twenty is fewer people than a quarter of a group of one hundred. Compare the actual totals when the question asks for counts rather than proportions."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Collect a small set of measurements, calculate the total and average, and draw one suitable graph. Ask a partner to describe a claim that the data supports and one that goes beyond it. A survey of friends may describe those friends without representing every child in Singapore.\n\nBefore practice, identify the population or set, read units and scale, and separate a value from a change in value. When using an average, keep the total and count visible. Good data reasoning includes noticing missing information and resisting conclusions the evidence cannot support."}]},{"id":"m-modelling","title":"Turn a word problem into a model","subject":"maths","strand":"Problem solving","skill":"model","prerequisites":["m-ratio","m-algebra","m-remainders"],"source":"maths","sourcePages":"Applications across the primary syllabus","goals":["Represent the unknown and relationships","Choose and compare strategies","Verify a solution against every condition"],"visual":"bars","art":"maths-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Read for relationships","text":"A word problem contains quantities and relationships. First say what is unknown in a complete sentence. Then list what is known, including units. Some information describes a total, some a difference, some a repeated group and some a change. A useful model records those relationships without yet deciding every calculation.\n\nDo not choose operations from single keywords. 'More' may describe a comparison where the smaller amount must be found by subtraction. 'Altogether' may describe a known total from which a missing part is removed. Explain the situation in your own words before using symbols."},{"title":"Sum and difference","text":"Two lengths total 58 cm, and the longer exceeds the shorter by 14 cm. Draw two bars aligned at one end. Removing the excess 14 cm from the total leaves two equal copies of the shorter length: 58 − 14 = 44 cm. Each copy is 22 cm, and the longer is 36 cm.\n\nVerify both conditions: 22 + 36 = 58 and 36 − 22 = 14. Checking only the sum could allow many wrong pairs. Every condition in the original story should be used either to construct the solution or to test it."},{"title":"Work backwards","text":"A number is multiplied by three and then increased by eight to give 41. Reverse the last change first: 41 − 8 = 33. Then undo the multiplication: 33 ÷ 3 = 11. Check forwards with 11 × 3 + 8 = 41.\n\nA bar model and an equation, 3x + 8 = 41, describe the same structure. Choose the representation that helps you see the inverse steps. Working backwards is particularly useful when the final amount is known and each preceding transformation is reversible."},{"title":"Make an organised table","text":"Tickets cost $3 for a child and $5 for an adult. Eight tickets cost $32. Begin with eight child tickets costing $24. Each replacement of a child ticket by an adult ticket adds $2. We need $8 more, so four replacements are needed: four adults and four children.\n\nAn alternative table lists adult counts from zero to eight and the corresponding totals. The total rises by $2 each step because the number of tickets remains fixed. Explain why the list is exhaustive and ordered; random guesses would make it harder to know when to stop."},{"title":"Follow a multi-part dependency","text":"Suppose part (a) finds the water left in a tank and part (b) asks how many bottles it fills. Label the result of part (a) with its meaning and unit. Reuse it as an input to part (b), converting units if needed. A correct early calculation should not become an unexplained number later.\n\nIf a later result is impossible, trace the dependency backwards. Did you use full tank capacity instead of remaining water? Did you count partial bottles as complete ones? A structured model helps locate which assumption or step needs repair."},{"title":"A useful failed attempt","text":"A model can fail productively. If you assume all eight tickets are adult tickets, the cost is $40, too high by $8. Replacing an adult by a child reduces the total by $2, again giving four replacements. Two different starting assumptions converge on the same result.\n\nKeep a short note about why your first approach stalled. Perhaps a ratio unit was not fixed, a difference was mistaken for a total, or a case list was incomplete. Such notes help you select a representation next time without assigning yourself a permanent 'learning style'."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Write a solution with four parts: model, calculation, interpretation and check. Ask someone to cover your calculations and judge whether the model alone reflects the story. Then let them examine the arithmetic separately. This separates a conceptual error from a computational slip.\n\nFor transfer, change the context while preserving the relationship: ticket prices can become two kinds of coins or packages. Then change one relationship while preserving the context. Recognising both sameness and difference is more powerful than memorising named question types."}]},{"id":"m-proof","title":"Investigations: explain why a claim holds","subject":"maths","strand":"Reasoning enrichment","skill":"inquiry","prerequisites":["m-factors","m-modelling"],"source":"faq","sourcePages":"Enrichment informed by NUS High FAQ Q11; not an official test specification","goals":["Use counterexamples","Count without omissions or duplicates","Build a general argument from a small case"],"visual":"counting","art":"maths-workbench","extension":true,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Examples suggest; reasons establish","text":"The sums 1 + 3 = 4, 3 + 5 = 8 and 7 + 9 = 16 suggest that two odd numbers sum to an even number. Examples help us notice the pattern, but a finite list alone does not establish it for every pair of odd numbers.\n\nRepresent an odd number as pairs of counters plus one unpaired counter. Combining two odd collections brings together all the pairs and the two unpaired counters, which form one additional pair. Nothing remains unpaired, so the sum is even. This explains the entire family at once."},{"title":"One counterexample can defeat always","text":"Consider the claim 'Every number ending in 3 is prime'. Thirteen and twenty-three support it, but 33 = 3 × 11 defeats it. One valid counterexample is enough because the word every includes 33. Giving more supporting examples cannot repair the original universal claim.\n\nA counterexample must satisfy the claim's starting conditions while failing its conclusion. The number 35 is composite, but it does not end in 3 and therefore is not a counterexample to this particular statement. State which condition your example meets and which conclusion it contradicts."},{"title":"Count systematically","text":"How many outfits can be made from three shirts and two pairs of trousers? Draw a table with shirts as rows and trousers as columns. Each of its six cells represents a different pairing. The table explains both completeness and uniqueness: every shirt has both options, and no cell repeats a pair.\n\nIf additional restrictions are given, cross out the forbidden cells rather than still using the unrestricted product. Counting methods must reflect the actual rules. Distinguish ordered arrangements from unordered selections; red-then-blue may differ from blue-then-red in a sequence but not in a bag of two counters."},{"title":"Look for something that cannot change","text":"An invariant is a property preserved by an allowed operation. If you add two to a whole number repeatedly, its odd-or-even status never changes. Therefore starting from an odd number, you cannot reach an even number using only that operation.\n\nThis is stronger than trying many unsuccessful sequences. It explains why no permitted sequence can succeed. In a new puzzle, inspect totals, parity, colour balance or remainder after division. The useful invariant depends on the operation; do not assume the same one fits every problem."},{"title":"A worst-case guarantee","text":"A bag contains red and blue counters, with at least three of each. Draw without looking. Two draws might give one of each colour, so two do not guarantee a matching pair. With three draws, at least two must share a colour because there are only two colour categories.\n\nThe argument concerns a guarantee, not the most likely outcome. Three may be needed even though a matching pair often occurs in two. To justify a minimum, give a possible arrangement showing that one fewer can fail, then explain why the proposed number must succeed."},{"title":"Optimise with a complete comparison","text":"Rectangles with whole-number side lengths and area 24 have side pairs 1 by 24, 2 by 12, 3 by 8 and 4 by 6. Their perimeters are 50, 28, 22 and 20. Thus 4 by 6 has the smallest perimeter among these permitted rectangles.\n\nThe restriction to whole-number sides matters. Without it, more rectangles are possible and this case list is no longer complete. A valid optimum must be best among all allowed choices, not merely the few examples tried. Explain why your search covers the permitted set."},{"title":"Explain, challenge and read further","text":"Present an investigation with a conjecture, a strategy, evidence and a conclusion. Invite someone to challenge it. If their example breaks your claim, revise the claim precisely rather than treating the challenge as failure. Mathematical creativity and careful criticism work together.\n\nFor wider reading, choose a reputable mathematics resource linked from the course and summarise one unfamiliar idea in your own words. Test a small case and identify what remains unproved. These enrichment tasks practise reasoning; they are not copied NUS High questions or a calibrated admissions score."}]},{"id":"s-inquiry","title":"Think and work like a scientist","subject":"science","strand":"Working scientifically","skill":"inquiry","prerequisites":[],"source":"science","sourcePages":"20–32: inquiry practices","goals":["Separate observations from explanations","Design a fair comparison","Use evidence with appropriate limits"],"visual":"fair-test","art":"inquiry-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Start with a question","text":"Science is a way of building and checking explanations about the natural world. A useful investigation question says what will change and what will be observed. “Do plants like music?” is unclear. “Do seedlings exposed to a particular sound grow taller than similar seedlings kept without that sound?” is more testable, although a fair investigation would still need careful planning.\n\nAn observation describes what you noticed or measured. An inference is an interpretation of those observations. “The leaf has brown edges” is an observation. “The plant lacked water” is one possible inference. Heat, disease or damaged roots might also explain the observation. Keep possible explanations open until evidence distinguishes them."},{"title":"Change, measure, keep the same","text":"For a fair test, change the factor you are investigating, measure an outcome, and keep other factors that could affect that outcome the same. To investigate how exposed water surface area affects evaporation, use the same starting volume, water temperature, location and duration. Change the exposed surface area and measure the water remaining after a fixed time.\n\nThe changed factor is not always what you measure. If you change the number of batteries, bulb brightness is an outcome. If two important conditions change together, an observed difference cannot be confidently attributed to either one alone. State specific controls instead of writing only “make it a fair test”."},{"title":"Measure meaningfully","text":"Choose a quantity that matches your question. Plant height, leaf number and dry mass describe different features of growth. “Healthier” is too vague unless you define what will count as evidence. Record units, starting values and the time of each observation.\n\nRepeat measurements or use several similar specimens to see whether a pattern is consistent. Repetition helps reveal variation; it does not repair an unfair design. Three repetitions of a test that changes both light and water still cannot isolate the effect of light. Keep unusual results visible and investigate them instead of deleting them merely because they do not fit your prediction."},{"title":"Worked example: seedlings","text":"Imagine three seedlings in each of two groups. Both groups have the same type of plant, similar starting sizes, equal soil and water, and the same duration. One receives more light. Record each plant’s starting and final height, then compare changes rather than just final heights.\n\nEven this design has limits: a lamp may also warm one group. Check temperature or choose a setup that reduces that difference. A taller seedling is not automatically healthier; a seedling in dim light can become long and pale. A careful conclusion identifies what was measured and avoids claiming more than the evidence shows."},{"title":"Turn results into an explanation","text":"Suppose equal volumes of water in two containers fall by 8 ml and 3 ml. Describe the pattern, identify which condition belongs to each result, then connect the pattern to a relevant scientific idea. “Under these conditions, more water evaporated from the container with the larger exposed surface area” is stronger than “my prediction was right”.\n\nTables preserve exact values. Graphs help reveal relationships. Put the changed numerical factor on the horizontal axis when appropriate, label quantities and units, and select a sensible scale. Do not invent values between categories such as wood and metal as though they formed a numerical sequence."},{"title":"Try a safe investigation","text":"Fold two equal sheets of paper into different bridge shapes. Keep the distance between supports the same. Add identical light counters one at a time and record the number supported before the bridge bends to a chosen limit. Keep fingers clear of falling objects and use only a low setup.\n\nBefore testing, write your prediction and a reason. Afterward, record whether the result supports it, what could have affected the comparison, and one improvement. A prediction that turns out to be wrong can still lead to excellent science if the evidence changes your explanation."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Use the sequence question, prediction, method, observations, explanation, limitation and next test. It is a thinking routine, not a sentence template that replaces understanding. Your explanation must match the actual investigation.\n\nFor unfamiliar problems, underline what the evidence establishes and circle what it leaves uncertain. Ask what observation would distinguish two competing explanations. This habit is useful in school questions, science reading and everyday claims about products or health."}]},{"id":"s-diversity","title":"Living things and classification","subject":"science","strand":"Diversity","skill":"diversity","prerequisites":["s-inquiry"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"38: diversity of living and non-living things","goals":["Identify living things using several characteristics","Classify using observable similarities","Explain the limits of a grouping rule"],"visual":"","art":"living-world","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"What makes something alive?","text":"Living things need resources, grow and develop, respond to their surroundings, and can reproduce as part of their life cycle. No single quick observation is sufficient in every case. A car moves and uses fuel but is not alive. A seed may appear inactive while still being alive.\n\nAsk for a combination of characteristics. A particular animal may be unable to reproduce, but it remains a living thing. Dead leaves came from something living; a stone did not. These distinctions prevent a simple “moves or does not move” rule from producing misleading classifications."},{"title":"Group to make sense of diversity","text":"Classification groups things by shared characteristics. We can sort objects by material, shape or use, but we must state the rule and apply it consistently. Scientific classification uses meaningful similarities among organisms, rather than personal preferences such as “nice animals”.\n\nAt primary level, recognise broad groups including plants, animals, fungi and bacteria. Flowering and non-flowering plants are plant groups. Fungi are not plants: they do not make food by photosynthesis. Bacteria are living organisms too small to see individually with the unaided eye. Do not grow unknown microbes at home to investigate them."},{"title":"Compare plant groups","text":"Flowering plants produce flowers as part of reproduction, although flowers may be small or appear only during some seasons. Many produce fruits containing seeds. Non-flowering plants do not produce flowers; ferns reproduce by spores, while some other non-flowering plants produce seeds.\n\nTherefore “all non-flowering plants reproduce by spores” is too broad. A single photograph with no visible flower also does not prove that a plant is non-flowering. Reliable classification may require observations across its life cycle or a trustworthy reference about that organism."},{"title":"Recognise animal groups","text":"Useful primary animal groups include mammals, birds, fish, reptiles, amphibians and insects. Mammals have hair and females feed their young with milk. Birds have feathers. Fish typically have fins and gills. Insects have six legs as adults and three main body parts.\n\nUse several relevant characteristics when comparing reptiles and amphibians: their skin, reproduction and life cycles differ. Do not rely only on habitat. Whales live in water but are mammals; penguins are birds even though they do not fly. Exceptions help us improve an over-simple rule rather than abandon careful observation."},{"title":"Worked example: an identification key","text":"Imagine three animals: a butterfly, a bird and a cat. Start with a feature that separates them: “Does it have feathers?” separates the bird. For the remaining two, “Does the adult have six legs?” identifies the butterfly. The cat follows the other branch.\n\nEvery organism in the set must reach one unambiguous endpoint. A key is a tool for a defined collection; it is not proof that the chosen feature classifies every animal on Earth. If you add a spider, the key needs another distinguishing question because a spider is neither an insect nor a mammal."},{"title":"Observe without harming","text":"Study the living-world illustration as a scene to explore, not a complete species guide. List visible organisms, their observable features and one fact that would require a reference. You cannot reliably infer every animal’s diet from a picture.\n\nOn a walk, photograph leaves, bark and insects without touching unfamiliar organisms. Build a two-choice key for a small set of leaves using features such as edge shape and arrangement. Check the key with someone who has not seen your notes. If two leaves reach the same endpoint, refine your questions."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"A good classification answer names the relevant feature and links it to the group. “It is a bird because it has feathers” is more informative than “it looks like a bird”. Do not claim that all flying animals are birds or all water animals are fish.\n\nWhen a new example breaks your rule, ask whether the rule was necessary, sufficient or merely common. In everyday language: must every member have that feature, and does having that feature guarantee membership? This is the same careful reasoning used in mathematical definitions."}]},{"id":"s-materials","title":"Materials: choose properties for a purpose","subject":"science","strand":"Diversity","skill":"materials","prerequisites":["s-inquiry"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"39–40: properties and uses of materials","goals":["Distinguish objects from materials","Compare useful material properties","Justify a design using evidence"],"visual":"","art":"science-bench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Object and material","text":"An object is something made or used; a material is what it is made from. A spoon might be made of metal, wood or plastic. One material can have many uses, and one object can contain several materials. A raincoat has fabric, fasteners and possibly a waterproof coating.\n\nWhen comparing materials, name the property needed for the job. Appearance alone rarely explains suitability. A transparent window must allow enough light through; a safe handle should provide a comfortable grip and suit the temperatures involved."},{"title":"Strength and flexibility","text":"Strength concerns how well a material withstands a load or force without breaking under a specified test. Flexibility concerns how readily it bends. They are different: something flexible is not necessarily weak, and something stiff is not necessarily strong against every kind of force.\n\nTest equal-sized samples using the same arrangement when you want to compare materials. A thick strip and a thin strip may behave differently because of their dimensions, even if their materials are identical. Describe the test: resistance to pulling, bending or impact can lead to different results."},{"title":"Water and light","text":"A waterproof material prevents water passing through under the tested conditions. An absorbent material takes water in. A towel is useful because it absorbs water; a raincoat’s outer layer should resist water passing through. These are different design goals, not a ranking from good to bad.\n\nMaterials also differ in how much light passes through. Clear glass allows much light through; some frosted materials allow light through but prevent a clear view; cardboard blocks it. The words transparent, translucent and opaque are useful vocabulary, but the important scientific idea is the observed passage of light."},{"title":"Floating is an observation","text":"Some objects float in water and others sink. Test safely with clean water and small non-sharp objects. Record both the material and the object’s form. A hollow metal boat and a solid metal piece can behave differently; “all metal sinks” is therefore not a reliable rule for every object.\n\nA detailed explanation using average density is extension work, not a required calculation in this primary unit. Here, describe observations accurately and avoid turning a result from one sample into a claim about every possible shape or size made from that material."},{"title":"Worked design: a lunch carrier","text":"Suppose a carrier must withstand light rain, hold a lunch box and bend enough to fold away. Consider waterproofness, strength and flexibility. Test equal-sized samples, then explain a choice using the observations. Colour may matter to the user, but it does not establish waterproofness.\n\nThere may be a trade-off: a very strong rigid container may be less convenient to fold. Combining materials can meet different needs, such as a strong outer fabric with a waterproof lining. A design answer should connect each required function to a supporting property."},{"title":"Try and record","text":"With an adult, place equal-sized samples of paper towel, plastic sheet and cloth over separate cups. Add the same small amount of room-temperature water and observe after the same time. Protect the table and keep water away from electrical items.\n\nWrite what happened before explaining it. Water may soak into a sample without immediately dripping through, so record both absorption and leakage. This distinction improves your conclusion. Repeat with fresh samples if an earlier sample is already wet."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Use the chain requirement, property, evidence and decision. “Choose this material because it is good” skips the reasoning. “The outer layer must keep rain out; this sample prevented water passing through in our test” makes the connection visible.\n\nAn investigation supports a conclusion under tested conditions. Very high pressure or a long exposure may produce a different result. This is not a reason to avoid conclusions; it is a reason to state their limits clearly."}]},{"id":"s-life-cycles","title":"Life cycles: change and continuity","subject":"science","strand":"Cycles","skill":"life_cycles","prerequisites":["s-diversity"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"42: life cycles of plants and animals","goals":["Compare three-stage and four-stage life cycles","Describe growth and reproduction","Use repeated observations to infer stages"],"visual":"life-cycle","art":"plant-study","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A cycle links generations","text":"An individual organism begins life, grows and develops, and eventually dies. A life cycle describes the sequence of stages through which a kind of organism passes, including reproduction that begins another generation. The individual does not return to being an egg; the next generation begins the cycle again.\n\nA cycle diagram is a model. Its arrows show sequence, not equal time intervals. Different stages may last very different lengths of time. Label the stages and explain what the arrows mean before interpreting a diagram."},{"title":"Three-stage insect life cycles","text":"Some insects, such as grasshoppers, have egg, nymph and adult stages. The nymph resembles the adult in several ways but is smaller and does not yet have the adult’s fully developed wings and reproductive structures. It grows through changes that include shedding its outer covering.\n\nA nymph is not a miniature adult in every respect. Observe what remains similar and what changes. The three-stage description is useful for these insects, but it does not describe the life cycle of every animal with three stages in a simplified drawing."},{"title":"Four-stage insect life cycles","text":"Butterflies have egg, larva, pupa and adult stages. A caterpillar is the larva of a butterfly or moth. The larva feeds and grows; major changes occur during the pupal stage; the adult can reproduce. The pupa is living even though it may show little obvious movement.\n\nThe stages can differ greatly in appearance, food and habitat. This helps explain why the young and adult of one species may use different resources. Do not call every young insect a caterpillar: different insects have different larval forms and life cycles."},{"title":"Plants across a generation","text":"A flowering plant can grow from a seed into a seedling and then a mature plant. Under suitable conditions, it produces flowers, followed by fruits and seeds after successful reproduction. Seeds may then germinate and start another generation.\n\nGermination and reproduction are different processes. A seed has already formed through reproduction before it germinates. A seedling uses stored food initially; as green leaves develop, photosynthesis becomes important. Light requirements for germination vary, so do not assume every kind of seed must be exposed to light to begin germinating."},{"title":"Worked comparison","text":"Compare a grasshopper and a butterfly. Both start as eggs and eventually become adults that reproduce. A grasshopper has a nymph stage; a butterfly has larval and pupal stages. The absence or presence of a pupal stage is a useful distinguishing feature.\n\nNow compare a frog’s egg, tadpole and adult in a simplified cycle. A tadpole changes substantially as it develops. The fact that this simplified diagram has three stages does not make the frog an insect. Stage count and animal classification answer different questions."},{"title":"Observe a plant safely","text":"Place a few suitable seeds on moist paper in a ventilated container, following an adult’s guidance. Keep them moist without submerging them. Record dates, root appearance and shoot growth. Handle soil hygienically and wash your hands. Do not eat experimental seeds or grow mouldy samples.\n\nDraw observations at intervals with labels and a scale where possible. If one seed does not germinate, consider whether it was viable and whether conditions were suitable. One failure does not establish that the species never germinates in those conditions."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"When ordering life-cycle pictures, use evidence about structures and development rather than size alone. An adult of one species can be smaller than the young of another. Explain how reproduction connects the adult stage to a new generation.\n\nFor an unfamiliar organism, ask what observations would establish its stages. Long-term observation, photographs at known times and reliable reference sources can be combined. A complete cycle is a claim supported by evidence, not just a circle of attractive pictures."}]},{"id":"s-magnets","title":"Magnets and magnetic interactions","subject":"science","strand":"Interactions","skill":"magnets","prerequisites":["s-materials"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"62: magnets","goals":["Predict interactions between magnetic poles","Distinguish magnets from magnetic materials","Plan a fair comparison of magnetic effects"],"visual":"","art":"science-bench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Attraction without touching","text":"A magnet can exert a force on certain materials without touching them. Iron and many kinds of steel are familiar magnetic materials. Not every metal is attracted strongly to a classroom magnet: aluminium and copper, for example, do not behave like iron in this test.\n\nMagnetic force is an interaction. A nearby magnet may pull an iron object toward it, even across a small gap. Whether the object actually moves also depends on other forces and on how it is supported. Observe the effect rather than assuming that no movement means no force."},{"title":"Two poles","text":"A bar magnet has a north pole and a south pole. Like poles repel; unlike poles attract. Repulsion pushes the magnets apart, while attraction pulls them toward each other. The effects are usually strongest near a bar magnet’s poles.\n\nThe labels north and south do not mean positive and negative electrical charges. Keep magnetic poles and battery terminals as separate ideas. A magnet freely suspended so that it can turn tends to align roughly north–south; a compass uses this behaviour for finding direction."},{"title":"Magnet or magnetic material?","text":"An iron object may be attracted by either pole of a magnet. Therefore attraction alone does not prove that the object is itself a permanent magnet. Repulsion between an unknown object and a known magnet is stronger evidence of magnetic poles in the unknown object.\n\nFor example, if one end of an unknown bar repels a known north pole, that end behaves as a north pole. The other end of a simple bar magnet is south. Explain the observation using like-pole repulsion, not a claim that all metal has north and south ends."},{"title":"Making a magnet","text":"A suitable iron or steel object can be magnetised by a stroking method using a magnet. In a classroom demonstration, strokes follow a consistent direction and procedure rather than random back-and-forth rubbing. Magnetic effects can also be produced using an electric current in a coil around a suitable core.\n\nAn electromagnet can be controlled by switching its current on and off. Any practical coil activity should use an adult-approved low-voltage kit and short supervised trials: wires and batteries can heat. Never connect wires directly across a battery or use mains electricity."},{"title":"Worked investigation","text":"To compare two bar magnets by how many identical paper clips they support, keep the clip type, contact location and method the same. Repeat the test. The result compares performance in that test; it is not a complete measurement of every possible magnetic effect.\n\nTo investigate distance, use the same magnet, object, surface and orientation while changing the gap. Do not change both magnet and gap and then attribute every difference to distance. A force can act through some non-magnetic materials, so a thin sheet between objects does not necessarily stop the effect."},{"title":"Explore safely","text":"Use a large classroom magnet with adult supervision and a tray of suitable objects. Predict and test which are attracted, recording the material where known. Keep magnets away from electronics, medical devices and loose small objects that could be swallowed. Do not use small powerful magnets.\n\nSeparate observations from assumptions. “This object is attracted” is an observation. “It contains iron” is a possible explanation that may require product information. Colour or shininess alone cannot tell you whether a material is magnetic."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For every pole problem, label the known pole, state attraction or repulsion, and apply the correct rule. For an unknown object, ask whether the evidence distinguishes a magnet from a magnetic material.\n\nUseful applications connect a property to a purpose: a compass can turn to indicate direction; an electromagnet can release an attracted object when switched off. Learning the causal connection helps you reason about unfamiliar devices without memorising a list of uses."}]},{"id":"s-matter","title":"Matter: mass, space and states","subject":"science","strand":"Cycles","skill":"matter","prerequisites":["s-materials"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"48: matter","goals":["Describe matter by mass and occupied space","Compare solids liquids and gases","Use observations to reason about invisible air"],"visual":"states","art":"water-landscape","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Matter has mass and occupies space","text":"Matter includes air, water, rocks and living things. It has mass and occupies space. An object can be invisible and still be matter: clean air is a familiar example. Light and sound are not substances that fill a container in the same way; they are associated with energy transfer.\n\nMass describes the amount of matter in the primary model and is measured with a balance, commonly in grams or kilograms. Volume describes the space occupied, commonly in cubic centimetres or millilitres for small quantities. Mass and volume are different measurements, so never substitute one unit for the other."},{"title":"Solids","text":"A solid has a definite shape and a definite volume under ordinary conditions. A wooden block keeps its shape when placed in different containers. A flexible solid can bend under a force and still be a solid: flexibility does not make rubber a liquid.\n\nA pile of sand can change its overall outline when poured, but individual grains are solids. Classify the material at the appropriate scale. If a solid is broken into pieces, each piece occupies space; breaking it does not automatically change its state of matter."},{"title":"Liquids","text":"A liquid has a definite volume but takes the shape of the part of its container that it occupies. Pour the same water into a tall narrow vessel and a short wide vessel: the water level changes, but the amount need not change if none is spilled or lost.\n\nRead a measuring container at eye level using its scale correctly. A taller column does not by itself prove a larger volume. Geometry and measurement provide better evidence than appearance alone. Water can flow, but the ability to flow is not unique to liquids because gases also flow."},{"title":"Gases","text":"A gas has neither a definite shape nor a definite volume: it spreads to fill the available container. Air in a balloon occupies space, and adding more air changes the amount of matter inside. Gas can escape through openings, so an apparently empty container may actually contain air.\n\nA gas can be compressed much more readily than a liquid in simple classroom comparisons. Detailed particle arrangements are a useful later explanation, but are not required to describe the primary observations in this lesson. First make clear what the experiment actually shows."},{"title":"Worked example: trapped air","text":"Push an inverted clear cup with dry tissue at its inner base straight down into water. In a careful demonstration, the trapped air prevents water from filling the entire cup, keeping the tissue dry. Tilt the cup and air bubbles can escape, allowing water farther in.\n\nThe explanation is that air occupies space, not that the cup contains “nothing”. A real test may fail if the cup tilts or the tissue hangs into water. Recognising such details helps distinguish an invalid idea from an imperfect setup."},{"title":"Measure with care","text":"Compare equal water volumes in different unbreakable containers using a measuring jug. Record the volume before and after pouring, allowing for spills and drops left behind. Predict first whether changing the container changes the volume.\n\nFor an air demonstration, use only an adult-approved plastic syringe without a needle, and never point it at anyone. Do not heat or seal containers to investigate gas. The thinking goal is to relate resistance to compression and occupied space, not to create high pressure."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Ask three separate questions: Does it have mass? Does it occupy space? Does its shape or volume depend on its container? These help organise evidence about matter and states.\n\nDo not confuse a change of shape with a change of state. Bending wire, crushing a can and breaking ice are changes to objects; melting ice is a change from the solid state to the liquid state. The next lessons explain how heat is involved in such changes."}]},{"id":"s-heat","title":"Heat transfer and temperature","subject":"science","strand":"Energy","skill":"heat","prerequisites":["s-matter"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"76–77: heat","goals":["Distinguish heat from temperature","Predict the direction of heat transfer","Explain conductors insulators and temperature change"],"visual":"cooling","art":"water-landscape","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Temperature is a measurement","text":"Temperature tells us how hot or cold something is and is measured with a thermometer. Degrees Celsius are used in this course. Touch is not a reliable measuring instrument: two objects at the same temperature can feel different because they transfer heat at different rates.\n\nHeat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. Do not use “heat” and “temperature” as interchangeable quantities. A large container and a small container of water can have the same temperature without containing the same total amount of thermal energy. No numerical heat-energy formula is needed in this primary lesson."},{"title":"Direction of transfer","text":"Heat flows from a region at higher temperature to a region at lower temperature. A warm drink loses heat to cooler surroundings and its temperature generally falls. A cold drink gains heat from warmer surroundings and its temperature generally rises, provided no change of state or other complication dominates.\n\nThe cooler object does not send a substance called cold into the warmer one. Explain the direction using heat transfer. When two objects reach the same temperature, there is no net heat transfer between them due to a temperature difference."},{"title":"Conductors and insulators","text":"Good conductors allow heat to pass through readily. Metals are generally good heat conductors compared with wood or plastic in common classroom examples. Poor conductors slow heat transfer and are often called heat insulators.\n\nAn insulator does not create heat or stop transfer perfectly. A jacket reduces heat loss from a warm body; an insulated bottle can slow warming of a cold drink as well as cooling of a hot drink. Explain the actual direction of heat transfer in each situation rather than memorising that insulation “keeps things hot”."},{"title":"Expansion and contraction","text":"Many materials expand when heated and contract when cooled. Expansion means an increase in dimensions; it does not mean new matter has been added. Everyday designs may allow space for expansion when temperature changes.\n\nThese are general patterns, not a claim that every substance behaves identically at every temperature. In particular, water has special behaviour around freezing. For a primary question, use the stated material and conditions and avoid inventing precise expansion amounts without measurements."},{"title":"Worked example: two spoons","text":"Two spoons of the same shape, one metal and one plastic, stand in the same warm water. Compare temperatures at corresponding points on their handles after equal times. The metal handle generally warms more quickly because metal conducts heat more readily.\n\nControl dimensions, immersed length, starting temperature and water conditions for a fair comparison. Feeling the handle can suggest a difference, but a suitable temperature measurement gives stronger evidence. Do not use boiling water or touch very hot objects for this activity."},{"title":"Read a cooling record","text":"Imagine a drink measured at 60°C, 48°C, 40°C and 35°C at equal time intervals in a 25°C room. The temperature falls, and the successive decreases become smaller. The evidence supports cooling toward room temperature under those conditions.\n\nIt does not show that the drink will fall below room temperature by that same heat-transfer process. Nor should you extend a straight line indefinitely below zero. Use the physical explanation as a check on graph extrapolation. Different containers and air movement can change the rate."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For a heat question, identify the warmer and cooler objects, state the direction of transfer, identify whether the material speeds or slows it, then connect that to the observed temperature or state change.\n\nIn your notebook, compare an ice box and a winter coat. Both use insulation, but heat enters the cold box and leaves the warmer body. One general principle explains both. This is the kind of transfer that shows understanding beyond memorised examples."}]},{"id":"s-water","title":"Water changes state and cycles","subject":"science","strand":"Cycles","skill":"water","prerequisites":["s-matter","s-heat"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"49: water and changes of state","goals":["Explain melting freezing evaporation boiling and condensation","Connect state changes to heat gain or loss","Apply the water cycle to unfamiliar situations"],"visual":"water-cycle","art":"water-landscape","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"One substance, different states","text":"Ice is water in the solid state. Liquid water is water in the liquid state. Water vapour is water in the gaseous state. Melting changes ice into liquid water when it gains heat; the substance is still water. This is a change of state, not the production of a different substance.\n\nDuring the melting of pure ice under normal conditions, heat can be gained while the temperature stays about 0°C until melting is complete. Heat gain does not always mean an immediate temperature rise.\n\nFreezing changes liquid water into ice when it loses heat. Under normal atmospheric conditions, pure water freezes and ice melts at about 0°C. Impurities and pressure can affect these temperatures, so keep the usual classroom conditions in mind."},{"title":"Evaporation and boiling","text":"Evaporation is the change from liquid water into water vapour at the surface. It can occur below the boiling point. Boiling occurs throughout the liquid, with bubbles of water vapour forming. Pure water boils at about 100°C under normal atmospheric pressure.\n\nVisible white mist above hot water consists mainly of tiny liquid droplets, not invisible water vapour itself. Do not describe every visible cloud as a gas. The distinction matters when explaining condensation on a mirror or the formation of clouds."},{"title":"What affects evaporation?","text":"For comparable conditions, a higher water temperature, a larger exposed surface area and greater air movement can increase the rate of evaporation. A spread-out wet cloth commonly dries faster than the same cloth folded up because more wet surface is exposed.\n\nHumidity also affects evaporation, but treat it as useful context rather than an additional memorised primary formula. In an investigation, keep other conditions the same. If a sunny location is also windier, the result does not isolate temperature alone."},{"title":"Condensation","text":"Condensation changes water vapour into liquid water when it loses heat. Water vapour in surrounding air can lose heat near a cold glass, forming droplets on the outside. Those droplets need not have leaked through the glass.\n\nA lid held above warm water can also collect droplets as water vapour cools. Never try this over boiling water without an appropriate adult-led demonstration. Explain where the water vapour came from and what it transferred heat to, rather than writing only “cold makes water”."},{"title":"Worked explanation: a cold bottle","text":"A sealed dry bottle containing cold water is placed in a warm room. Droplets appear outside. The cold surface cools nearby water vapour sufficiently for condensation. The external liquid can therefore originate from water vapour in the room air.\n\nA useful comparison is a similar sealed bottle at room temperature. Keep location and observation time the same. The comparison helps investigate the effect of surface temperature. It still does not measure the amount of water vapour directly, so avoid claiming more than the observations show."},{"title":"The water cycle","text":"Energy from the Sun drives evaporation from water surfaces and other sources. Water vapour can cool and condense into tiny droplets that form clouds. Water returns to the ground as precipitation such as rain; some collects in water bodies, flows over land or enters the ground.\n\nThe landscape illustration is a context picture, not a measured map. Trace possible routes and name the processes separately. Water does not have to visit every part of the drawing in one fixed order. Conserving water and protecting sources from pollution matter even though water cycles through the environment."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For every state change, name the starting state, ending state and heat gain or loss. “Water disappears” fails to explain evaporation; “ice melts into liquid water as it gains heat” identifies the process clearly.\n\nTry connecting three everyday cases: drying laundry, a fogged mirror and ice in a drink. They involve evaporation, condensation and melting respectively. Compare their heat transfers and identify the source of the water in each case before attempting independent questions."}]},{"id":"s-light","title":"Light, sight and shadows","subject":"science","strand":"Energy","skill":"light","prerequisites":["s-materials","s-inquiry"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"76: light","goals":["Explain seeing through reflected light","Predict shadows using straight-line paths","Design a fair test of shadow size"],"visual":"shadow","art":"inquiry-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Light helps us see","text":"We see an object when light from it enters our eyes. A lamp and the Sun produce light. A book usually does not; we see it because light reflects from its surface into our eyes. In a completely dark room, merely opening our eyes does not make the book visible.\n\nDistinguish a light source from an object that reflects light. The Moon reflects sunlight; it is not producing the visible light by the same process as the Sun. Never look directly at the Sun or shine bright light into anyone’s eyes."},{"title":"Straight paths","text":"In a uniform medium, light travels in straight lines. A simple classroom model represents paths with straight lines and arrowheads indicating the direction of travel. A drawn line is a representation, not a visible string coming out of the source.\n\nWhen a source, an opening and an observer are aligned, light can pass through the opening to the observer. Move an opaque screen into the path and the light is blocked. This straight-line model helps explain shadows and why we cannot see through a cardboard wall."},{"title":"Materials and transmission","text":"Some materials allow much light through; some allow some light through but prevent a clear image; others block most light. The same material may behave differently at different thicknesses. Describe the observation under stated conditions rather than assuming all plastic behaves identically.\n\nReflection and transmission are different. A surface can reflect some light and transmit some light. For primary questions, focus on the property relevant to the setup: whether light reaches the screen or the eye, and whether an opaque object interrupts the path."},{"title":"Forming a shadow","text":"A shadow forms where an object blocks light from reaching a surface. Identify the light source, object and screen. Without a receiving surface, a familiar shadow patch may not be visible even though light is blocked in a region.\n\nFor a small source and fixed source-to-screen distance, moving the object nearer the source generally makes its shadow larger. Moving it nearer the screen generally makes it smaller. This statement requires the stated geometry; do not apply it automatically when several distances change together."},{"title":"Worked explanation","text":"Imagine a torch, a card and a wall along a line. Light passing around the edges of the card continues toward the wall. The card blocks paths through its area, leaving a darker region. Sketch two limiting straight paths past opposite card edges to reason about the shadow size.\n\nUse arrowheads away from the source, and place labels outside the paths. A decorative illustration is not accurate evidence of angles or distances. For a geometry-based answer, use the interactive model or your measured setup and state which distances stayed fixed."},{"title":"Investigate safely","text":"Use a small torch, a plain card and a wall in a safely dimmed room. Keep the torch and wall fixed. Move the card to measured positions and record shadow width. Keep card size and orientation the same. Avoid lasers, hot lamps and complete darkness that creates a tripping risk.\n\nPredict before measuring. Repeat readings at the same positions and compare results. If the torch has a broad source, the shadow edges may be blurred; record that limitation rather than pretending every boundary is perfectly sharp."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For sight, trace source to object to eye. For a shadow, trace source toward screen and identify which paths the object blocks. These two sequences answer different questions and should not be mixed.\n\nExplain why a white wall is visible without producing its own light, then explain why a shadow moves when the source moves. A strong answer connects the light path to the observation instead of using “because light” as a complete explanation."}]},{"id":"s-plant-parts","title":"Plant parts work together","subject":"science","strand":"Systems","skill":"plants","prerequisites":["s-diversity","s-life-cycles"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"56: plant parts and functions","goals":["Connect roots stems and leaves to their functions","Distinguish food-making from mineral uptake","Explain a plant as an interacting system"],"visual":"","art":"plant-study","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A plant is a system","text":"A flowering plant has parts that work together. Roots, stems, leaves, flowers and fruits have different functions, although their shapes vary greatly among species. A useful labelled drawing shows relationships, not just a list of names.\n\nStart with the question “What does this part help the plant do?” Then ask how it depends on other parts. Leaves need water supplied from the roots; roots need food made in green parts. Damage to one part can therefore affect the whole plant."},{"title":"Roots","text":"Roots anchor the plant and absorb water and mineral salts from the surroundings. The water and dissolved mineral salts are transported to other parts. Some roots also store food. A root’s position underground is common but is not the definition of a root: some roots grow above ground.\n\nMineral salts are important nutrients, but they are not the plant’s food in the primary photosynthesis explanation. Green plants make food using light energy, water and carbon dioxide. Saying that roots “eat soil” hides this important distinction."},{"title":"Stems","text":"Stems support leaves, flowers and fruits. They also contain transport pathways connecting the roots and other parts. Holding leaves in suitable positions can help them receive light. Some stems store food or have special forms.\n\nA plant can have a soft stem or a woody stem. Size and stiffness vary, but the support and transport functions remain useful ideas. Do not identify every underground structure as a root merely because of its location; use a reference when identifying unfamiliar storage organs."},{"title":"Leaves","text":"Green leaves are important sites of photosynthesis, the process through which plants use light energy to make food. Leaves also exchange gases with their surroundings. They are parts of a living system, not solar panels that directly store sunlight as a substance.\n\nLeaf size, arrangement and structure affect how plants interact with their environment. At this stage, identify the function before learning detailed microscopic structures. The later photosynthesis lesson explains inputs, products and the difference between making food and releasing energy from food."},{"title":"Flowers, fruits and seeds","text":"Flowers are involved in sexual reproduction in flowering plants. After successful fertilisation, seeds develop, and the flower’s ovary develops into a fruit in the usual primary model. Fruits protect seeds and can help their dispersal.\n\nFlowers do not all have large colourful petals, and a mature plant may not be flowering when observed. Link the structure to its role and life cycle. Seeds contain a young plant and stored food that helps support early growth under suitable conditions."},{"title":"Worked system explanation","text":"Suppose roots are severely damaged. Water uptake may decrease, reducing the water supply to leaves. Leaves may wilt and food-making can be affected. The explanation links damage, reduced function and consequences for other parts.\n\nDo not conclude that every wilted plant has damaged roots. Lack of water, heat and other conditions can also contribute. Record observations and investigate possible causes. A system explanation is stronger when it distinguishes what is known from what is inferred."},{"title":"Draw, explain and transfer","text":"Draw a familiar plant and label roots, stem, leaves and any reproductive parts visible. Beside each label, write one function. Add a sentence linking two parts, such as roots supplying water used by leaves.\n\nCompare your drawing with a reliable plant reference and correct missing or misleading features. Before practice, explain why a plant grown with water and mineral salts still needs suitable light for sustained food production. This prepares you to reason about transport and photosynthesis together."}]},{"id":"s-reproduction","title":"Reproduction in plants and humans","subject":"science","strand":"Cycles","skill":"reproduction","prerequisites":["s-life-cycles","s-plant-parts"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"43–45: reproduction; cells","goals":["Distinguish pollination fertilisation dispersal and germination","Describe basic human reproductive cells and organs","Explain how reproduction continues a kind of organism"],"visual":"flower-cycle","art":"plant-study","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Cells and new organisms","text":"Living things are made of cells, the basic units of life. In sexual reproduction, a male reproductive cell joins with a female reproductive cell during fertilisation. The resulting fertilised cell can develop into a new organism under suitable conditions.\n\nReproduction enables a kind of organism to continue across generations. It is different from growth of an existing individual. This lesson uses accurate, age-appropriate biological terms. A parent or teacher can discuss questions that arise; detailed human reproductive anatomy beyond the primary syllabus is not required here."},{"title":"Pollination in flowering plants","text":"Pollen is produced in the anthers. Pollination is the transfer of pollen from an anther to a stigma. Wind or animals such as insects can help transfer pollen, depending on the plant. The stigma and anther have different roles; pollination is not the movement of an entire seed.\n\nSome flowers have features that help attract pollinators, while wind-pollinated flowers may look different. Do not infer the pollination method solely from one attractive illustration. Use the flower’s features and reliable information about the plant."},{"title":"Fertilisation and seed formation","text":"After suitable pollination, a male reproductive cell can fuse with a female reproductive cell during fertilisation. Fertilisation is distinct from pollen transfer. In the usual primary flowering-plant model, fertilised ovules develop into seeds and the ovary develops into the fruit.\n\nA pollinated flower does not automatically guarantee successful seed formation: fertilisation and suitable development must follow. The important sequence is pollination, fertilisation, then seed and fruit development. Detailed pollen-tube growth is beyond what this primary lesson requires."},{"title":"Seed dispersal and germination","text":"Dispersal moves seeds away from the parent plant. Wind, water, animals or splitting fruits may help, depending on the seed or fruit’s structure. Wings, hairs, buoyant coverings, hooks or edible fruits can be connected to suitable dispersal methods.\n\nDispersal can reduce competition near the parent and help plants reach new places. Germination is the start of growth of a seed into a seedling under suitable conditions, including water, oxygen and a suitable temperature. Light requirements differ among seeds; do not assume all seeds require light to begin germination."},{"title":"Human reproduction","text":"In humans, testes produce sperm cells and ovaries produce egg cells. Fertilisation occurs when a sperm cell fuses with an egg cell. The fertilised egg can develop into a baby in the womb, also called the uterus.\n\nThe baby does not develop in the stomach, which belongs to the digestive system. Names of body parts help distinguish their functions. Detailed stages of fetal development and the umbilical cord are beyond the required primary scope of this lesson."},{"title":"Worked comparison","text":"Compare a flowering plant and a human. Both use male and female reproductive cells in sexual reproduction, and fertilisation begins development of a new individual. Their organs, methods of bringing cells together and later development differ.\n\nNow compare pollination and seed dispersal. Pollination moves pollen before fertilisation; dispersal moves seeds after they have formed. Confusing these processes changes the sequence and explanation. Build a timeline and state what is moving at each stage."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Choose an unfamiliar seed image from a reliable reference. Describe an observed feature, propose a dispersal method, then explain how the feature helps. A wing-like structure may help wind transport; a hook may attach to an animal. The appearance suggests a hypothesis, which observations can test.\n\nFor human biology, practise naming the reproductive cells, their producing organs and the place where the developing baby grows. Use respectful scientific language and ask an adult about anything unclear. The goal is accurate understanding of life processes, not memorising an oversized anatomy list."}]},{"id":"s-body","title":"Digestion: from food to absorbed nutrients","subject":"science","strand":"Systems","skill":"body","prerequisites":["s-diversity"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"53: human digestive system","goals":["Trace food through the digestive system","Distinguish digestion absorption and removal of undigested food","Link organ functions in a sequence"],"visual":"digestion","art":"inquiry-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Why digest food?","text":"Food supplies materials for growth and repair and is a source from which the body releases energy. Much of the food we eat must be broken down into simpler substances that can be absorbed. Digestion is this breakdown; absorption is the movement of suitable digested substances into the blood.\n\nChewing food into smaller pieces helps but is not the whole digestive process. A food item becoming smaller does not automatically mean all its useful substances have entered the bloodstream. Keep breakdown, absorption and transport as connected but distinct ideas."},{"title":"Mouth and gullet","text":"Teeth break food into smaller pieces in the mouth. Saliva moistens food and begins digestion of some components. The tongue helps move food for swallowing. Different parts contribute different actions, so the mouth is more than an opening into the body.\n\nThe gullet carries swallowed food to the stomach. It is not the windpipe. Mixing these names can produce an incorrect explanation of both digestion and breathing. Trace food and air along separate routes in your own labelled diagrams."},{"title":"Stomach","text":"The stomach mixes food and continues digestion. Its muscular action and digestive juices help process the food. It does not simply store an unchanged meal until it passes out of the body.\n\nThe stomach is also not where a baby develops; that is the womb. Scientific vocabulary prevents everyday phrases such as “baby in the stomach” from confusing different body systems. At primary level, focus on the sequence and main functions rather than memorising names of digestive chemicals."},{"title":"Small intestine","text":"Digestion continues in the small intestine, and digested food substances are absorbed into the blood there. Blood then transports useful substances to the rest of the body. This connects the digestive and circulatory systems.\n\nThe small intestine is not called “small” because it is shorter than the large intestine. Its name refers to its narrower width. You do not need detailed microscopic structures to explain the main primary function: digestion and absorption of digested food."},{"title":"Large intestine and undigested material","text":"The large intestine absorbs water from the remaining contents. Undigested material eventually leaves the body as faeces through the anus. Do not describe all faeces as unused digested food: some material was not digested and absorbed.\n\nRemoving undigested material is different from releasing energy from food in cells. Food does not disappear when eaten; materials move through processes and are used, transported or removed. Careful tracking avoids the idea that the stomach turns everything directly into energy."},{"title":"Worked journey of a meal","text":"Trace a piece of bread: it is chewed and mixed with saliva in the mouth, travels along the gullet, undergoes further processing in the stomach, and reaches the small intestine where digestion and absorption occur. Remaining material passes to the large intestine, where water is absorbed.\n\nNow ask what happens after absorption. Useful substances in the blood reach body parts that need them. An answer ending at the stomach misses the key connection between eating and supplying the rest of the body. Different food components are processed differently, so this is a simplified system account."},{"title":"Model and explain","text":"Draw the organs in sequence with arrows that mean movement of food contents. Separately draw an arrow from the small intestine to blood, labelled absorption of digested food. This second arrow represents a different movement, not the next segment of the tube.\n\nExplain why a digestive problem affecting absorption can matter even if someone eats enough food. The body must obtain usable substances, not merely take food into the mouth. This reasoning links organ function to whole-body consequences without requiring a memorised medical diagnosis."}]},{"id":"s-transport","title":"Transport and breathing: connected systems","subject":"science","strand":"Systems","skill":"body","prerequisites":["s-body","s-plant-parts"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"54 and 57: respiratory circulatory and plant transport systems","goals":["Trace air and blood functions without confusing their routes","Explain connections among human systems","Distinguish plant water and food transport"],"visual":"transport","art":"plant-study","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A system serves other systems","text":"Digested food must reach body parts, and oxygen must reach cells. Carbon dioxide produced in the body must also be transported away. The digestive, respiratory and circulatory systems work together to make these exchanges possible.\n\nNo one organ completes all these tasks. The small intestine absorbs digested food, lungs exchange gases, and the heart pumps blood through blood vessels. Linking functions is more useful than memorising organ names as an isolated list."},{"title":"Air into the lungs","text":"Air enters through the nose or mouth, passes along the windpipe and reaches the lungs. In the lungs, oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide moves from blood into the air that is breathed out. The primary model does not require naming tiny air sacs or detailed branching structures.\n\nAir is a mixture containing mainly nitrogen and oxygen, with smaller amounts of gases including carbon dioxide and water vapour. Breathed-out air still contains oxygen; it is not pure carbon dioxide. Relative amounts change because of gas exchange."},{"title":"Blood transports materials","text":"The heart pumps blood through blood vessels. Blood carries oxygen from the lungs and digested food from the digestive system to body parts. It also carries carbon dioxide back toward the lungs for removal during breathing out.\n\nDo not say that the heart makes oxygen or digests food. It enables transport by pumping. Detailed heart chambers, valves and classifications of vessels are beyond the required primary scope here, so focus on the connected routes and functions."},{"title":"Breathing and energy release","text":"Breathing moves air into and out of the lungs. Respiration releases energy from food in living cells; it is not simply another word for breathing. Oxygen is used in the usual aerobic process, and carbon dioxide is produced. The detailed chemical equation is extension work.\n\nDuring exercise, the body’s demand for energy increases. Breathing and heartbeat commonly increase to support faster supply and removal of materials. Measurements vary with effort, health and fitness, so avoid diagnosing a person from a single reading in a school activity."},{"title":"Plant transport","text":"Roots absorb water and mineral salts. Water-carrying tubes transport these to the stem and leaves. Food made in green parts is transported through food-carrying tubes to other parts that need it, including roots and growing structures.\n\nWater and food pathways have different roles. Food is not simply carried only upward from the soil. The specialist terms xylem and phloem can be learned later; understanding the two functions is the primary goal. Plants also release energy from food through respiration in living cells."},{"title":"Worked comparison","text":"Consider a leafy cutting placed in coloured water in an adult-led demonstration. Colour appearing along particular paths supports a conclusion about water transport. It does not by itself show the route of all transported food, because the colour entered with water.\n\nCompare this with a human system diagram. Arrows must be labelled with what is moving: air, blood, oxygen or digested food. Without labels, an attractive arrow diagram can wrongly imply that whole food pieces travel from the stomach into the heart."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Explain how oxygen reaches a working leg muscle and how digested food reaches the same area. Both involve blood transport, but their starting routes differ. Then explain how a root receives food from leaves while supplying water used by them.\n\nUse the pattern source, pathway, destination and purpose. If an organ or transport pathway is damaged, predict the consequence by following what it normally supplies. Avoid jumping directly to a disease name that the evidence does not establish."}]},{"id":"s-electricity","title":"Electric circuits: complete paths and comparisons","subject":"science","strand":"Systems","skill":"electricity","prerequisites":["s-materials","s-inquiry"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"59: electrical systems","goals":["Build a complete circuit model","Compare series and parallel bulb arrangements","Explain fair circuit investigations and safety"],"visual":"circuit","art":"science-bench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A complete conducting path","text":"A simple circuit needs a source such as a battery, connecting conductors and a component such as a bulb. For the bulb to light, there must be a complete conducting path through it between the battery terminals. A gap or an open switch breaks the path.\n\nTouching one wire to a bulb is usually insufficient. Both contacts of the bulb must be connected appropriately in a complete path. Use the circuit diagram to trace the route continuously; an attractive picture without correct connections is not evidence of a working circuit."},{"title":"Conductors and insulators","text":"Electrical conductors allow electric current to pass through readily. Metals such as copper are commonly used for wires. Electrical insulators, including the plastic coating on many wires, help prevent unintended conducting paths.\n\nHeat conduction and electrical conduction are different properties, even though metals commonly do both well. Water near electrical equipment is hazardous. Use only a supervised low-voltage classroom kit, never mains sockets, damaged batteries or improvised connections that directly join battery terminals."},{"title":"Switches and current","text":"Closing a switch completes its part of a conducting path; opening it breaks that path. Whether all bulbs turn off depends on where the switch is located. A switch in a shared part of a circuit can affect several branches, while one in a separate branch may affect only that branch.\n\nCurrent is not “used up” by a bulb as it moves around a simple series circuit. Energy is transferred in components. At primary level, reason with complete paths, component arrangements and observed brightness rather than numerical current or resistance formulas."},{"title":"Series bulbs","text":"In a series arrangement, components lie along one path. If that path is broken at one bulb, the other bulbs in the same path stop working. With comparable identical bulbs and the same suitable battery, adding bulbs in series commonly makes each bulb dimmer.\n\nThis brightness comparison depends on the specified components and working battery. Real bulbs and batteries are not perfect mathematical models. Do not claim a precise fraction of brightness without measurements, and do not assume all bulbs of different designs behave identically."},{"title":"Parallel bulbs","text":"In a parallel arrangement, branches provide separate paths. If one bulb’s branch is broken, another complete branch can still work. Identical bulbs connected directly across the same suitable battery in separate branches can have similar brightness to a single bulb across it in the simple classroom model.\n\nMore parallel branches can make a real battery run down faster. The battery is not an unlimited source. A diagram must show where branches split and join; lines crossing on a page are connected only if the diagram indicates a junction."},{"title":"Worked investigation","text":"Investigate the effect of adding a battery in series on one suitable bulb’s brightness using a rated classroom kit. Keep bulb type, wire connections and other conditions the same, and follow the kit’s allowed battery count. More batteries do not mean “always safer” or “always better”; too much can damage components.\n\nFor a bulb-arrangement comparison, use identical bulbs and the same supply conditions. Predict which paths remain complete when one branch switch opens. Then test in the app’s model or an adult-approved kit. A simulation is a simplified model, not a promise of exact real-world brightness."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For any circuit, trace each complete path from one battery terminal, through the relevant components, to the other terminal. Identify the location of a break and which paths it interrupts.\n\nConnect cause and observation: opening this branch switch breaks this bulb’s path, while the other branch remains complete. This is stronger than memorising “parallel is better”. Series and parallel arrangements serve different purposes; the reasoning comes from the actual connections."}]},{"id":"s-photosynthesis","title":"Photosynthesis and the need for food","subject":"science","strand":"Energy","skill":"plants","prerequisites":["s-plant-parts","s-transport","s-light"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"78: photosynthesis and food; systems links","goals":["Identify inputs and products of photosynthesis","Distinguish food-making from respiration","Evaluate evidence from plant investigations"],"visual":"photosynthesis","art":"inquiry-workbench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Food-making uses light energy","text":"Green plants use light energy to make food from water and carbon dioxide through photosynthesis. Oxygen is released. Chlorophyll, the green pigment, helps absorb light energy. Leaves are common sites of photosynthesis, although other green parts can also carry it out.\n\nLight is an energy input, not a material ingredient that becomes water. Water and carbon dioxide supply materials used to make food. The distinction between energy and matter helps explain why simply watering a plant in darkness cannot support food-making indefinitely."},{"title":"Where the inputs come from","text":"Roots commonly absorb water, which is transported to leaves through water-carrying tubes. Carbon dioxide enters from the surrounding air. Light comes from a suitable source such as the Sun. The plant must have the necessary structures and suitable conditions to use these inputs.\n\nMineral salts absorbed by roots support healthy growth but are not the sugar food made by photosynthesis. Fertiliser does not replace the need for light and carbon dioxide. It is misleading to say that plants obtain all their food by “eating nutrients from soil”."},{"title":"Food is used and stored","text":"Food made in green parts can be transported to other parts, used for growth and repair, or used in respiration to release energy. Some may be stored in forms such as starch. Roots and other non-green parts depend on this supply even though they may not photosynthesise themselves.\n\nA plant is therefore both a food producer and a living organism that uses food. Producing food does not remove its need to release energy. This point connects plant systems, food chains and energy conversion."},{"title":"Photosynthesis and respiration differ","text":"Photosynthesis makes food using light energy. Respiration releases energy from food in living cells. Plants respire during both day and night. Photosynthesis requires suitable light, so the balance of gas exchanges can change with conditions.\n\nDo not memorise “plants take in carbon dioxide and animals take in oxygen” as a complete account. Plants also use oxygen in aerobic respiration. For primary questions, identify which process is being asked about before stating the gas inputs and outputs."},{"title":"Worked investigation: light","text":"To investigate whether light is needed for photosynthesis, a classroom demonstration may compare leaf regions receiving light with regions kept dark, then test for starch after appropriate preparation. The setup must account for previously stored starch and other differences.\n\nThe chemical procedures in a starch test require trained adult supervision and are not a home activity in this app. Learn the reasoning from recorded observations or a safe classroom demonstration: a starch result is evidence about stored food under the test conditions, not a direct photograph of energy."},{"title":"Evaluate an illustration","text":"The seedling illustration presents a possible setup, not measured results. Compare starting sizes, water, soil, temperature, duration and light. If one plant begins larger, final height alone is not a fair measure of growth. A shade box may also change temperature and air movement.\n\nPropose improvements before predicting results. Measure change in height and other suitable signs, use similar starting plants and repeat observations. A long pale shoot in darkness does not establish that darkness produces a healthier plant; height and health are different claims."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Explain the chain light energy enables food-making; food reaches plant parts; respiration releases energy from food; growth and other life processes use materials and energy. Keep each step distinct.\n\nThen consider a food chain beginning with a green plant. Photosynthesis introduces a source of food for organisms that cannot make food this way. This prepares you to explain why reducing suitable light can affect organisms beyond the plants themselves."}]},{"id":"s-energy","title":"Energy forms and conversions","subject":"science","strand":"Energy","skill":"energy","prerequisites":["s-heat","s-electricity","s-photosynthesis"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"80: energy forms and conversions","goals":["Identify relevant forms of energy","Trace conversions through a system","Distinguish energy transfer from material movement"],"visual":"energy","art":"science-bench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Energy helps explain change","text":"Energy is needed for processes such as movement, heating, producing light and living activities. We describe useful forms including kinetic, potential, electrical, light, heat and sound energy at primary level. The choice of form depends on the situation being explained.\n\nKinetic energy is associated with movement. Potential energy is associated with position or stored conditions, such as a raised object or a stretched spring. Food and fuels store chemical potential energy. Energy is not the same as a material that can be weighed in a lunch box."},{"title":"A conversion chain","text":"In a battery-powered torch, chemical potential energy in the battery is converted to electrical energy, then to light and heat. The battery does not contain a miniature beam of light waiting to travel down the wire. Naming conversions explains how different parts contribute.\n\nA device often produces more than one output. A motor may produce movement as well as sound and heat. “Useful output” depends on the intended purpose; heat is useful in a heater but often an unwanted output in a motor."},{"title":"Movement and stored energy","text":"Raise a book and its gravitational potential energy increases relative to the chosen lower position. If it falls, this can be converted into kinetic energy. When it stops on a surface, energy is transferred to the surroundings, including as heat and sound.\n\nThe energy does not simply vanish because motion stops. However, you need not calculate kinetic or potential energy using secondary-school formulas in this primary unit. Focus on identifying the relevant changes and the system involved."},{"title":"Living things and sunlight","text":"Green plants convert light energy into chemical potential energy stored in food through photosynthesis. Animals obtain food by feeding on plants or other organisms. Respiration releases energy from food for life processes.\n\nA food chain therefore also represents energy transfer. Matter and energy must not be treated as identical: organisms take in materials, use them and release wastes, while energy is transferred and becomes less available for subsequent useful processes. The detailed energy budget is extension work."},{"title":"Worked example: a wind-up toy","text":"Winding a toy stores elastic potential energy in its spring. When released, the spring drives moving parts, producing kinetic energy. Some energy is also transferred as sound and heat. A toy stopping does not prove that energy has been destroyed.\n\nCompare it with a battery toy. Both can produce movement, but the initial stored energy and conversion pathway differ. A good answer identifies the actual source instead of writing the same generic chain for every moving object."},{"title":"Evidence and boundaries","text":"When describing a conversion, define the system. If you study only the bulb, electrical energy enters and light and heat leave. If you include the battery, begin with the battery’s stored chemical energy. Both descriptions can be useful when their boundaries are clear.\n\nDo not add energy outputs solely because they are on a memorised list. Name those relevant to the device and observations. Claims about exact efficiency require measurements, so an illustration of a bright bulb cannot establish a numerical efficiency."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"Choose a familiar appliance and trace input, conversion and outputs. Identify its useful output and one additional output. Explain why a warm casing is evidence of heat transfer, not proof that the device has stopped obeying energy principles.\n\nFor an unfamiliar machine, follow what supplies it, what moves or changes, and what reaches the surroundings. This systematic approach is more reliable than guessing a chain from the machine’s name."}]},{"id":"s-forces","title":"Forces: motion, shape and interactions","subject":"science","strand":"Interactions","skill":"forces","prerequisites":["s-magnets","s-energy"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"63–64: forces","goals":["Describe effects of forces on motion and shape","Identify gravity friction magnetic and elastic spring forces","Explain evidence without overextending the model"],"visual":"forces","art":"science-bench","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"A force is an interaction","text":"A force is a push or pull exerted by one object on another. Forces can change an object’s speed, direction or shape. They do not always produce visible movement: pressing gently on a wall involves an interaction even if the wall does not noticeably move.\n\nIdentify both objects in the interaction. “There is a force” is less useful than “the hand pushes the trolley”. Then connect the force to the observed effect. Not every change has the same cause, so look carefully at the setup before naming a force."},{"title":"Contact and non-contact forces","text":"Friction and the force from a compressed spring usually involve contact. Gravitational and magnetic forces can act without direct contact between the interacting objects. An object can experience several forces at the same time.\n\nPrimary questions focus on qualitative effects. Numerical acceleration, formal vector calculations and detailed resultant-force equations are extension topics, not assumed requirements here. A simple model should still be accurate: contact is not required for every force."},{"title":"Gravity and weight","text":"Earth exerts gravitational force on objects, pulling them toward Earth. Weight is the gravitational force acting on an object. Mass and weight are different ideas even though everyday language sometimes mixes them. Mass is commonly measured in kilograms; force, including weight, is measured in newtons.\n\nYou do not need a weight formula for this primary lesson. Explain falling in terms of gravity, while recognising that air resistance or other forces can also act. The statement “heavier objects always fall faster” is not a valid general rule for every setup."},{"title":"Friction","text":"Friction opposes relative sliding, or the tendency to slide, between surfaces in contact. It can slow a sliding object and produce heating. It can also be useful for grip, such as when a shoe grips the floor or a brake slows a wheel.\n\nAvoid the over-broad rule “friction always acts opposite to the object’s motion” in every possible situation. Rolling and driven-wheel directions require more detailed analysis and are outside the primary friction-direction scope used here. For a block sliding across a fixed table, friction acts against its sliding motion."},{"title":"Springs and shape changes","text":"Stretching or compressing a spring produces an elastic spring force tending to restore its shape. Within an appropriate working range, greater stretching usually produces a greater restoring force. Stretching too far can damage the spring, so the pattern must not be extended without limits.\n\nA spring-based measuring device can compare forces after calibration. The app’s model is illustrative, not a calibrated instrument. Use only a safe classroom spring and light loads with an adult; never launch stretched objects toward people."},{"title":"Worked investigation: sliding","text":"To investigate how surface type affects the distance a block slides, keep the block, starting conditions and measurement method the same. Use a repeatable release rather than an inconsistent hand push. Measure stopping distance and repeat trials.\n\nIf one surface gives a shorter stopping distance under those controls, greater resistance to sliding is a possible explanation. The test does not prove that every object on that surface behaves identically. Roughness, deformation and the contact pair all matter; state the observed comparison rather than a universal rule about appearance."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For a forces explanation, identify the interacting objects, name the force, describe the relevant direction qualitatively, and connect it to change in motion or shape. Keep force and energy as related but different concepts.\n\nCompare a falling ball, a sliding block, a compass needle and a stretched spring. Each involves interactions, but different forces dominate the explanation. A strong answer chooses the force from the evidence and describes its effect instead of selecting a familiar word at random."}]},{"id":"s-ecosystems","title":"Food chains, webs and communities","subject":"science","strand":"Interactions","skill":"ecology","prerequisites":["s-diversity","s-photosynthesis"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"67–70: interactions within the environment","goals":["Distinguish organism population community and habitat","Interpret food-chain arrows as energy transfer","Predict changes with stated ecological assumptions"],"visual":"food-web","art":"living-world","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Organisms and their surroundings","text":"A habitat is the place where an organism lives. Physical conditions include light, temperature, water and air. Organisms also interact with other living things. A suitable habitat provides conditions and resources that meet the organism’s needs.\n\nA population consists of organisms of the same kind living in a particular area. A community includes populations of different kinds living and interacting in that area. A pond illustration can show a community, but it does not establish exact population sizes or every interaction."},{"title":"Producers, consumers and decomposers","text":"Green plants are producers because they make food through photosynthesis. Consumers obtain food by feeding on other organisms. Decomposers, including many fungi and bacteria, break down dead organisms and wastes, helping return materials to the environment.\n\nDecomposers are not simply “bad organisms”. Their role helps explain recycling of materials. Energy and materials must not be confused: materials cycle, while energy enters many ecosystems through sunlight and is transferred through feeding and other processes."},{"title":"Food-chain arrows","text":"In a food chain, an arrow points from the food organism to the organism that feeds on it. It represents transfer of energy through feeding. Grass → grasshopper → frog means the grasshopper eats grass and the frog eats the grasshopper.\n\nDo not reverse arrows to show where an animal moves while hunting. The arrow has a defined meaning. A food chain is a simplified model; many organisms have more than one food source and may be eaten by more than one kind of consumer."},{"title":"Food webs","text":"A food web combines several feeding relationships. Follow individual arrows to identify food sources and consumers. Before predicting a change, check whether alternative foods or predators exist. Removing one food source may have a smaller effect when others remain available.\n\nA web diagram records specified relationships; it does not automatically give feeding rates or population sizes. Avoid claiming exact numbers from arrows alone. Different time periods, movement into the area and other environmental changes can alter outcomes."},{"title":"Worked prediction","text":"Suppose the stated chain is grass → grasshopper → frog, and frogs decrease while other conditions initially remain similar. Reduced feeding on grasshoppers may allow their population to increase. More grasshoppers may then consume more grass.\n\nUse “may” and state the assumptions. Disease, migration, food shortage or other predators could change the outcome. This does not make the chain useless; it makes the prediction conditional on the model. Explain each link instead of writing an unsupported “everything will die”."},{"title":"Observe a habitat","text":"Study a small safe area such as a garden patch. Record visible organisms and physical conditions without disturbing them. Use references to check identities and feeding relationships. Do not assume that two organisms near one another must eat each other.\n\nConstruct a food chain only from supported relationships. Mark unknown links as questions to research. The original pond illustration is a starting point for observation and inquiry, not a species-accurate record of a particular habitat or an experimentally established food web."},{"title":"Explain and transfer","text":"For a population-change question, name the changed factor, trace the direct effect, then consider indirect effects and alternatives. State which relationships come from the question and which require further evidence.\n\nExplain why protecting producers can affect consumers and why decomposition matters. This connects photosynthesis, feeding, energy transfer and recycling into a system account rather than a collection of disconnected facts."}]},{"id":"s-environment","title":"Adaptations, environmental change and evidence","subject":"science","strand":"Interactions","skill":"ecology","prerequisites":["s-ecosystems","s-inquiry"],"source":"science","sourcePages":"67–70: adaptations and human impact; inquiry practices","goals":["Connect features and behaviour to survival","Explain competition and environmental impacts","Evaluate claims and plan an evidence-based response"],"visual":"","art":"living-world","extension":false,"minutes":"45–75, split across several sittings","pages":[{"title":"Adaptations connect features to conditions","text":"An adaptation is a feature or behaviour that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment. Explain both the feature and its effect. A broad leaf may help capture light in a particular setting; a body covering may reduce water loss in another.\n\nAvoid purpose-only stories such as “it has this because it wanted to survive”. Organisms do not choose to grow every useful inherited feature when they need it. Detailed evolutionary mechanisms belong to later study; here, focus on how an observed feature functions under stated conditions."},{"title":"Structural and behavioural examples","text":"Structural adaptations involve body features; behavioural adaptations involve actions. A streamlined body can reduce resistance during movement through water. Seeking shade during the hottest part of a day can reduce exposure to heat.\n\nOne feature may help in one environment and have costs in another. A very insulating covering may be useful in cold conditions but create difficulties in heat. Do not classify an organism as perfectly adapted to every possible environment from a single feature."},{"title":"Competition and resources","text":"Organisms compete when they need a limited resource such as food, water, light or space. Competition can occur among the same kind of organism and among different kinds. A resource being present does not mean there is enough for everyone.\n\nFor plants close together, shading can reduce light available to shorter plants, while roots may compete for water and mineral salts. An investigation must distinguish these possible causes. Spacing, water and light can change together, so a simple growth difference may not isolate one factor."},{"title":"Human impacts","text":"Human activities can change habitats through pollution, land clearing, resource use and introduction of organisms. Effects can spread through food webs and physical conditions. A polluted water body may affect producers, consumers and people who depend on it.\n\nConservation aims to protect living things and the resources and conditions they need. Proposals should connect action to mechanism: protecting vegetation can preserve habitat; reducing pollutants can improve water conditions. “Save nature” expresses a goal but does not explain how a particular action helps."},{"title":"Worked decision: a school pond","text":"Imagine a school pond with fewer visible insects after a nearby area is cleared. One explanation is habitat loss; another is seasonal change or altered observation conditions. First check how counts were made, over what duration, and whether other conditions changed.\n\nA useful response might combine repeated standardised observations with restoring suitable vegetation. Record both the action and the predicted effect. If insect counts rise later, consider other changes before claiming the intervention alone caused the increase. Good intentions do not replace careful evidence."},{"title":"Read beyond the lesson","text":"Choose a suitable science report from a museum, university or recognised science organisation. Identify its main claim, the evidence offered, the date and who produced it. Separate a directly measured result from an expert prediction or an illustration.\n\nWrite one paragraph explaining the idea in your own words, then list a question the report does not answer. Check that a second source actually addresses the same claim. Wider reading supports curiosity and application; it is not a requirement to memorise every current science headline for an unpublished examination blueprint."},{"title":"Explain, discuss and revise","text":"Give a two-minute explanation of an environmental problem: the organisms affected, the changed condition, the causal links, possible actions and the evidence needed. Ask another person to challenge one assumption. Revise your explanation if the challenge reveals a gap.\n\nThis is an enrichment discussion routine alongside primary science content. It develops communication, independence and problem-solving that NUS High describes in its admissions FAQ, but it is not an official camp task or a guaranteed predictor of selection. Use external teaching and assessments to check broader readiness."}]}],"questions":[{"id":"m-number-q1","unit":"m-number","level":1,"form":"m-number-f1","prompt":"What is the value of 7 in 572,406?","choices":["70,000","700","7,000"],"answer":0,"why":"The 7 occupies the ten-thousands place, so its value is 70,000."},{"id":"m-number-q2","unit":"m-number","level":1,"form":"m-number-f2","prompt":"Which number is greatest?","choices":["305,900","350,009","305,090"],"answer":1,"why":"The hundred-thousands agree; compare ten thousands next."},{"id":"m-number-q3","unit":"m-number","level":2,"form":"m-number-f3","prompt":"Round 28,650 to the nearest thousand.","choices":["28,700","28,000","29,000"],"answer":2,"why":"28,650 is above the midpoint 28,500."},{"id":"m-number-q4","unit":"m-number","level":2,"form":"m-number-f4","prompt":"How many hundreds are in 4,000?","choices":["400","4","40"],"answer":2,"why":"4,000 ÷ 100 = 40 hundreds."},{"id":"m-number-q5","unit":"m-number","level":3,"form":"m-number-f5","prompt":"A calculation 49 × 201 gives 98,490. Which check exposes a size error?","choices":["50 × 200 is about 10,000","49 is odd","201 has three digits"],"answer":0,"why":"An estimate near 10,000 makes 98,490 implausible; the exact product is 9,849."},{"id":"m-number-q6","unit":"m-number","level":3,"form":"m-number-f6","prompt":"Which whole number rounds to 6,000 to the nearest thousand and 6,400 to the nearest hundred?","choices":["5,949","6,501","6,449"],"answer":2,"why":"6,449 is below 6,500 and below 6,450, so both rounded values fit."},{"id":"m-operations-q1","unit":"m-operations","level":1,"form":"m-operations-f1","prompt":"Which expression means 5 groups of 8?","choices":["8 − 5","5 × 8","5 + 8"],"answer":1,"why":"Multiplication combines equal groups."},{"id":"m-operations-q2","unit":"m-operations","level":1,"form":"m-operations-f2","prompt":"Evaluate 30 − 12 ÷ 3.","choices":["26","6","18"],"answer":0,"why":"Divide first: 12 ÷ 3 = 4; then subtract."},{"id":"m-operations-q3","unit":"m-operations","level":2,"form":"m-operations-f3","prompt":"Evaluate (17 + 7) ÷ 4.","choices":["18.75","24","6"],"answer":2,"why":"The bracketed total is 24; sharing it into four gives 6."},{"id":"m-operations-q4","unit":"m-operations","level":2,"form":"m-operations-f4","prompt":"A quotient is 23 remainder 2 when dividing by 5. What was the original number?","choices":["125","115","117"],"answer":2,"why":"The original number equals divisor × quotient + remainder: 5 × 23 + 2 = 117. The remainder 2 is less than the divisor 5."},{"id":"m-operations-q5","unit":"m-operations","level":3,"form":"m-operations-f5","prompt":"95 people travel in vehicles holding at most 6 each. How many vehicles are needed?","choices":["16","15","17"],"answer":0,"why":"15 vehicles hold only 90 people; one more is needed."},{"id":"m-operations-q6","unit":"m-operations","level":3,"form":"m-operations-f6","prompt":"Which equals 48 × 19 without changing the quantity?","choices":["48 × 20 − 48","48 × 18 − 48","48 × 20 − 1"],"answer":0,"why":"Nineteen groups are twenty groups minus one whole group of 48."},{"id":"m-factors-q1","unit":"m-factors","level":1,"form":"m-factors-f1","prompt":"Which is a factor of 28?","choices":["9","6","7"],"answer":2,"why":"28 = 7 × 4 with no remainder."},{"id":"m-factors-q2","unit":"m-factors","level":1,"form":"m-factors-f2","prompt":"Which is a positive multiple of both 3 and 4?","choices":["7","12","18"],"answer":1,"why":"A common multiple must be divisible by both numbers. 12 = 3 × 4 and 12 = 4 × 3, so it satisfies both conditions."},{"id":"m-factors-q3","unit":"m-factors","level":2,"form":"m-factors-f3","prompt":"What is the greatest common factor of 16 and 24?","choices":["4","16","8"],"answer":2,"why":"The common factors are 1, 2, 4 and 8."},{"id":"m-factors-q4","unit":"m-factors","level":2,"form":"m-factors-f4","prompt":"What is the smallest positive common multiple of 6 and 8?","choices":["48","24","14"],"answer":1,"why":"The first shared positive value in their multiples lists is 24."},{"id":"m-factors-q5","unit":"m-factors","level":3,"form":"m-factors-f5","prompt":"All 30 red and 42 blue beads are packed into the greatest number of identical packs, with no beads left over. How many blue beads are in each pack?","choices":["7","12","6"],"answer":0,"why":"The greatest number of packs is 6; 42 ÷ 6 = 7 blue beads per pack."},{"id":"m-factors-q6","unit":"m-factors","level":3,"form":"m-factors-f6","prompt":"Two bells ring every 9 and 12 minutes, starting together. Which claim is justified?","choices":["They next ring together after 36 minutes","They next meet after 21 minutes","They must wait 108 minutes"],"answer":0,"why":"36 is the first positive common multiple; 108 is a later meeting."},{"id":"m-fractions-q1","unit":"m-fractions","level":1,"form":"m-fractions-f1","prompt":"In 3/8, what does 8 tell us?","choices":["The whole is divided into eight equal parts","Eight parts are selected","The whole contains three objects"],"answer":0,"why":"The denominator names the equal subdivision of one whole."},{"id":"m-fractions-q2","unit":"m-fractions","level":1,"form":"m-fractions-f2","prompt":"Which equals 2/3?","choices":["3/4","4/6","2/6"],"answer":1,"why":"Each third can be split into two sixths."},{"id":"m-fractions-q3","unit":"m-fractions","level":2,"form":"m-fractions-f3","prompt":"Which is greater?","choices":["7/10","They are equal","3/5"],"answer":0,"why":"3/5 = 6/10, which is less than 7/10."},{"id":"m-fractions-q4","unit":"m-fractions","level":2,"form":"m-fractions-f4","prompt":"What is 11/4 as a mixed number?","choices":["2 3/4","3 1/4","2 1/4"],"answer":0,"why":"Eight quarters make two wholes, leaving three quarters."},{"id":"m-fractions-q5","unit":"m-fractions","level":3,"form":"m-fractions-f5","prompt":"Half of a 10 cm strip and half of an 18 cm strip are compared. Which is longer?","choices":["The half from the 10 cm strip","The half from the 18 cm strip","They must be equal"],"answer":1,"why":"The wholes differ: the halves are 9 cm and 5 cm."},{"id":"m-fractions-q6","unit":"m-fractions","level":3,"form":"m-fractions-f6","prompt":"2/7 of 35 counters are red. How many are not red?","choices":["25","10","30"],"answer":0,"why":"One seventh is 5; red is 10; the remaining five sevenths are 25."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q1","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":1,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f1","prompt":"Find 1/4 + 2/4.","choices":["2/4","3/4","3/8"],"answer":1,"why":"Three quarter-sized pieces make 3/4."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q2","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":1,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f2","prompt":"What is half of 3/5?","choices":["6/5","3/5","3/10"],"answer":2,"why":"Half of each fifth produces tenths; three tenths remain."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q3","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":2,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f3","prompt":"Calculate 2/3 + 1/6.","choices":["3/9","1/2","5/6"],"answer":2,"why":"2/3 = 4/6; adding one sixth gives five sixths."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q4","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":2,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f4","prompt":"Calculate 2 1/5 − 3/5.","choices":["1 3/5","2 2/5","1 2/5"],"answer":0,"why":"Regroup 2 1/5 as 1 6/5, then subtract 3/5."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q5","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":3,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f5","prompt":"A full recipe uses 3/4 litre of milk. You make 2/3 of the recipe. How much milk is needed?","choices":["9/8 litre","5/7 litre","1/2 litre"],"answer":2,"why":"2/3 × 3/4 = 6/12 = 1/2 litre."},{"id":"m-fraction-ops-q6","unit":"m-fraction-ops","level":3,"form":"m-fraction-ops-f6","prompt":"Which claim about positive numbers is correct?","choices":["Multiplying by 3/4 gives three quarters of the starting number","Multiplication always increases a number","Adding fractions requires adding denominators"],"answer":0,"why":"Scaling by a proper fraction reduces a positive quantity."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q1","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":1,"form":"m-fraction-division-f1","prompt":"1/5 litre is shared equally between 2 cups. Each receives…","choices":["1/7 litre","2/5 litre","1/10 litre"],"answer":2,"why":"A fifth split into two equal parts produces tenths."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q2","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":1,"form":"m-fraction-division-f2","prompt":"How many quarters fit in 2 wholes?","choices":["4","2","8"],"answer":2,"why":"Each whole contains four quarters."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q3","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":2,"form":"m-fraction-division-f3","prompt":"Calculate 3/5 ÷ 3.","choices":["1/5","9/5","3/8"],"answer":0,"why":"Three fifth-sized pieces shared among three give one fifth each."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q4","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":2,"form":"m-fraction-division-f4","prompt":"Calculate 2/3 ÷ 1/6.","choices":["2","1/9","4"],"answer":2,"why":"Two thirds equal four sixths."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q5","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":3,"form":"m-fraction-division-f5","prompt":"A 7/8 m ribbon is cut into 1/4 m pieces. How many complete pieces can be cut?","choices":["3.5","3","4"],"answer":1,"why":"7/8 ÷ 1/4 = 3.5 piece-lengths, allowing three complete pieces."},{"id":"m-fraction-division-q6","unit":"m-fraction-division","level":3,"form":"m-fraction-division-f6","prompt":"A 7/8 m ribbon has three pieces of length 1/4 m cut from it. What length remains?","choices":["1/8 m","1/4 m","1/2 m"],"answer":0,"why":"7/8 − 3/4 = 7/8 − 6/8 = 1/8 m."},{"id":"m-remainders-q1","unit":"m-remainders","level":1,"form":"m-remainders-f1","prompt":"After using 2/5 of some ribbon, what fraction of the original remains?","choices":["3/5","1/5","2/5"],"answer":0,"why":"The whole is 5/5; subtract 2/5."},{"id":"m-remainders-q2","unit":"m-remainders","level":1,"form":"m-remainders-f2","prompt":"Half of the remaining money refers to…","choices":["Half of the amount left at that stage","Always half of the original","A fixed dollar amount"],"answer":0,"why":"The reference whole is the current remainder."},{"id":"m-remainders-q3","unit":"m-remainders","level":2,"form":"m-remainders-f3","prompt":"60 counters lose 1/3, then 1/4 of the remainder. How many remain?","choices":["20","35","30"],"answer":2,"why":"40 remain after the first removal; three quarters of 40 are 30."},{"id":"m-remainders-q4","unit":"m-remainders","level":2,"form":"m-remainders-f4","prompt":"A final 18 ml is 3/4 of an earlier amount. What was that earlier amount?","choices":["24 ml","13.5 ml","72 ml"],"answer":0,"why":"One quarter is 6 ml; four quarters are 24 ml."},{"id":"m-remainders-q5","unit":"m-remainders","level":3,"form":"m-remainders-f5","prompt":"1/5 of some money is spent, then half of the remainder. $24 remains. What was the original amount?","choices":["$48","$60","$120"],"answer":1,"why":"The remainder is 4/5 × 1/2 = 2/5 of the original; 24 ÷ 2 × 5 = 60."},{"id":"m-remainders-q6","unit":"m-remainders","level":3,"form":"m-remainders-f6","prompt":"A bottle loses 1/3 of its water and then 50 ml. It has 150 ml left. What was the original volume?","choices":["600 ml","225 ml","300 ml"],"answer":2,"why":"Add back 50: 200 ml is two thirds. The original is 300 ml."},{"id":"m-decimals-q1","unit":"m-decimals","level":1,"form":"m-decimals-f1","prompt":"Which equals 0.4?","choices":["4/10","4/100","4/1000"],"answer":0,"why":"The digit 4 is in the tenths place, so 0.4 means four tenths, written as 4/10."},{"id":"m-decimals-q2","unit":"m-decimals","level":1,"form":"m-decimals-f2","prompt":"Which is larger?","choices":["0.9","0.81","They are equal"],"answer":0,"why":"0.90 has nine tenths; 0.81 has eight."},{"id":"m-decimals-q3","unit":"m-decimals","level":2,"form":"m-decimals-f3","prompt":"Calculate 2.35 + 0.8.","choices":["10.35","3.15","2.43"],"answer":1,"why":"Align hundredths: 2.35 + 0.80 = 3.15."},{"id":"m-decimals-q4","unit":"m-decimals","level":2,"form":"m-decimals-f4","prompt":"Calculate 0.36 ÷ 4.","choices":["0.009","0.9","0.09"],"answer":2,"why":"36 hundredths shared in four groups gives 9 hundredths."},{"id":"m-decimals-q5","unit":"m-decimals","level":3,"form":"m-decimals-f5","prompt":"Round 2.449 directly to one decimal place.","choices":["2.45","2.5","2.4"],"answer":2,"why":"2.449 is below the midpoint 2.45. Do not round twice."},{"id":"m-decimals-q6","unit":"m-decimals","level":3,"form":"m-decimals-f6","prompt":"Five bottles contain equal volumes of water, 3.75 litres altogether. Each bottle contains…","choices":["750 ml","75 ml","7,500 ml"],"answer":0,"why":"3.75 ÷ 5 = 0.75 litre = 750 ml."},{"id":"m-percent-q1","unit":"m-percent","level":1,"form":"m-percent-f1","prompt":"25% is equivalent to…","choices":["1/25","1/4","25/10"],"answer":1,"why":"25/100 simplifies to one quarter."},{"id":"m-percent-q2","unit":"m-percent","level":1,"form":"m-percent-f2","prompt":"In a 20% discount calculation, 100% usually means…","choices":["The sale price","The discount","The original price"],"answer":2,"why":"A discount is calculated relative to the stated original price."},{"id":"m-percent-q3","unit":"m-percent","level":2,"form":"m-percent-f3","prompt":"Find 30% of 90.","choices":["27","60","30"],"answer":0,"why":"10% is 9; three such parts make 27."},{"id":"m-percent-q4","unit":"m-percent","level":2,"form":"m-percent-f4","prompt":"12 out of 20 pupils walk to school. What percentage is this?","choices":["40%","60%","12%"],"answer":1,"why":"The whole group is 20 pupils. 12/20 = 3/5 = 60/100, so 60% of the group walk to school."},{"id":"m-percent-q5","unit":"m-percent","level":3,"form":"m-percent-f5","prompt":"An $80 item has a 25% discount and then a stated 10% tax on its sale price. What is the total?","choices":["$68","$66","$72"],"answer":1,"why":"The discounted price is $60; tax is $6, so pay $66."},{"id":"m-percent-q6","unit":"m-percent","level":3,"form":"m-percent-f6","prompt":"Simple interest of 4% per year on $150 gives how much interest after one year?","choices":["$6","$156","$154"],"answer":0,"why":"4/100 × 150 = 6; interest is different from the total balance."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q1","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":1,"form":"m-percent-whole-f1","prompt":"If 25% is 9, the whole is…","choices":["45","36","18"],"answer":1,"why":"Four quarters make the whole: 9 × 4 = 36."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q2","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":1,"form":"m-percent-whole-f2","prompt":"After a 30% discount, the sale price is what percentage of the original?","choices":["30%","70%","130%"],"answer":1,"why":"The original price represents 100%. Removing 30% leaves 100% − 30% = 70% of that original price."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q3","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":2,"form":"m-percent-whole-f3","prompt":"A sale price is $56 after 20% off. What was the original price?","choices":["$280","$70","$67.20"],"answer":1,"why":"$56 corresponds to 80%; divide by 0.8."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q4","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":2,"form":"m-percent-whole-f4","prompt":"A quantity rises from 80 to 100. What is its percentage increase?","choices":["25%","125%","20%"],"answer":0,"why":"The increase 20 is one quarter of the original 80."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q5","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":3,"form":"m-percent-whole-f5","prompt":"A value rises by 10%, then falls by 10% of its new value. What percentage of the original remains?","choices":["100%","90%","99%"],"answer":2,"why":"1.1 × 0.9 = 0.99, with different bases at the two stages."},{"id":"m-percent-whole-q6","unit":"m-percent-whole","level":3,"form":"m-percent-whole-f6","prompt":"A tank goes from 35% full to 100% full when 26 litres are added. What is its capacity?","choices":["74 litres","40 litres","65 litres"],"answer":1,"why":"26 litres represents 65%; 26 ÷ 0.65 = 40 litres."},{"id":"m-ratio-q1","unit":"m-ratio","level":1,"form":"m-ratio-f1","prompt":"In red : blue = 2 : 5, red is what fraction of the total?","choices":["2/5","2/7","5/7"],"answer":1,"why":"There are seven equal units altogether."},{"id":"m-ratio-q2","unit":"m-ratio","level":1,"form":"m-ratio-f2","prompt":"Which equals 3 : 4?","choices":["3 : 8","6 : 8","4 : 5"],"answer":1,"why":"Both terms are multiplied by two."},{"id":"m-ratio-q3","unit":"m-ratio","level":2,"form":"m-ratio-f3","prompt":"Share 45 in the ratio 2 : 3. What is the smaller share?","choices":["18","15","27"],"answer":0,"why":"Five units total 45, so two units are 18."},{"id":"m-ratio-q4","unit":"m-ratio","level":2,"form":"m-ratio-f4","prompt":"Two amounts have ratio 4 : 7 and difference 18. What is the larger?","choices":["24","42","77"],"answer":1,"why":"Three units are 18; seven units are 42."},{"id":"m-ratio-q5","unit":"m-ratio","level":3,"form":"m-ratio-f5","prompt":"A : B changes from 2 : 3 to 5 : 6 by adding only to A. If 8 are added, what is B?","choices":["64","24","48"],"answer":2,"why":"Rewrite 2 : 3 as 4 : 6. One common unit is added, so six units of B are 48."},{"id":"m-ratio-q6","unit":"m-ratio","level":3,"form":"m-ratio-f6","prompt":"A 2 : 3 : 5 mixture has a middle share of 21 g. What is the total mass?","choices":["63 g","42 g","70 g"],"answer":2,"why":"Three units are 21 g, making ten units 70 g."},{"id":"m-rate-q1","unit":"m-rate","level":1,"form":"m-rate-f1","prompt":"A rate of 5 litres per minute describes…","choices":["Only a volume","Only a duration","Volume for each unit of time"],"answer":2,"why":"A rate links two quantities with their units."},{"id":"m-rate-q2","unit":"m-rate","level":1,"form":"m-rate-f2","prompt":"Six identical pens cost $9. What is the unit price?","choices":["$3","$54","$1.50"],"answer":2,"why":"9 ÷ 6 = 1.50 dollars per pen."},{"id":"m-rate-q3","unit":"m-rate","level":2,"form":"m-rate-f3","prompt":"A pump delivers 4 litres per minute for 7 minutes. How much is delivered?","choices":["11 litres","1.75 litres","28 litres"],"answer":2,"why":"At a constant 4 litres each minute, 7 minutes gives 7 groups of 4 litres: 4 × 7 = 28 litres."},{"id":"m-rate-q4","unit":"m-rate","level":2,"form":"m-rate-f4","prompt":"120 pages print at 15 pages per minute. How long is needed?","choices":["15 minutes","8 minutes","1,800 minutes"],"answer":1,"why":"Count how many groups of 15 pages make 120: 120 ÷ 15 = 8. Each group takes one minute, so the duration is 8 minutes."},{"id":"m-rate-q5","unit":"m-rate","level":3,"form":"m-rate-f5","prompt":"A tank holds 10 litres and needs 46 litres. A tap adds 6 litres per minute. How long does adding the water take?","choices":["9 minutes","7 minutes","6 minutes"],"answer":2,"why":"Only 36 litres must be added, taking six minutes."},{"id":"m-rate-q6","unit":"m-rate","level":3,"form":"m-rate-f6","prompt":"Which relationship is not necessarily proportional?","choices":["Cost of identical pens at a fixed price each","Total fare with a fixed starting fee plus a distance charge","Water delivered by a constant-flow tap"],"answer":1,"why":"The starting fee prevents the total from scaling directly with distance."},{"id":"m-algebra-q1","unit":"m-algebra","level":1,"form":"m-algebra-f1","prompt":"If n is the price of one pen, 4n means…","choices":["4 + n","A number ending in n","4 × n"],"answer":2,"why":"It describes four copies of the price."},{"id":"m-algebra-q2","unit":"m-algebra","level":1,"form":"m-algebra-f2","prompt":"Simplify 2a + 5a.","choices":["7","10a","7a"],"answer":2,"why":"Two a-sized quantities and five a-sized quantities make seven."},{"id":"m-algebra-q3","unit":"m-algebra","level":2,"form":"m-algebra-f3","prompt":"Evaluate 3x + 2 when x = 6.","choices":["11","20","38"],"answer":1,"why":"Replace x with 6, then multiply before adding: 3 × 6 + 2 = 18 + 2 = 20."},{"id":"m-algebra-q4","unit":"m-algebra","level":2,"form":"m-algebra-f4","prompt":"Solve 4x + 3 = 27.","choices":["24","6","7.5"],"answer":1,"why":"Subtract three on both sides, then divide by four."},{"id":"m-algebra-q5","unit":"m-algebra","level":3,"form":"m-algebra-f5","prompt":"Four tickets at the same price each, plus one $5 fee, total $37. What is the price of one ticket?","choices":["$10.50","$8","$9.25"],"answer":1,"why":"Four tickets cost 37 − 5 = 32, so each costs eight."},{"id":"m-algebra-q6","unit":"m-algebra","level":3,"form":"m-algebra-f6","prompt":"Which step preserves 5y − 7 = 18?","choices":["Divide only the left side by 5","Add 7 to both sides","Subtract 7 on the right only"],"answer":1,"why":"Equal operations on both sides preserve equality."},{"id":"m-measure-q1","unit":"m-measure","level":1,"form":"m-measure-f1","prompt":"Which unit measures mass?","choices":["ml","cm","kg"],"answer":2,"why":"A kilogram (kg) is a unit of mass. Length and volume describe different quantities and use different units."},{"id":"m-measure-q2","unit":"m-measure","level":1,"form":"m-measure-f2","prompt":"One litre equals…","choices":["100 ml","10 ml","1,000 ml"],"answer":2,"why":"The prefix milli indicates a thousandth."},{"id":"m-measure-q3","unit":"m-measure","level":2,"form":"m-measure-f3","prompt":"Convert 2.07 m to cm.","choices":["2,070 cm","20.7 cm","207 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"Multiply the number of metres by 100."},{"id":"m-measure-q4","unit":"m-measure","level":2,"form":"m-measure-f4","prompt":"A ribbon extends from 3 cm to 11.5 cm on a ruler. Its length is…","choices":["14.5 cm","8.5 cm","11.5 cm"],"answer":1,"why":"Length is the difference of endpoint positions."},{"id":"m-measure-q5","unit":"m-measure","level":3,"form":"m-measure-f5","prompt":"A 1.25 kg mass and an 850 g mass total…","choices":["9.75 kg","1.335 kg","2.1 kg"],"answer":2,"why":"850 g is 0.85 kg; the total is 2.10 kg."},{"id":"m-measure-q6","unit":"m-measure","level":3,"form":"m-measure-f6","prompt":"A scale shows 200 ml to 500 ml in six equal intervals. Each interval represents…","choices":["60 ml","300 ml","50 ml"],"answer":2,"why":"The 300 ml difference is divided into six equal spaces."},{"id":"m-time-q1","unit":"m-time","level":1,"form":"m-time-f1","prompt":"1 hour 20 minutes equals…","choices":["80 minutes","120 minutes","1.2 minutes"],"answer":0,"why":"One hour contains 60 minutes. Add the extra 20 minutes: 60 + 20 = 80 minutes."},{"id":"m-time-q2","unit":"m-time","level":1,"form":"m-time-f2","prompt":"18:30 is…","choices":["8:30 p.m.","6:30 p.m.","6:30 a.m."],"answer":1,"why":"Subtract twelve from an hour after noon."},{"id":"m-time-q3","unit":"m-time","level":2,"form":"m-time-f3","prompt":"How long is 09:40 to 11:15?","choices":["75 minutes","135 minutes","95 minutes"],"answer":2,"why":"20 minutes to ten, sixty to eleven, then fifteen."},{"id":"m-time-q4","unit":"m-time","level":2,"form":"m-time-f4","prompt":"An event ends at 17:05 after 50 minutes. When did it start?","choices":["17:55","16:15","16:55"],"answer":1,"why":"Five minutes back reaches 17:00, then forty-five reaches 16:15."},{"id":"m-time-q5","unit":"m-time","level":3,"form":"m-time-f5","prompt":"A journey runs from 23:50 to 00:35 next day. How long is it?","choices":["85 minutes","45 minutes","15 minutes"],"answer":1,"why":"Ten minutes to midnight plus thirty-five afterwards."},{"id":"m-time-q6","unit":"m-time","level":3,"form":"m-time-f6","prompt":"Arrive by 10:00, travel 40 minutes, prepare 25 minutes. When must preparation begin?","choices":["08:55","09:20","09:35"],"answer":0,"why":"Departure is 09:20, and preparation begins 25 minutes earlier."},{"id":"m-area-q1","unit":"m-area","level":1,"form":"m-area-f1","prompt":"Which unit is appropriate for area?","choices":["cm³","cm²","cm"],"answer":1,"why":"Area measures the surface covered and is expressed in square units, such as square centimetres (cm²)."},{"id":"m-area-q2","unit":"m-area","level":1,"form":"m-area-f2","prompt":"A 7 cm by 4 cm rectangle has area…","choices":["11 cm²","22 cm²","28 cm²"],"answer":2,"why":"Seven squares per row across four rows make 28."},{"id":"m-area-q3","unit":"m-area","level":2,"form":"m-area-f3","prompt":"A rectangle has area 63 cm² and length 9 cm. Find its breadth.","choices":["72 cm","54 cm","7 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"A rectangle’s area is length × breadth. Undo multiplication by the length: breadth = 63 ÷ 9 = 7 cm. Check 9 × 7 = 63."},{"id":"m-area-q4","unit":"m-area","level":2,"form":"m-area-f4","prompt":"A triangle has base 12 cm and perpendicular height 5 cm. Find its area.","choices":["17 cm²","30 cm²","60 cm²"],"answer":1,"why":"A triangle has half the area of a rectangle with the same base and perpendicular height: 12 × 5 ÷ 2 = 30 cm²."},{"id":"m-area-q5","unit":"m-area","level":3,"form":"m-area-f5","prompt":"An 8 by 7 rectangle has a 3 by 2 corner removed. What area remains?","choices":["50 square units","56 square units","62 square units"],"answer":0,"why":"56 − 6 = 50; remove only the missing region."},{"id":"m-area-q6","unit":"m-area","level":3,"form":"m-area-f6","prompt":"A rectangle has both dimensions doubled. Which statement is true?","choices":["Perimeter quadruples","Area doubles","Area quadruples and perimeter doubles"],"answer":2,"why":"Area uses a product of two lengths, while perimeter is their doubled sum."},{"id":"m-angles-q1","unit":"m-angles","level":1,"form":"m-angles-f1","prompt":"The vertex of ∠PQR is…","choices":["Q","R","P"],"answer":0,"why":"The middle letter names the vertex."},{"id":"m-angles-q2","unit":"m-angles","level":1,"form":"m-angles-f2","prompt":"Angles around a point total…","choices":["360°","90°","180°"],"answer":0,"why":"A full turn measures 360 degrees."},{"id":"m-angles-q3","unit":"m-angles","level":2,"form":"m-angles-f3","prompt":"Two angles make a straight line. One is 73°. Find the other.","choices":["117°","287°","107°"],"answer":2,"why":"Adjacent angles forming a straight line sum to 180°. The other angle is 180° − 73° = 107°."},{"id":"m-angles-q4","unit":"m-angles","level":2,"form":"m-angles-f4","prompt":"An isosceles triangle has unequal angle 36°. Each equal angle is…","choices":["72°","36°","144°"],"answer":0,"why":"The remaining 144 degrees are shared equally."},{"id":"m-angles-q5","unit":"m-angles","level":3,"form":"m-angles-f5","prompt":"A parallelogram has an interior angle of 68°. What is an adjacent interior angle?","choices":["112°","122°","68°"],"answer":0,"why":"Adjacent interior angles total 180 degrees."},{"id":"m-angles-q6","unit":"m-angles","level":3,"form":"m-angles-f6","prompt":"A diagram looks right-angled but has no right-angle mark or supporting information. What should you do?","choices":["Use only stated or deduced properties","Assume 90°","Measure the screen and treat it as exact"],"answer":0,"why":"A schematic appearance is not a valid premise."},{"id":"m-solids-q1","unit":"m-solids","level":1,"form":"m-solids-f1","prompt":"How many faces does a cube have?","choices":["4","8","6"],"answer":2,"why":"A cube has six square faces."},{"id":"m-solids-q2","unit":"m-solids","level":1,"form":"m-solids-f2","prompt":"A non-square rectangle has how many lines of reflection symmetry?","choices":["0","2","4"],"answer":1,"why":"Its horizontal and vertical midlines work; diagonals do not."},{"id":"m-solids-q3","unit":"m-solids","level":2,"form":"m-solids-f3","prompt":"A point is 4 squares left of a vertical mirror. Its reflection is…","choices":["4 squares right at the same height","4 squares above","On the mirror"],"answer":0,"why":"Reflection preserves perpendicular distance to the mirror."},{"id":"m-solids-q4","unit":"m-solids","level":2,"form":"m-solids-f4","prompt":"Which condition is necessary for a cube net?","choices":["It contains six equal squares","It is a straight strip","It contains any six shapes"],"answer":0,"why":"Six equal square faces are necessary, but their arrangement must also fold correctly."},{"id":"m-solids-q5","unit":"m-solids","level":3,"form":"m-solids-f5","prompt":"Why can six squares fail to form a cube net?","choices":["Squares cannot fold","A cube has eight faces","Two faces may overlap after folding"],"answer":2,"why":"Correct face count alone does not ensure six distinct folded positions."},{"id":"m-solids-q6","unit":"m-solids","level":3,"form":"m-solids-f6","prompt":"A drawing shows a stack of unit cubes from the front only. What is justified?","choices":["There may be different depths giving the same front view","The total must equal the visible squares","Every hidden cube is known"],"answer":0,"why":"A front projection may hide cubes behind others."},{"id":"m-circles-q1","unit":"m-circles","level":1,"form":"m-circles-f1","prompt":"A circle has diameter 18 cm. Its radius is…","choices":["18 cm","36 cm","9 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"The radius is half the diameter."},{"id":"m-circles-q2","unit":"m-circles","level":1,"form":"m-circles-f2","prompt":"Which formula gives circumference?","choices":["2πr","πr²","r²"],"answer":0,"why":"Circumference is π times the diameter."},{"id":"m-circles-q3","unit":"m-circles","level":2,"form":"m-circles-f3","prompt":"Find the area for radius 5 cm using π = 3.14.","choices":["78.5 cm²","15.7 cm²","31.4 cm²"],"answer":0,"why":"Circle area is π × radius × radius. Using the supplied value, 3.14 × 5 × 5 = 78.5 cm²."},{"id":"m-circles-q4","unit":"m-circles","level":2,"form":"m-circles-f4","prompt":"A semicircle has radius 7 cm. Using π = 22/7, find its complete perimeter.","choices":["22 cm","44 cm","36 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"Half circumference is 22; add diameter 14."},{"id":"m-circles-q5","unit":"m-circles","level":3,"form":"m-circles-f5","prompt":"A quarter circle has radius 4 cm. Using π = 3.14, find its perimeter.","choices":["12.56 cm","6.28 cm","14.28 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"The arc is 6.28 and the two radii total eight."},{"id":"m-circles-q6","unit":"m-circles","level":3,"form":"m-circles-f6","prompt":"A semicircle is attached to a rectangle along its diameter. Is that shared diameter part of the combined outer perimeter?","choices":["No, it is an internal edge","Only half of it","Yes, always"],"answer":0,"why":"The shared line is inside the union, not on its exposed boundary."},{"id":"m-volume-q1","unit":"m-volume","level":1,"form":"m-volume-f1","prompt":"Volume is measured in…","choices":["cm","cm³","cm²"],"answer":1,"why":"Volume measures occupied space using cubic units. One cubic centimetre (cm³) is the volume of a cube with 1 cm edges."},{"id":"m-volume-q2","unit":"m-volume","level":1,"form":"m-volume-f2","prompt":"A cuboid is 3 cm by 4 cm by 5 cm. Its volume is…","choices":["60 cm³","120 cm³","12 cm³"],"answer":0,"why":"Each layer has twelve cubes and there are five layers."},{"id":"m-volume-q3","unit":"m-volume","level":2,"form":"m-volume-f3","prompt":"A cuboid tank with a horizontal base area of 200 cm² holds 1,000 cm³ of water. Find the water depth.","choices":["20 cm","5 cm","200,000 cm"],"answer":1,"why":"For a cuboid, water volume = base area × water depth. Divide 1,000 cm³ by 200 cm² to obtain a depth of 5 cm."},{"id":"m-volume-q4","unit":"m-volume","level":2,"form":"m-volume-f4","prompt":"A cube has volume 64 cm³. Find its edge length.","choices":["64/3 cm","8 cm","4 cm"],"answer":2,"why":"A cube has three equal dimensions. An edge of 4 cm gives 4 × 4 × 4 = 64 cm³, matching the stated volume."},{"id":"m-volume-q5","unit":"m-volume","level":3,"form":"m-volume-f5","prompt":"900 ml of water is poured without loss into an empty cuboid tank with a horizontal base area of 150 cm². Find the water depth.","choices":["6 cm","135 cm","0.006 cm"],"answer":0,"why":"900 ml = 900 cm³; divide by 150."},{"id":"m-volume-q6","unit":"m-volume","level":3,"form":"m-volume-f6","prompt":"A cuboid has volume 180 cm³ and height 9 cm. What can be determined uniquely?","choices":["Its base area","Its length","Its breadth"],"answer":0,"why":"The base area is 20 cm², but several length–breadth pairs fit."},{"id":"m-data-q1","unit":"m-data","level":1,"form":"m-data-f1","prompt":"The average of 4, 6 and 8 is…","choices":["6","3","18"],"answer":0,"why":"Their total 18 is shared across three values."},{"id":"m-data-q2","unit":"m-data","level":1,"form":"m-data-f2","prompt":"A graph scale has 10 objects per interval. Three intervals represent…","choices":["30","13","3"],"answer":0,"why":"Multiply the number of intervals by the scale."},{"id":"m-data-q3","unit":"m-data","level":2,"form":"m-data-f3","prompt":"Five numbers average 12. Their total is…","choices":["60","17","2.4"],"answer":0,"why":"Average = total ÷ number of values. Undo the division: total = 12 × 5 = 60."},{"id":"m-data-q4","unit":"m-data","level":2,"form":"m-data-f4","prompt":"Three scores average 14. Two are 10 and 15. Find the third.","choices":["14","3","17"],"answer":2,"why":"The total is 42; remove 25."},{"id":"m-data-q5","unit":"m-data","level":3,"form":"m-data-f5","prompt":"Two groups have 2 pupils averaging 8 and 4 pupils averaging 14. Find the combined average.","choices":["11","12","22"],"answer":1,"why":"Total = 16 + 56 = 72 across six pupils, giving 12."},{"id":"m-data-q6","unit":"m-data","level":3,"form":"m-data-f6","prompt":"A survey of ten friends finds eight prefer cats. Which conclusion is supported directly?","choices":["Eight of the ten surveyed friends prefer cats","Cats are objectively better pets","80% of all children prefer cats"],"answer":0,"why":"The data directly describes this sample, not every child."},{"id":"m-modelling-q1","unit":"m-modelling","level":1,"form":"m-modelling-f1","prompt":"Two amounts have total 30 and difference 8. Which calculation finds two copies of the smaller amount?","choices":["30 + 8","30 × 8","30 − 8"],"answer":2,"why":"Removing the excess leaves two equal smaller amounts."},{"id":"m-modelling-q2","unit":"m-modelling","level":1,"form":"m-modelling-f2","prompt":"To undo “multiply by 4, then add 3”, first…","choices":["Add 3","Divide by 4","Subtract 3"],"answer":2,"why":"Undo the last transformation first."},{"id":"m-modelling-q3","unit":"m-modelling","level":2,"form":"m-modelling-f3","prompt":"Two numbers total 50 and differ by 12. Find the smaller.","choices":["38","31","19"],"answer":2,"why":"Remove the extra 12 from the total to leave two equal copies of the smaller number. (50 − 12) ÷ 2 = 19; the larger is 31."},{"id":"m-modelling-q4","unit":"m-modelling","level":2,"form":"m-modelling-f4","prompt":"A number is doubled then reduced by 5 to give 17. Find it.","choices":["24","6","11"],"answer":2,"why":"Add back five to get 22, then halve."},{"id":"m-modelling-q5","unit":"m-modelling","level":3,"form":"m-modelling-f5","prompt":"Ten tickets cost $38. Child tickets are $3 and adult tickets $5. How many adult tickets?","choices":["4","6","8"],"answer":0,"why":"All-child cost is 30; each adult replacement adds two, so four are needed."},{"id":"m-modelling-q6","unit":"m-modelling","level":3,"form":"m-modelling-f6","prompt":"A proposed pair satisfies a stated total but not the stated difference. Is it a solution?","choices":["Only if the arithmetic is neat","No, every condition must hold","Yes, one condition is enough"],"answer":1,"why":"A solution must satisfy the complete problem, not a selected condition."},{"id":"m-proof-q1","unit":"m-proof","level":1,"form":"m-proof-f1","prompt":"Which would disprove “all multiples of 3 are odd”?","choices":["9","15","12"],"answer":2,"why":"12 is a multiple of three but is even."},{"id":"m-proof-q2","unit":"m-proof","level":1,"form":"m-proof-f2","prompt":"Four hats and three scarves allow how many unrestricted hat–scarf pairs?","choices":["24","12","7"],"answer":1,"why":"Each of four hats pairs with each of three scarves."},{"id":"m-proof-q3","unit":"m-proof","level":2,"form":"m-proof-f3","prompt":"Adding 2 repeatedly to an odd number always leaves…","choices":["An even number","An odd number","A prime number"],"answer":1,"why":"Adding an even quantity preserves parity."},{"id":"m-proof-q4","unit":"m-proof","level":2,"form":"m-proof-f4","prompt":"With two possible colours and enough counters, how many blind draws guarantee a same-colour pair?","choices":["4","3","2"],"answer":1,"why":"Two can differ; the third must match one of the first two."},{"id":"m-proof-q5","unit":"m-proof","level":3,"form":"m-proof-f5","prompt":"A rectangle has area 18 and positive whole-number sides. Which has the smallest perimeter?","choices":["3 by 6","2 by 9","1 by 18"],"answer":0,"why":"These are all factor pairs; their perimeters are 38, 22 and 18."},{"id":"m-proof-q6","unit":"m-proof","level":3,"form":"m-proof-f6","prompt":"A pattern works for the first 20 cases. Which conclusion is justified?","choices":["It must work forever","It is false","It is a conjecture needing a general argument"],"answer":2,"why":"Finite agreement supports exploration but does not by itself prove a universal claim."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q1","unit":"s-inquiry","level":1,"form":"s-inquiry-f1","prompt":"Which statement is an observation?","choices":["The seedling needs more minerals.","The seedling is 12 cm tall.","The seedling grew because of music."],"answer":1,"why":"Height is directly measurable. The other statements are explanations that require evidence."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q2","unit":"s-inquiry","level":1,"form":"s-inquiry-f2","prompt":"A test changes exposed water surface area. Which outcome is useful?","choices":["Water lost after the same duration","Container colour only","The name of the room"],"answer":0,"why":"Water lost over a fixed time provides evidence about evaporation."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q3","unit":"s-inquiry","level":2,"form":"s-inquiry-f3","prompt":"Plants A and B receive different light and different water. What can their growth establish?","choices":["Light alone caused the difference.","The test cannot isolate the effect of light.","Water alone caused the difference."],"answer":1,"why":"Both conditions changed, so either or both may account for a difference."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q4","unit":"s-inquiry","level":2,"form":"s-inquiry-f4","prompt":"A seedling starts at 8 cm and finishes at 14 cm. Another starts at 12 cm and finishes at 16 cm. Which grew more in height?","choices":["The first: 6 cm compared with 4 cm","Both grew equally","The second because 16 is larger"],"answer":0,"why":"Compare changes from the starting values, not final heights alone."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q5","unit":"s-inquiry","level":3,"form":"s-inquiry-f5","prompt":"An unfair test is repeated ten times with both light and water changed. What improves?","choices":["It proves every plant behaves the same way.","It may show consistency, but still cannot isolate light.","It now isolates light."],"answer":1,"why":"Repetition can reveal consistency but cannot remove a confounding variable."},{"id":"s-inquiry-q6","unit":"s-inquiry","level":3,"form":"s-inquiry-f6","prompt":"A lamp group grows differently and is also warmer. What is the best next step?","choices":["Conclude light is the only cause.","Ignore temperature.","Compare groups while controlling temperature."],"answer":2,"why":"Control the additional factor so the effect of light can be investigated more fairly."},{"id":"s-diversity-q1","unit":"s-diversity","level":1,"form":"s-diversity-f1","prompt":"Which feature is strongest evidence that an animal is a bird?","choices":["It flies.","It has feathers.","It lays eggs."],"answer":1,"why":"Feathers distinguish birds. Flying and egg-laying also occur in other groups."},{"id":"s-diversity-q2","unit":"s-diversity","level":1,"form":"s-diversity-f2","prompt":"An adult insect has how many legs?","choices":["Eight","Four","Six"],"answer":2,"why":"An adult insect has six legs. A spider has eight and is not an insect."},{"id":"s-diversity-q3","unit":"s-diversity","level":2,"form":"s-diversity-f3","prompt":"Why is “moves by itself” an inadequate rule for living things?","choices":["Plants can never respond.","Machines can move, and some living things show little visible movement.","Movement proves reproduction."],"answer":1,"why":"A combination of characteristics is needed."},{"id":"s-diversity-q4","unit":"s-diversity","level":2,"form":"s-diversity-f4","prompt":"A fern has no flowers and reproduces by spores. Which group fits?","choices":["Flowering plant","Non-flowering plant","Mammal"],"answer":1,"why":"Ferns are non-flowering plants. This does not mean every non-flowering plant uses spores."},{"id":"s-diversity-q5","unit":"s-diversity","level":3,"form":"s-diversity-f5","prompt":"A photograph shows a plant with no flowers. What is justified?","choices":["It must be non-flowering.","It must be dead.","More life-cycle information is needed to classify it this way."],"answer":2,"why":"A flowering plant may have no flowers at the time of the photograph."},{"id":"s-diversity-q6","unit":"s-diversity","level":3,"form":"s-diversity-f6","prompt":"A key uses “lives in water” to classify all fish. A whale exposes which flaw?","choices":["Fish cannot live in water.","Whales must be fish.","Habitat alone does not distinguish the animal group."],"answer":2,"why":"Animals from several groups live in water, so this feature alone is insufficient."},{"id":"s-materials-q1","unit":"s-materials","level":1,"form":"s-materials-f1","prompt":"Which is a material rather than an object?","choices":["Chair","Spoon","Wood"],"answer":2,"why":"Wood is a material. Spoons and chairs are objects that can be made from different materials."},{"id":"s-materials-q2","unit":"s-materials","level":1,"form":"s-materials-f2","prompt":"Which property is most relevant to a raincoat outer layer?","choices":["Absorbing as much water as possible","Waterproofness","Dissolving quickly"],"answer":1,"why":"The layer should prevent rain passing through under normal use."},{"id":"s-materials-q3","unit":"s-materials","level":2,"form":"s-materials-f3","prompt":"Why compare equal-sized strips in a bending test?","choices":["Every material must bend equally.","Dimensions can affect bending as well as material.","Colour then becomes identical."],"answer":1,"why":"Controlling dimensions makes the material comparison fairer."},{"id":"s-materials-q4","unit":"s-materials","level":2,"form":"s-materials-f4","prompt":"A towel takes up spilled water. Which property is useful?","choices":["Water absorption","Blocking all light","Being magnetic"],"answer":0,"why":"An absorbent material takes water in."},{"id":"s-materials-q5","unit":"s-materials","level":3,"form":"s-materials-f5","prompt":"A metal boat floats but a solid metal piece sinks. What should change?","choices":["Conclude the boat is not metal.","Conclude all metal floats.","Reject the rule that every metal object sinks."],"answer":2,"why":"The object’s form matters; one rule based only on material is insufficient."},{"id":"s-materials-q6","unit":"s-materials","level":3,"form":"s-materials-f6","prompt":"Two samples stop drops for one minute. Which conclusion is supported?","choices":["They will stop water forever.","They have equal strength.","Both prevented passage in this test; longer exposure is untested."],"answer":2,"why":"The evidence concerns the tested duration and conditions, not all properties or all durations."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q1","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":1,"form":"s-life-cycles-f1","prompt":"Which is the correct butterfly sequence?","choices":["Egg → pupa → larva → adult","Adult → larva → egg → pupa","Egg → larva → pupa → adult"],"answer":2,"why":"A butterfly develops through egg, larva, pupa and adult stages."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q2","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":1,"form":"s-life-cycles-f2","prompt":"A grasshopper’s young stage is called a…","choices":["Pupa","Nymph","Seedling"],"answer":1,"why":"The three-stage grasshopper cycle is egg, nymph and adult."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q3","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":2,"form":"s-life-cycles-f3","prompt":"Does little visible movement mean a butterfly pupa is non-living?","choices":["No, development continues in a living stage.","Yes, it is only a shell.","Yes, movement alone defines life."],"answer":0,"why":"Visible movement is not a sufficient test for life."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q4","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":2,"form":"s-life-cycles-f4","prompt":"Which statement distinguishes germination from reproduction?","choices":["Germination creates the first egg cell.","Both mean exactly the same process.","Germination begins growth of an already formed seed."],"answer":2,"why":"A seed forms before it germinates; these are different stages and processes."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q5","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":3,"form":"s-life-cycles-f5","prompt":"A frog and a grasshopper each have a simplified three-stage cycle. What follows?","choices":["Equal stage counts do not establish the same animal group.","They belong to the same animal group.","Both have a pupal stage."],"answer":0,"why":"Classification depends on organism characteristics, not the number of stages chosen in a diagram."},{"id":"s-life-cycles-q6","unit":"s-life-cycles","level":3,"form":"s-life-cycles-f6","prompt":"One seed fails to germinate. What is the best conclusion?","choices":["Check viability and conditions before making a broad claim.","Water always prevents germination.","That species never germinates."],"answer":0,"why":"A single failed seed does not isolate the cause or establish a species-wide rule."},{"id":"s-magnets-q1","unit":"s-magnets","level":1,"form":"s-magnets-f1","prompt":"Two north poles brought together will…","choices":["Attract","Repel","Always melt"],"answer":1,"why":"Like magnetic poles repel."},{"id":"s-magnets-q2","unit":"s-magnets","level":1,"form":"s-magnets-f2","prompt":"Which statement is correct?","choices":["Only plastic is magnetic.","All metals are strongly attracted to a classroom magnet.","Iron is a familiar magnetic material."],"answer":2,"why":"Iron is magnetic; being a metal alone does not guarantee this behaviour."},{"id":"s-magnets-q3","unit":"s-magnets","level":2,"form":"s-magnets-f3","prompt":"An unknown bar repels a known north pole. What does the tested end behave as?","choices":["An unmagnetised wooden end","A north pole","A south pole"],"answer":1,"why":"Repulsion occurs between like poles, so the tested end behaves as north."},{"id":"s-magnets-q4","unit":"s-magnets","level":2,"form":"s-magnets-f4","prompt":"An object is attracted to both poles of a magnet. What is established?","choices":["It must be a permanent magnet.","Attraction alone does not prove it is a permanent magnet.","It has no interaction with magnets."],"answer":1,"why":"A magnetic material such as iron can be attracted to either pole."},{"id":"s-magnets-q5","unit":"s-magnets","level":3,"form":"s-magnets-f5","prompt":"A test changes both magnet type and gap. Why is the result limited?","choices":["The separate effect of gap cannot be isolated.","Gaps never affect magnetic effects.","Repeating once removes all confounding."],"answer":0,"why":"Changing two relevant factors prevents attributing the result to gap alone."},{"id":"s-magnets-q6","unit":"s-magnets","level":3,"form":"s-magnets-f6","prompt":"Why can a controlled electromagnet release an iron object when switched off?","choices":["The current producing the magnetic effect stops.","The object stops being matter.","Its mass disappears."],"answer":0,"why":"The magnetic effect of the electromagnet depends on the current in its coil."},{"id":"s-matter-q1","unit":"s-matter","level":1,"form":"s-matter-f1","prompt":"Which is matter?","choices":["Air","Light alone","Sound alone"],"answer":0,"why":"Air has mass and occupies space."},{"id":"s-matter-q2","unit":"s-matter","level":1,"form":"s-matter-f2","prompt":"A liquid has…","choices":["Neither mass nor volume","A fixed shape in every container","A definite volume but no definite shape"],"answer":2,"why":"A liquid takes the shape of the occupied part of its container while retaining its volume under the stated conditions."},{"id":"s-matter-q3","unit":"s-matter","level":2,"form":"s-matter-f3","prompt":"The same water is poured without loss into a narrower vessel. What must remain the same?","choices":["The container shape","The water level","The volume of water"],"answer":2,"why":"A different vessel can change water level without changing the amount of water."},{"id":"s-matter-q4","unit":"s-matter","level":2,"form":"s-matter-f4","prompt":"Why can trapped air keep tissue dry inside an inverted cup?","choices":["Water becomes a solid.","Air has no volume.","Air occupies space and prevents water filling that region."],"answer":2,"why":"The trapped air occupies the space into which water would otherwise move."},{"id":"s-matter-q5","unit":"s-matter","level":3,"form":"s-matter-f5","prompt":"A pile of sand changes shape when poured. Which explanation is best?","choices":["Anything that can be poured must be liquid.","Each grain becomes a gas.","Individual grains remain solids even though the pile rearranges."],"answer":2,"why":"The collection can rearrange while its individual grains retain solid properties."},{"id":"s-matter-q6","unit":"s-matter","level":3,"form":"s-matter-f6","prompt":"Which observation establishes a change of state?","choices":["A solid ice cube becomes liquid water.","A wire bends.","A can is crushed."],"answer":0,"why":"Melting changes the state from solid to liquid. Bending and crushing change shape."},{"id":"s-heat-q1","unit":"s-heat","level":1,"form":"s-heat-f1","prompt":"Heat transfers naturally from…","choices":["Every object to colder space regardless of temperature","Higher to lower temperature","Lower to higher temperature only"],"answer":1,"why":"Net heat transfer due to a temperature difference is from higher to lower temperature."},{"id":"s-heat-q2","unit":"s-heat","level":1,"form":"s-heat-f2","prompt":"Which instrument measures temperature?","choices":["Ruler","Thermometer","Compass"],"answer":1,"why":"A thermometer measures temperature."},{"id":"s-heat-q3","unit":"s-heat","level":2,"form":"s-heat-f3","prompt":"Why does an insulated bottle slow the warming of a cold drink?","choices":["It slows heat gain from warmer surroundings.","It adds ice automatically.","It produces cold continuously."],"answer":0,"why":"Insulation slows heat transfer into the colder drink."},{"id":"s-heat-q4","unit":"s-heat","level":2,"form":"s-heat-f4","prompt":"A metal spoon handle warms faster than a similar plastic one in warm water. Which idea explains this?","choices":["The metal creates heat from nothing.","Plastic has no matter.","Metal generally conducts heat more readily."],"answer":2,"why":"Under comparable conditions, metal generally transfers heat along the handle more readily."},{"id":"s-heat-q5","unit":"s-heat","level":3,"form":"s-heat-f5","prompt":"A drink cools toward a 25°C room. Can the same simple trend justify predicting −10°C?","choices":["Yes, because all drinks freeze eventually.","No, the physical heat-transfer conditions constrain extrapolation.","Yes, always extend the line."],"answer":1,"why":"Without a colder source or other process, that cooling model approaches the surrounding temperature."},{"id":"s-heat-q6","unit":"s-heat","level":3,"form":"s-heat-f6","prompt":"Two objects at equal temperature feel different to touch. What is a reasonable explanation?","choices":["Their rates of heat transfer to or from the hand can differ.","Touch measures temperature perfectly.","Their temperatures must differ."],"answer":0,"why":"Touch depends on heat transfer as well as the objects’ temperatures."},{"id":"s-water-q1","unit":"s-water","level":1,"form":"s-water-f1","prompt":"Melting changes…","choices":["Ice into liquid water as it gains heat","Water vapour into ice only","Liquid water into a different substance"],"answer":0,"why":"Melting changes the state of water from solid to liquid; it remains water."},{"id":"s-water-q2","unit":"s-water","level":1,"form":"s-water-f2","prompt":"Condensation changes water vapour into liquid water when it…","choices":["Loses heat","Becomes invisible for the first time","Gains mass from nowhere"],"answer":0,"why":"Water vapour condenses to liquid as it loses heat under suitable conditions."},{"id":"s-water-q3","unit":"s-water","level":2,"form":"s-water-f3","prompt":"Why can a spread-out wet cloth dry faster than the same folded cloth?","choices":["Folding always raises its temperature.","It creates extra water.","More wet surface is exposed for evaporation."],"answer":2,"why":"With other conditions comparable, larger exposed surface area increases evaporation rate."},{"id":"s-water-q4","unit":"s-water","level":2,"form":"s-water-f4","prompt":"Droplets form outside a sealed cold bottle. A likely source is…","choices":["The bottle turning into water","Water vapour in surrounding air","Liquid passing through intact glass"],"answer":1,"why":"Water vapour in air can lose heat near the cold surface and condense."},{"id":"s-water-q5","unit":"s-water","level":3,"form":"s-water-f5","prompt":"Water evaporates at 30°C. Does this contradict a normal boiling point of 100°C?","choices":["Yes, evaporation requires boiling.","Yes, the water must be another substance.","No, evaporation can occur below the boiling point."],"answer":2,"why":"Evaporation and boiling are different processes; surface evaporation occurs below the boiling point."},{"id":"s-water-q6","unit":"s-water","level":3,"form":"s-water-f6","prompt":"A sunny drying spot is also windier. What can a faster drying result establish?","choices":["Temperature alone caused it.","The combined conditions helped, but the separate effects are not isolated.","Wind alone caused it."],"answer":1,"why":"Both temperature and air movement can affect evaporation, so this comparison cannot isolate one."},{"id":"s-light-q1","unit":"s-light","level":1,"form":"s-light-f1","prompt":"Why can you see a book under a lamp?","choices":["Your eyes send visible light to it.","The book always produces its own light.","Light reflected from the book enters your eyes."],"answer":2,"why":"Light from the lamp reflects from the book into the eyes."},{"id":"s-light-q2","unit":"s-light","level":1,"form":"s-light-f2","prompt":"A shadow forms when an object…","choices":["Turns light into water","Blocks light reaching a surface","Creates darkness as a substance"],"answer":1,"why":"A shadow is a region receiving less light because paths are blocked."},{"id":"s-light-q3","unit":"s-light","level":2,"form":"s-light-f3","prompt":"Which sequence explains seeing a non-luminous object?","choices":["Source → object → eye","Object → lamp → eye","Eye → object → lamp"],"answer":0,"why":"Light travels from the source, reflects from the object and enters the eye."},{"id":"s-light-q4","unit":"s-light","level":2,"form":"s-light-f4","prompt":"With a small source and screen fixed, moving an opaque card nearer the source generally makes its shadow…","choices":["Larger","Disappear because light curves around it","Smaller in every setup"],"answer":0,"why":"The card blocks a wider bundle of straight paths reaching the screen."},{"id":"s-light-q5","unit":"s-light","level":3,"form":"s-light-f5","prompt":"A shadow experiment changes both card size and card position. What is the limitation?","choices":["It proves position has no effect.","It cannot isolate the effect of position.","It proves every card is transparent."],"answer":1,"why":"Both size and position can affect shadow size."},{"id":"s-light-q6","unit":"s-light","level":3,"form":"s-light-f6","prompt":"A shadow has blurred edges with a broad lamp. What should the investigator do?","choices":["Record the edge uncertainty and use a consistent measuring rule.","Discard all light-path reasoning.","Invent a sharp measurement."],"answer":0,"why":"A broad source can produce partial shadows; the measurement limitation should be acknowledged."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q1","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":1,"form":"s-plant-parts-f1","prompt":"Which part commonly anchors a plant and absorbs water?","choices":["Fruit skin only","Petals only","Roots"],"answer":2,"why":"Roots commonly anchor the plant and absorb water and mineral salts."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q2","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":1,"form":"s-plant-parts-f2","prompt":"Which statement about plant food is correct?","choices":["Mineral salts are identical to sugar.","Green plants make food using light energy, water and carbon dioxide.","Soil is the food plants eat."],"answer":1,"why":"Mineral uptake and food-making are different processes."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q3","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":2,"form":"s-plant-parts-f3","prompt":"Why can stem damage affect leaves?","choices":["Leaves never need roots.","The stem only changes colour.","The stem supports transport between plant parts."],"answer":2,"why":"Damage can interrupt transport as well as support."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q4","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":2,"form":"s-plant-parts-f4","prompt":"A fruit’s useful functions can include…","choices":["Replacing every root","Protecting seeds and helping dispersal","Absorbing all sunlight for the plant"],"answer":1,"why":"Fruits protect seeds and can aid their dispersal."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q5","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":3,"form":"s-plant-parts-f5","prompt":"A wilted plant is observed. Which conclusion is justified immediately?","choices":["Its roots must be cut.","Water supply or other stresses may be involved; investigate the cause.","It is definitely a non-living object."],"answer":1,"why":"Wilting is evidence of a problem but does not identify a unique cause."},{"id":"s-plant-parts-q6","unit":"s-plant-parts","level":3,"form":"s-plant-parts-f6","prompt":"A plant has water and mineral salts but no light for a long period. Why can sustained growth fail?","choices":["It cannot maintain photosynthesis without a light source.","Light supplies additional soil.","Water becomes non-matter."],"answer":0,"why":"Stored food is limited; continued food-making requires suitable light."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q1","unit":"s-reproduction","level":1,"form":"s-reproduction-f1","prompt":"Pollination is the transfer of…","choices":["Pollen from anther to stigma","Water from root to leaf","Seeds into soil"],"answer":0,"why":"Pollination transfers pollen; it is distinct from fertilisation and dispersal."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q2","unit":"s-reproduction","level":1,"form":"s-reproduction-f2","prompt":"Where does a developing baby grow?","choices":["Lung","Stomach","Womb or uterus"],"answer":2,"why":"The womb is part of the reproductive system; the stomach is digestive."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q3","unit":"s-reproduction","level":2,"form":"s-reproduction-f3","prompt":"What is fertilisation?","choices":["Fusion of male and female reproductive cells","Movement of a whole fruit","Evaporation from a leaf"],"answer":0,"why":"Fertilisation joins the reproductive cells and can begin development of a new organism."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q4","unit":"s-reproduction","level":2,"form":"s-reproduction-f4","prompt":"A fruit has hooks that attach to fur. Which dispersal method fits?","choices":["Only by fertilisation","By animals","Only by photosynthesis"],"answer":1,"why":"Hooks can attach to animals and carry the fruit or seeds elsewhere."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q5","unit":"s-reproduction","level":3,"form":"s-reproduction-f5","prompt":"Why is pollination not the same as seed dispersal?","choices":["Dispersal happens before every flower exists.","Pollination moves pollen before seeds form; dispersal moves formed seeds.","Both always move identical structures."],"answer":1,"why":"The processes move different structures at different stages."},{"id":"s-reproduction-q6","unit":"s-reproduction","level":3,"form":"s-reproduction-f6","prompt":"Which statement about germination is most accurate?","choices":["Water, oxygen and suitable temperature matter; light requirements vary.","Every seed needs bright light to begin germinating.","A seed can germinate without suitable conditions."],"answer":0,"why":"Avoid treating light as a universal requirement for the start of germination."},{"id":"s-body-q1","unit":"s-body","level":1,"form":"s-body-f1","prompt":"Which organ carries food from mouth to stomach?","choices":["Heart","Windpipe","Gullet"],"answer":2,"why":"The gullet carries swallowed food to the stomach."},{"id":"s-body-q2","unit":"s-body","level":1,"form":"s-body-f2","prompt":"Where is much digested food absorbed into blood?","choices":["Lungs","Small intestine","Womb"],"answer":1,"why":"The small intestine is a major site of digestion and absorption."},{"id":"s-body-q3","unit":"s-body","level":2,"form":"s-body-f3","prompt":"Which is the correct order for food?","choices":["Mouth → stomach → gullet → small intestine","Mouth → gullet → stomach → small intestine","Mouth → lungs → stomach"],"answer":1,"why":"Food passes from mouth through gullet to stomach and then small intestine."},{"id":"s-body-q4","unit":"s-body","level":2,"form":"s-body-f4","prompt":"What is a main function of the large intestine?","choices":["Absorbing water from remaining contents","Producing light energy","Pumping blood"],"answer":0,"why":"The large intestine absorbs water as remaining contents are processed."},{"id":"s-body-q5","unit":"s-body","level":3,"form":"s-body-f5","prompt":"How do digestion and absorption differ?","choices":["They are identical words.","Digestion breaks food down; absorption moves suitable substances into blood.","Absorption means chewing only."],"answer":1,"why":"Breakdown and movement into blood are different connected processes."},{"id":"s-body-q6","unit":"s-body","level":3,"form":"s-body-f6","prompt":"Why can impaired absorption affect the body even when food intake is adequate?","choices":["Useful substances may not enter blood sufficiently for transport.","The windpipe must digest the food instead.","Eating alone guarantees delivery to all cells."],"answer":0,"why":"Taking in food is not sufficient; digested substances must be absorbed and transported."},{"id":"s-transport-q1","unit":"s-transport","level":1,"form":"s-transport-f1","prompt":"What does the heart do?","choices":["Produce all oxygen","Pump blood","Digest food"],"answer":1,"why":"The heart pumps blood through blood vessels."},{"id":"s-transport-q2","unit":"s-transport","level":1,"form":"s-transport-f2","prompt":"Which route carries inhaled air toward the lungs?","choices":["Small intestine","Gullet","Windpipe"],"answer":2,"why":"The windpipe is part of the respiratory route."},{"id":"s-transport-q3","unit":"s-transport","level":2,"form":"s-transport-f3","prompt":"Which statement about breathed-out air is correct?","choices":["It is pure carbon dioxide.","It contains no oxygen.","It remains a mixture and still contains oxygen."],"answer":2,"why":"Gas proportions change, but breathed-out air is not pure carbon dioxide."},{"id":"s-transport-q4","unit":"s-transport","level":2,"form":"s-transport-f4","prompt":"How can food made in leaves reach roots?","choices":["Through food-carrying tubes","Only by falling into soil","It cannot reach roots"],"answer":0,"why":"Plants transport food to parts that need it, including roots."},{"id":"s-transport-q5","unit":"s-transport","level":3,"form":"s-transport-f5","prompt":"Why do breathing and heartbeat commonly increase during exercise?","choices":["Muscles no longer need materials.","Increased energy demand requires faster supply and removal of materials.","The heart changes into a lung."],"answer":1,"why":"The systems support the increased needs of working body parts."},{"id":"s-transport-q6","unit":"s-transport","level":3,"form":"s-transport-f6","prompt":"Coloured water moves along a plant stem. What does this directly investigate?","choices":["Every food-transport pathway","Water transport along the coloured paths","The number of seeds produced"],"answer":1,"why":"A substance entering with water provides evidence about water transport, not automatically all food transport."},{"id":"s-electricity-q1","unit":"s-electricity","level":1,"form":"s-electricity-f1","prompt":"A bulb in a simple circuit needs…","choices":["A complete conducting path through its contacts","Only a nearby battery with no connection","One wire touching any part"],"answer":0,"why":"A complete path through the bulb and source is required."},{"id":"s-electricity-q2","unit":"s-electricity","level":1,"form":"s-electricity-f2","prompt":"Opening a switch in the only path will…","choices":["Break the path and stop the current in that circuit","Create extra energy","Make every insulator a conductor"],"answer":0,"why":"An open switch breaks the complete conducting path."},{"id":"s-electricity-q3","unit":"s-electricity","level":2,"form":"s-electricity-f3","prompt":"Two bulbs are in series. One bulb breaks the only path. What happens to the other?","choices":["It always becomes brighter.","It goes off.","It works independently of the path."],"answer":1,"why":"Both bulbs share the single path, which is now incomplete."},{"id":"s-electricity-q4","unit":"s-electricity","level":2,"form":"s-electricity-f4","prompt":"Two bulbs have separate parallel branches. Only one branch opens. The other branch remains complete. What can happen?","choices":["The battery becomes an insulator.","The other bulb stays lit.","Every bulb must go off regardless of paths."],"answer":1,"why":"A separate complete branch can still carry current."},{"id":"s-electricity-q5","unit":"s-electricity","level":3,"form":"s-electricity-f5","prompt":"Why is “current is used up by the first series bulb” misleading?","choices":["Energy is transferred; current is not consumed this way along the series path.","Bulbs cannot transfer energy.","Only the first bulb needs a complete path."],"answer":0,"why":"Separate the flow of current from energy transfer in components."},{"id":"s-electricity-q6","unit":"s-electricity","level":3,"form":"s-electricity-f6","prompt":"A circuit test changes battery count and bulb type together. What is the best conclusion?","choices":["The test does not isolate either factor.","Battery count alone explains any change.","Bulb type alone explains any change."],"answer":0,"why":"Both supply and component properties can affect brightness."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q1","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":1,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f1","prompt":"Which materials are used to make food in photosynthesis?","choices":["Water and carbon dioxide","Soil alone","Oxygen and plastic"],"answer":0,"why":"Plants use water and carbon dioxide with light energy to make food."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q2","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":1,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f2","prompt":"Which gas is released during photosynthesis?","choices":["Oxygen","Only smoke","Only nitrogen"],"answer":0,"why":"Oxygen is released during photosynthesis."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q3","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":2,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f3","prompt":"Which statement about plant respiration is correct?","choices":["It occurs in living cells during day and night.","It occurs only at night.","It is identical to photosynthesis."],"answer":0,"why":"Plants need to release energy from food at all times; photosynthesis additionally requires light."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q4","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":2,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f4","prompt":"Why does fertiliser not replace light?","choices":["Roots photosynthesise in all conditions.","Mineral supply and light energy serve different roles.","Fertiliser is always pure sugar."],"answer":1,"why":"Mineral salts support growth but do not provide the light energy needed for photosynthesis."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q5","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":3,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f5","prompt":"A dark-grown seedling is taller but pale. What is justified?","choices":["Darkness definitely makes healthier plants.","Height alone does not establish health or sustained food-making.","Photosynthesis never needs light."],"answer":1,"why":"A taller shoot is not sufficient evidence of better health or sustained photosynthesis."},{"id":"s-photosynthesis-q6","unit":"s-photosynthesis","level":3,"form":"s-photosynthesis-f6","prompt":"A shade box changes light and temperature. How should a light investigation improve?","choices":["Ignore temperature because it is inconvenient.","Use different plant species too.","Control temperature and other relevant conditions while varying light."],"answer":2,"why":"Additional changed factors prevent isolating the effect of light."},{"id":"s-energy-q1","unit":"s-energy","level":1,"form":"s-energy-f1","prompt":"Which energy is associated with motion?","choices":["Only stored elastic energy","No energy","Kinetic energy"],"answer":2,"why":"Kinetic energy is associated with motion."},{"id":"s-energy-q2","unit":"s-energy","level":1,"form":"s-energy-f2","prompt":"A stretched spring stores…","choices":["Only light energy","Elastic potential energy","Only sound energy"],"answer":1,"why":"A stretched spring stores elastic potential energy."},{"id":"s-energy-q3","unit":"s-energy","level":2,"form":"s-energy-f3","prompt":"A battery torch commonly produces which outputs?","choices":["Light and heat","Water and soil","Light only, with no other transfer"],"answer":0,"why":"Electrical energy is converted into light and heat in the torch."},{"id":"s-energy-q4","unit":"s-energy","level":2,"form":"s-energy-f4","prompt":"A falling raised object can convert…","choices":["Gravitational potential energy into kinetic energy","Sound into soil only","All matter into nothing"],"answer":0,"why":"As height decreases, gravitational potential energy can be converted into movement."},{"id":"s-energy-q5","unit":"s-energy","level":3,"form":"s-energy-f5","prompt":"A toy stops moving. Which statement is best?","choices":["Energy has been transferred, including to the surroundings.","All energy has been destroyed.","The toy never had energy."],"answer":0,"why":"Stopping motion does not mean energy vanishes."},{"id":"s-energy-q6","unit":"s-energy","level":3,"form":"s-energy-f6","prompt":"Why do bulb-only and battery-plus-bulb energy chains start differently?","choices":["They describe systems with different boundaries.","Energy depends only on colour.","One must always be false."],"answer":0,"why":"The larger system includes the battery’s stored energy; the bulb-only account starts with electrical input."},{"id":"s-forces-q1","unit":"s-forces","level":1,"form":"s-forces-f1","prompt":"A force can change an object’s…","choices":["Name only","Speed, direction or shape","Past history"],"answer":1,"why":"Forces can affect motion or shape."},{"id":"s-forces-q2","unit":"s-forces","level":1,"form":"s-forces-f2","prompt":"Which is a non-contact interaction?","choices":["A hand pressing a book","A shoe rubbing a floor","Earth attracting a falling object"],"answer":2,"why":"Gravity can act without direct contact."},{"id":"s-forces-q3","unit":"s-forces","level":2,"form":"s-forces-f3","prompt":"For a block sliding across a fixed table, friction acts…","choices":["Against the sliding motion","Always in the same direction as sliding","Only upward"],"answer":0,"why":"In this specified sliding setup, friction opposes relative sliding."},{"id":"s-forces-q4","unit":"s-forces","level":2,"form":"s-forces-f4","prompt":"Why can friction be useful?","choices":["It makes all surfaces frictionless.","It can provide grip between shoe and floor.","It removes every force."],"answer":1,"why":"Friction supports grip and braking as well as resisting unwanted motion."},{"id":"s-forces-q5","unit":"s-forces","level":3,"form":"s-forces-f5","prompt":"A spring is stretched far beyond its safe range. What is wrong with assuming its earlier pattern continues forever?","choices":["Models have limits and the spring can be permanently damaged.","Distance cannot be measured.","Springs never exert forces."],"answer":0,"why":"The relationship applies within suitable conditions and material limits."},{"id":"s-forces-q6","unit":"s-forces","level":3,"form":"s-forces-f6","prompt":"Two sliding trials use different surfaces and different starting pushes. What can be concluded about surface alone?","choices":["It never matters.","It alone caused every distance difference.","Its separate effect cannot be isolated from starting conditions."],"answer":2,"why":"Both surface and initial motion can affect stopping distance."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q1","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":1,"form":"s-ecosystems-f1","prompt":"A population consists of…","choices":["Every non-living object on Earth","Organisms of the same kind in an area","All unrelated communities everywhere"],"answer":1,"why":"Population refers to organisms of the same kind in a specified area."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q2","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":1,"form":"s-ecosystems-f2","prompt":"In grass → grasshopper, the arrow indicates…","choices":["The grass hunts the grasshopper.","The direction of wind","Energy transfer from food to consumer"],"answer":2,"why":"Food-chain arrows point from what is eaten to the eater."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q3","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":2,"form":"s-ecosystems-f3","prompt":"Which role is commonly played by fungi breaking down dead material?","choices":["Decomposer","Predator of every animal","Producer by photosynthesis"],"answer":0,"why":"Decomposers break down dead material and help recycle materials."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q4","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":2,"form":"s-ecosystems-f4","prompt":"If a consumer loses one food source but has another, what should a prediction consider?","choices":["Only the drawing’s colour","That all consumers must immediately die","Availability of the alternative food"],"answer":2,"why":"Alternative food sources can affect the consequence of losing one source."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q5","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":3,"form":"s-ecosystems-f5","prompt":"Frogs decrease in a stated grass–grasshopper–frog chain. Under otherwise similar conditions, why might grasshoppers increase?","choices":["Reduced predation can increase survival.","Arrows prove an exact population doubling.","Frogs produce grasshoppers directly."],"answer":0,"why":"Fewer predators can mean fewer grasshoppers eaten, but the outcome depends on other factors."},{"id":"s-ecosystems-q6","unit":"s-ecosystems","level":3,"form":"s-ecosystems-f6","prompt":"Two organisms appear beside each other in a painting. What establishes a feeding relationship?","choices":["Being adjacent is enough.","Matching colours is enough.","Observation or a reliable source about their feeding"],"answer":2,"why":"A contextual illustration does not prove ecological interactions."},{"id":"s-environment-q1","unit":"s-environment","level":1,"form":"s-environment-f1","prompt":"Which is a behavioural adaptation?","choices":["Having feathers","Having a fixed shell shape","Seeking shade during hot periods"],"answer":2,"why":"Seeking shade is an action; shells and feathers are structural features."},{"id":"s-environment-q2","unit":"s-environment","level":1,"form":"s-environment-f2","prompt":"Competition occurs when organisms need…","choices":["No resources at all","Unlimited unrelated resources","The same limited resource"],"answer":2,"why":"Competition concerns shared resources that are limited."},{"id":"s-environment-q3","unit":"s-environment","level":2,"form":"s-environment-f3","prompt":"A useful adaptation explanation connects…","choices":["A feature or behaviour to its effect in an environment","An animal’s wish to a new inherited body part","Only the organism’s name and colour"],"answer":0,"why":"Explain how the feature helps under the stated conditions."},{"id":"s-environment-q4","unit":"s-environment","level":2,"form":"s-environment-f4","prompt":"Why can clearing vegetation affect animals?","choices":["It removes gravity.","Animals never depend on plants.","It can remove food sources and habitat."],"answer":2,"why":"Vegetation provides resources and habitat directly or through food webs."},{"id":"s-environment-q5","unit":"s-environment","level":3,"form":"s-environment-f5","prompt":"Insect counts rise after planting, but observation time also doubles. What is the limitation?","choices":["The planting alone is proven responsible.","Counts can never be useful.","More observation time may explain some of the count difference."],"answer":2,"why":"A change in sampling effort can affect counts and should be controlled or accounted for."},{"id":"s-environment-q6","unit":"s-environment","level":3,"form":"s-environment-f6","prompt":"A science article makes a claim without describing evidence. What is a useful next step?","choices":["Reject every scientific source forever.","Memorise it as certain because it is online.","Find the underlying study or an authoritative explanation of the evidence."],"answer":2,"why":"Investigate the basis of the claim and distinguish evidence from assertion."}]};
root.MochiCourseData=data;if(typeof module!=="undefined")module.exports=data;
})(typeof globalThis!=="undefined"?globalThis:this);
